/* Generated from the docs (docs/tutorial.md, docs/reference.md, docs/using.md, stdlib/README.md, SPEC.md) by demo/tools/build.mjs. Don't edit. */
(function (root) {
  (root.Stomp = root.Stomp || {}).docs = {"github":"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp","pages":[{"key":"tutorial","nav":"Tutorial","title":"Tutorial","file":"docs/tutorial.md","html":"<h1 id=\"tutorial-tutorial\">Tutorial</h1>\n<p>This tutorial builds footprints from the simplest two-pad part up to a whole library\nwith 3D models. Each step has an example: in the stomp editor, <strong>Try it</strong> opens it,\nand the drawing updates as you change it. Come back with <strong>Back to the tutorial</strong>\nabove the code. On GitHub, copy the code into the editor instead.</p>\n<p>You don't need to know anything about stomp to start. Knowing what a land pattern is\nhelps: the copper pads a part is soldered to, with the silkscreen, paste and\ncourtyard that go with them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-1-a-first-footprint\">1. A first footprint<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-1-a-first-footprint\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A footprint is a block: a header line, its statements, then <code>end</code>. This one is an 0402\nresistor, typed in from a recommended land pattern:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"0\"><code>footprint &quot;R0402&quot;\n  pins 2 horizontal            # two pads in a row, pin 1 on the left\n  default pad rect 0.5 x 0.6   # each pad 0.5 wide and 0.6 tall\n  gap: 0.5                     # 0.5 between the pads' facing edges\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>Three things to notice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>#</code> starts a comment.</li>\n<li>A value with a name takes a colon: <code>gap: 0.5</code>. Words that relate things, like the\n<code>x</code> in <code>0.5 x 0.6</code>, don't.</li>\n<li>Numbers are millimeters unless you say otherwise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Try changing the gap to <code>0.8</code>. The pads move apart as you type, and anything wrong is\nunderlined and listed under <strong>Problems</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-2-measure-the-way-the-drawing-does\">2. Measure the way the drawing does<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-2-measure-the-way-the-drawing-does\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Datasheet drawings rarely give coordinates. They measure from one feature to another:\npin to pin, edge to edge, edge to center. stomp lets you type those measurements as\ndrawn. This is a DPAK (TO-252):</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"1\"><code>footprint &quot;TO-252&quot;\n  pad 1 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 3 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 2 rect 5.5 x 6.2                       # the tab\n\n  pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1               # center to center\n  pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally\n  pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1             # the tab's near edge, from the pin centers\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A placement says how far one object is from another, in which direction. It measures\ncenter to center unless you name an edge: <code>inner</code> is the edge facing the other object,\n<code>outer</code> the edge facing away. So <code>pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1</code> measures from the\ntab's bottom edge to the center of pad 1, exactly as the drawing does.</p>\n<p>Put the cursor on a placement line: the drawing shows that dimension. Each object needs\nexactly one placement per axis, and stomp tells you, naming both lines, if one is\nplaced twice or not at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-3-describe-the-part-instead\">3. Describe the part instead<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-3-describe-the-part-instead\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>For a standard package you don't need to place pads at all. Describe the part, its\nbody, pins and leads, and stomp works out the land pattern the way IPC-7351 does:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"2\"><code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8&quot;\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5                 # width x length x height\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27              # two rows, pin 1 at the top left\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0  # lead width x foot length; span is tip to tip\n  pin1: dot\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>The numbers are the ones in the package drawing. The pads, the silkscreen outline kept\nclear of the copper, the pin-1 dot and the courtyard all follow from them. The details\npanel under the drawing lists every pad's size and position.</p>\n<p>Other arrangements work the same way: <code>pins 32 quad pitch: 0.5</code> for four sides,\n<code>pins 2 horizontal</code> for a chip, <code>pins grid 5 x 2 pitch: 2.54</code> for a header. The lead\nforms include <code>gullwing</code>, <code>jlead</code>, <code>flat</code> (QFN and DFN), <code>endcap</code> (chip passives),\n<code>ball</code> (BGA) and <code>pin</code> and <code>wire</code> for through-hole parts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-4-change-what-was-generated\">4. Change what was generated<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-4-change-what-was-generated\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Generated pads are ordinary pads, so any statement about one overrides what was\ngenerated. Here the SOIC gets longer pads for hand soldering and an exposed pad with\nits paste split into windows and thermal vias:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"3\"><code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8-EP-HS&quot;\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0\n  default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2           # 0.6 wide, 2.2 long\n  thermal pad 9 2.4 x 3.1 paste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 60%\n  pad 9 vias: 2 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.0\n  pin1: dot\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>thermal pad</code> adds a pad centered on the body. <code>pad 9 vias: …</code> changes one property of\npad 9 and keeps the rest. <code>pads 1..4 mask: +0.1</code> would change several at once, and\n<code>delete pad 3</code> removes one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-5-pin-1-polarity-and-the-silkscreen\">5. Pin 1, polarity and the silkscreen<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-5-pin-1-polarity-and-the-silkscreen\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The silkscreen and the courtyard are automatic. You choose the pin-1 mark, and mark a\npolarized part:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"4\"><code>footprint &quot;D-SMA&quot;\n  description: &quot;Diode, SMA (DO-214AC)&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;D&quot;\n  body 4.3 x 2.6 x 2.1\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead lbend 1.4 x 1.0\n  polarized                            # pin 1 is the anode; the bar marks the cathode\n  text &quot;K&quot; at (3.3, 0) size: 0.8       # your own silk text, by the cathode\n  courtyard: auto 0.5                  # more room around the part\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>pin1:</code> takes <code>dot</code>, <code>chamfer</code>, <code>notch</code>, <code>bar</code> or <code>none</code>. <code>polarized</code> puts a bar by\nthe cathode and a stripe on the 3D body. Lines, arcs, circles and text you write go on\nthe silkscreen, or on the assembly drawing with <code>layer: assembly</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-6-through-hole-parts\">6. Through-hole parts<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-6-through-hole-parts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A through-hole pad says <code>tht</code> and has a drill. <code>default pad</code> gives every pad the same\none, and a pad of its own overrides it, here the square pin 1:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"5\"><code>footprint &quot;HDR-1x4-2.54&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;J&quot;\n  body 10.16 x 2.54 x 8.5\n  pins grid 4 x 1 pitch: 2.54\n  lead pin 0.64 x 0.64                 # square pins\n  default pad tht circle 1.7 drill: 1.0\n  pad 1 tht rect 1.7 x 1.7 drill: 1.0\n  origin at pad 1                      # place the part by pin 1\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>origin at pad 1</code> moves the footprint's (0, 0), which is the body's center unless you\nsay otherwise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-7-names-units-and-arithmetic\">7. Names, units and arithmetic<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-7-names-units-and-arithmetic\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Give the datasheet's letter dimensions names and use them, with arithmetic where the\ndrawing needs it. <code>units: mil</code> switches a file, a footprint or a template to mils, and\nany number can carry its own unit (<code>0.3mm</code>, <code>12mil</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"6\"><code>E1 = 4.4          # body width\nD = 5.0           # body length\ne = 0.65          # pitch\nE = 6.4           # lead span\n\nfootprint &quot;TSSOP-14&quot;\n  body E1 x D x 1.2\n  pins 14 dual pitch: e\n  lead gullwing 0.25 x 0.6 span: E\n  default pad rect 0.45 x (E - E1) / 2 + 0.4\n  pin1: dot\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A name can be any word but <code>x</code> and <code>end</code>. Text in quotes can include values: <code>&quot;{e}&quot;</code>\nprints the pitch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-8-a-family-from-one-template\">8. A family from one template<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-8-a-family-from-one-template\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A template is a footprint with parameters. It can name the footprints it makes, and a\ntable calls it once per row. This is how stomp writes whole libraries:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"7\"><code>template soic(n, D, lspan = 6.0)\n  name: &quot;SOIC-{n}_3.9x{D}mm_P1.27mm&quot;\n  description: &quot;SOIC, {n} pins, 3.9 x {D} mm body&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;U&quot;\n  body 3.9 x D x 1.5\n  pins n dual pitch: 1.27\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: lspan\n  pin1: dot\nend\n\nfootprints from soic\n  n    D\n  8    4.9\n  14   8.65\n  16   9.9\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>The header row names the parameters each column fills. <strong>Library</strong> above the drawing\nshows every footprint in the file. One footprint can call a template too, with\narguments by position or by name: <code>footprint &quot;MY-SOIC-8&quot; from soic(8, 4.9, lspan: 6.2)</code>.\nAnd <code>extends</code> makes a variant of another footprint.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-9-the-3d-model\">9. The 3D model<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-9-the-3d-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><strong>3D</strong> above the drawing shows the part on a board. The model comes from what you've\nalready written: the body's height, the pins and the lead form. A <code>model</code> block adjusts\nit:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"8\"><code>footprint &quot;CP-RADIAL-6.3x11&quot;\n  description: &quot;Radial electrolytic, 6.3 mm can, 2.5 mm lead spacing&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;C&quot;\n  body cylinder d: 6.3 h: 11\n  pins 2 horizontal pitch: 2.5\n  lead wire d: 0.5\n  default pad tht circle 1.4 drill: 0.8\n  polarized                            # the stripe goes by the negative lead\n  model\n    body color: &quot;#2b5a3a&quot;              # a green sleeve\n    leads color: tin\n  end\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>The block can also change the standoff (<code>standoff: 0.1</code>), the leads' thickness, the\ndraft of a molded body, and add solids of your own, like <code>box 2 x 1 x 0.2 on body</code>.\nThe view's buttons export the model as VRML for KiCad or as STL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-10-features-of-your-own\">10. Features of your own<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-10-features-of-your-own\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A feature is a named drawing you can use anywhere in the file: a mark, a logo, a 3D\npart. It takes parameters like a template:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"9\"><code>feature arrowhead(s = 0.6)\n  polygon (0, 0) (-s, s / 2) (-s, -s / 2) fill\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;SOT-23&quot;\n  body 1.3 x 2.9 x 1.0\n  pins\n    left: 1, 2 pitch: 1.9\n    right: 3\n  end\n  lead gullwing 0.4 x 0.5 span: 2.4\n  pin1: arrowhead                      # the pin-1 mark, where the dot would go\n  feature arrowhead(0.8) at (2.4, 0) rotate: 180   # and one pointing at pin 3\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A feature can hold solids too, and choose between versions of itself with\n<strong>sections</strong> that a flag in the call turns on:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"10\"><code>feature fin_stack(W, L, h)\n  box W x L x 0.3 color: aluminum\n  box W x 0.4 x h at (0, -L / 3, 0) color: aluminum\n  box W x 0.4 x h at (0, L / 3, 0) color: aluminum\n  section tall\n    box W x 0.4 x 2 * h at (0, 0, 0) color: aluminum\n  end\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;QFN-16-HS&quot;\n  body 3 x 3 x 0.9\n  pins 16 quad pitch: 0.5\n  lead flat 0.25 x 0.4\n  thermal pad 1.7 x 1.7\n  feature fin_stack(2.6, 2.6, 1.5, tall) on body\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>on body</code> rests the feature's solids on top of the body. There's no <code>if</code> in stomp:\nsections are how a feature makes choices.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-11-the-standard-library\">11. The standard library<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-11-the-standard-library\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Every body, lead and mark stomp draws comes from its standard library, written in\nstomp. Its packages name a body the lead form can't describe, its symbols mark the\nsilk, and the part's refdes adds details on its own, like an LED's dome for <code>D</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"11\"><code>footprint &quot;TO-220-3_Vertical&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;U&quot;\n  body 10.2 x 4.5 x 15.6\n  pins grid 3 x 1 pitch: 2.54\n  lead wire 0.8 x 0.5\n  default pad tht oval 1.8 x 2.4 drill: 1.2\n  pin1: triangle(a: -90)               # a symbol from the library\n  model\n    body shape: to220                  # a package from the library\n  end\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A feature of yours with a library feature's name replaces it in your file, so\n<code>feature can_vent(D)</code> redraws the vent of every can. The\n<a href=\"#library\">standard library page</a> lists everything in it, and the library's\nfiles open in the editor's file picker, at the bottom.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tutorial-12-save-your-work-and-export-it\">12. Save your work and export it<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tutorial-12-save-your-work-and-export-it\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Examples are yours to change: your edits are kept until you press <strong>Reset</strong>. To keep a\nfile of your own, use the <strong>File</strong> menu:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New file</strong> or <strong>Save as</strong> keeps a file in this browser, saved as you type.</li>\n<li><strong>Save to disk</strong> writes a <code>.stomp</code> file to your computer, and <strong>Open from disk</strong>\n(or dropping files on the page) brings <code>.stomp</code> files back.</li>\n<li><strong>Export to KiCad</strong> saves every footprint in the file as a KiCad library, with\nits 3D models inside.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><a href=\"#using\">Using stomp</a> covers the editor in more detail, and the\n<a href=\"#reference\">language reference</a> has every statement.</p>","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-1-a-first-footprint","text":"1. A first footprint"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-2-measure-the-way-the-drawing-does","text":"2. Measure the way the drawing does"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-3-describe-the-part-instead","text":"3. Describe the part instead"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-4-change-what-was-generated","text":"4. Change what was generated"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-5-pin-1-polarity-and-the-silkscreen","text":"5. Pin 1, polarity and the silkscreen"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-6-through-hole-parts","text":"6. Through-hole parts"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-7-names-units-and-arithmetic","text":"7. Names, units and arithmetic"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-8-a-family-from-one-template","text":"8. A family from one template"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-9-the-3d-model","text":"9. The 3D model"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-10-features-of-your-own","text":"10. Features of your own"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-11-the-standard-library","text":"11. The standard library"},{"level":2,"id":"tutorial-12-save-your-work-and-export-it","text":"12. Save your work and export it"}],"snippets":[{"code":"footprint \"R0402\"\n  pins 2 horizontal            # two pads in a row, pin 1 on the left\n  default pad rect 0.5 x 0.6   # each pad 0.5 wide and 0.6 tall\n  gap: 0.5                     # 0.5 between the pads' facing edges\nend","heading":"1. A first footprint","id":"tutorial-1-a-first-footprint","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"TO-252\"\n  pad 1 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 3 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 2 rect 5.5 x 6.2                       # the tab\n\n  pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1               # center to center\n  pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally\n  pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1             # the tab's near edge, from the pin centers\nend","heading":"2. Measure the way the drawing does","id":"tutorial-2-measure-the-way-the-drawing-does","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"SOIC-8\"\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5                 # width x length x height\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27              # two rows, pin 1 at the top left\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0  # lead width x foot length; span is tip to tip\n  pin1: dot\nend","heading":"3. Describe the part instead","id":"tutorial-3-describe-the-part-instead","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"SOIC-8-EP-HS\"\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0\n  default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2           # 0.6 wide, 2.2 long\n  thermal pad 9 2.4 x 3.1 paste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 60%\n  pad 9 vias: 2 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.0\n  pin1: dot\nend","heading":"4. Change what was generated","id":"tutorial-4-change-what-was-generated","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"D-SMA\"\n  description: \"Diode, SMA (DO-214AC)\"\n  refdes: \"D\"\n  body 4.3 x 2.6 x 2.1\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead lbend 1.4 x 1.0\n  polarized                            # pin 1 is the anode; the bar marks the cathode\n  text \"K\" at (3.3, 0) size: 0.8       # your own silk text, by the cathode\n  courtyard: auto 0.5                  # more room around the part\nend","heading":"5. Pin 1, polarity and the silkscreen","id":"tutorial-5-pin-1-polarity-and-the-silkscreen","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"HDR-1x4-2.54\"\n  refdes: \"J\"\n  body 10.16 x 2.54 x 8.5\n  pins grid 4 x 1 pitch: 2.54\n  lead pin 0.64 x 0.64                 # square pins\n  default pad tht circle 1.7 drill: 1.0\n  pad 1 tht rect 1.7 x 1.7 drill: 1.0\n  origin at pad 1                      # place the part by pin 1\nend","heading":"6. Through-hole parts","id":"tutorial-6-through-hole-parts","runnable":true},{"code":"E1 = 4.4          # body width\nD = 5.0           # body length\ne = 0.65          # pitch\nE = 6.4           # lead span\n\nfootprint \"TSSOP-14\"\n  body E1 x D x 1.2\n  pins 14 dual pitch: e\n  lead gullwing 0.25 x 0.6 span: E\n  default pad rect 0.45 x (E - E1) / 2 + 0.4\n  pin1: dot\nend","heading":"7. Names, units and arithmetic","id":"tutorial-7-names-units-and-arithmetic","runnable":true},{"code":"template soic(n, D, lspan = 6.0)\n  name: \"SOIC-{n}_3.9x{D}mm_P1.27mm\"\n  description: \"SOIC, {n} pins, 3.9 x {D} mm body\"\n  refdes: \"U\"\n  body 3.9 x D x 1.5\n  pins n dual pitch: 1.27\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: lspan\n  pin1: dot\nend\n\nfootprints from soic\n  n    D\n  8    4.9\n  14   8.65\n  16   9.9\nend","heading":"8. A family from one template","id":"tutorial-8-a-family-from-one-template","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"CP-RADIAL-6.3x11\"\n  description: \"Radial electrolytic, 6.3 mm can, 2.5 mm lead spacing\"\n  refdes: \"C\"\n  body cylinder d: 6.3 h: 11\n  pins 2 horizontal pitch: 2.5\n  lead wire d: 0.5\n  default pad tht circle 1.4 drill: 0.8\n  polarized                            # the stripe goes by the negative lead\n  model\n    body color: \"#2b5a3a\"              # a green sleeve\n    leads color: tin\n  end\nend","heading":"9. The 3D model","id":"tutorial-9-the-3d-model","runnable":true},{"code":"feature arrowhead(s = 0.6)\n  polygon (0, 0) (-s, s / 2) (-s, -s / 2) fill\nend\n\nfootprint \"SOT-23\"\n  body 1.3 x 2.9 x 1.0\n  pins\n    left: 1, 2 pitch: 1.9\n    right: 3\n  end\n  lead gullwing 0.4 x 0.5 span: 2.4\n  pin1: arrowhead                      # the pin-1 mark, where the dot would go\n  feature arrowhead(0.8) at (2.4, 0) rotate: 180   # and one pointing at pin 3\nend","heading":"10. Features of your own","id":"tutorial-10-features-of-your-own","runnable":true},{"code":"feature fin_stack(W, L, h)\n  box W x L x 0.3 color: aluminum\n  box W x 0.4 x h at (0, -L / 3, 0) color: aluminum\n  box W x 0.4 x h at (0, L / 3, 0) color: aluminum\n  section tall\n    box W x 0.4 x 2 * h at (0, 0, 0) color: aluminum\n  end\nend\n\nfootprint \"QFN-16-HS\"\n  body 3 x 3 x 0.9\n  pins 16 quad pitch: 0.5\n  lead flat 0.25 x 0.4\n  thermal pad 1.7 x 1.7\n  feature fin_stack(2.6, 2.6, 1.5, tall) on body\nend","heading":"10. Features of your own","id":"tutorial-10-features-of-your-own","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"TO-220-3_Vertical\"\n  refdes: \"U\"\n  body 10.2 x 4.5 x 15.6\n  pins grid 3 x 1 pitch: 2.54\n  lead wire 0.8 x 0.5\n  default pad tht oval 1.8 x 2.4 drill: 1.2\n  pin1: triangle(a: -90)               # a symbol from the library\n  model\n    body shape: to220                  # a package from the library\n  end\nend","heading":"11. The standard library","id":"tutorial-11-the-standard-library","runnable":true}]},{"key":"reference","nav":"Language reference","title":"Language reference","file":"docs/reference.md","html":"<h1 id=\"reference-language-reference\">Language reference</h1>\n<p>stomp describes a PCB footprint in a text file: the land pattern (copper, drills, mask,\npaste, silkscreen, courtyard, assembly drawing) and, if you want it, a 3D model. You can\ngive the pads directly (<em>land mode</em>), describe the part and let stomp derive them (<em>part\nmode</em>), or mix the two. This page lists every statement by task, with links to the\n<a href=\"#spec\">specification</a> for the fine print. The <a href=\"#tutorial\">tutorial</a> teaches the\nlanguage step by step, and <a href=\"#using\">using stomp</a> covers the editor.</p>\n<p>In syntax lines, CAPITALS are values you supply, <code>[ ]</code> marks an optional part, <code>…</code> means\nmore of the same, and <code>|</code> separates choices. Examples with a <code>footprint</code> line are\ncomplete files.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-contents\">Contents<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-contents\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-file-structure\">File structure</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-numbers-units-and-sizes\">Numbers, units and sizes</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-footprints-and-metadata\">Footprints and metadata</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-pads-and-holes\">Pads and holes</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-placement\">Placement</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-part-mode\">Part mode</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-pin-1-and-polarity\">Pin 1 and polarity</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-silk-assembly-and-courtyard\">Silk, assembly and courtyard</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-templates-tables-and-inheritance\">Templates, tables and inheritance</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-features\">Features</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-the-3d-model\">The 3D model</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#reference-diagnostics\">Diagnostics</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"reference-file-structure\">File structure<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-file-structure\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A file holds footprints, <code>footprints</code> tables, templates, features, named values and\n<code>units:</code> lines, in any order: a footprint can use a name, template or feature defined\nfurther down. <code>#</code> starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.\n<a href=\"#spec-5-files-footprints-metadata\">SPEC §5</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"0\"><code>L = 1.6                                 # a name for the datasheet's L\n\nfootprint &quot;R_0603_1608Metric&quot;           # a block: its header line,\n  description: &quot;Resistor, {L} mm long&quot;  # its statements,\n  refdes: &quot;R&quot;\n  body L x 0.8 x 0.45; pins 2 horizontal  # (two on one line)\n  lead endcap 0.3\nend                                     # then `end`</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-statements-and-blocks\">Statements and blocks<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-statements-and-blocks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A statement takes one line, and <code>;</code> separates statements that share one. A newline\ninside parentheses, or after a <code>,</code> or an operator, doesn't end a statement. A block is a\nheader line, its statements and <code>end</code>. Blocks nest, and stomp goes by <code>end</code>, not\nindentation: <code>section fine; c = 0.15; end</code> is a whole block.\n<a href=\"#spec-4-lexical-structure\">SPEC §4</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-named-values-keywords-and-names\">Named values, keywords and names<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-named-values-keywords-and-names\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A word with a colon names the value after it: <code>pitch: 1.27</code> (the space is optional).\nWords that relate things (<code>at</code>, <code>is</code>, <code>of</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>with</code>, <code>and</code>, …) and flags (<code>fill</code>,\n<code>tht</code>, <code>inner</code>, …) take no colon. Keywords count only where the grammar expects them, so\n<code>draft</code> or <code>pitch</code> can name a value; only <code>x</code> and <code>end</code> are reserved everywhere.</p>\n<p><code>NAME = value</code> names a value for the file or inside one footprint: type a datasheet's\nletter dimensions in once. A name gets one value per level, and a footprint's names hide\nthe file's. Pads, holes, rows and groups are named by a whole number, an identifier or a\nstring (<code>pad 14</code>, <code>hole M1</code>, <code>pad &quot;D+&quot;</code>); one named after a keyword is quoted, <code>pad &quot;at&quot;</code>.\n<a href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\">SPEC §7</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-strings-and-interpolation\">Strings and interpolation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-strings-and-interpolation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A string is <code>&quot;…&quot;</code> on one line, with the escapes <code>\\&quot;</code>, <code>\\\\</code>, <code>\\{</code> and <code>\\}</code>. In a string,\n<code>{expr}</code> becomes its value: a number in its shortest form, a length in the units in\nforce, text as written; <code>{expr:.N}</code> prints N decimals. Footprint and pad names,\ndescriptions and text interpolate: <code>thermal pad &quot;{n + 1}&quot; 3.45 x 3.45</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-numbers-units-and-sizes\">Numbers, units and sizes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-numbers-units-and-sizes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"reference-units\">Units<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-units\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Lengths are in mm unless <code>units: mm|um|nm|mil|in</code> says otherwise: at the top level for\nthe lines after it, or as the first statement of a footprint or template for that block.\nAny number can carry a unit, attached (<code>0.65mm</code>, <code>50mil</code>), and units mix freely. A plain\nnumber takes a unit only where it's used as a length, so in a template that starts with\n<code>units: mil</code>, a default or table cell of <code>375</code> is 375 mil.\n<a href=\"#spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\">SPEC §6</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-sizes-side-marks-percentages-and-angles\">Sizes, side marks, percentages and angles<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-sizes-side-marks-percentages-and-angles\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>W x L</code> is a 2D size: the width along x and the length along y, before any rotation\n(generated pads read it <a href=\"#reference-where-pad-geometry-comes-from\">across the lead</a>). If a drawing\ngives the height first, mark a side <code>w</code> or <code>h</code>: <code>rect 1.6h x 1.1w</code> is <code>rect 1.1 x 1.6</code>.\nThe mark goes after any unit (<code>43milw</code>), and a name takes parentheses (<code>(D)h</code>); a <code>body</code>\nor <code>box</code> is always <code>W x L x H</code>. A percentage (<code>25%</code>) sets a corner radius or a paste\ncoverage. Angles are degrees counterclockwise from +x: <code>45</code>, <code>45deg</code>, <code>0.7854rad</code>.\n<a href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\">SPEC §7</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-expressions-and-functions\">Expressions and functions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-expressions-and-functions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Expressions use <code>+ - * /</code> and parentheses: <code>(6.4 - 4.4) / 2</code>, <code>span / 2 - 0.3mm</code>. A\nlength times a length is a unit error, and a length over a length is a plain ratio. A\nname touching its <code>(</code> is a call. <code>pi</code> is 3.14159…, and none of these names is reserved.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>function</th><th>gives</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>min(a, …)</code>, <code>max(a, …)</code>, <code>clamp(v, lo, hi)</code></td><td>the smallest or largest argument; v kept between lo and hi</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>abs(a)</code>, <code>hypot(a, …)</code>, <code>sqrt(a)</code></td><td>the absolute value; the square root of the sum of squares; the square root of a plain number (not a length)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sin(a)</code>, <code>cos(a)</code>, <code>tan(a)</code></td><td>of an angle in degrees, exact at right angles</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>asin(a)</code>, <code>acos(a)</code>, <code>atan2(y, x)</code></td><td>an angle in degrees</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>mix(c1, c2, t)</code>, <code>metal(c)</code></td><td>a color t of the way from c1 to c2 (t from 0 to 1); color c, rendered as shiny metal</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-colors\">Colors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-colors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A color is <code>&quot;#rrggbb&quot;</code>, a built-in name, a <a href=\"#library-colorsstomp\">palette</a> name\nfrom the standard library (<code>can_blue</code>, <code>ferrite</code>, <code>led_green</code>, …), <code>mix(…)</code> or\n<code>metal(…)</code>. The built-in names are <code>black</code>, <code>gray</code>, <code>white</code>, <code>red</code>, <code>green</code>, <code>blue</code>,\n<code>yellow</code>, <code>orange</code>, <code>brown</code>, <code>tan</code>, <code>ceramic</code> and the shiny metals <code>tin</code>, <code>silver</code>,\n<code>gold</code>, <code>copper</code> and <code>aluminum</code>. Your own names come first, then the built-ins, then the\npalette. Colors take no arithmetic. <a href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\">SPEC §7</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-footprints-and-metadata\">Footprints and metadata<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-footprints-and-metadata\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>footprint &quot;NAME&quot;</code>, its statements, then <code>end</code>. Each footprint in a file needs a name of\nits own; <code>extends</code> and <code>from</code> make one from another (see\n<a href=\"#reference-templates-tables-and-inheritance\">templates</a>). These statements are optional:\n<a href=\"#spec-5-files-footprints-metadata\">SPEC §5</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>statement</th><th>holds</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>description: &quot;…&quot;</code>, <code>datasheet: &quot;…&quot;</code></td><td>a one-line description; the datasheet's URL</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>manufacturer: &quot;…&quot;</code>, <code>mpn: &quot;…&quot;</code></td><td>the maker and its part number</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>refdes: &quot;…&quot;</code></td><td>the reference designator prefix, the U of U3: the assembly label shows <code>U?</code>, and it sets the <a href=\"#reference-part-families\">part family</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>tags: …</code></td><td>keywords separated by commas: words, numbers or strings</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>density: least|nominal|most</code></td><td>the IPC-7351 density level</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>IPC-7351 is the standard for surface-mount land patterns. Its <code>most</code> density gives the\nlargest lands and courtyard (hand soldering, rugged boards), <code>least</code> the smallest (dense\nboards), and <code>nominal</code> is the default. The level also sizes through-hole drills.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-the-origin\">The origin<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-the-origin\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The origin, (0, 0), is the body's center for a part-mode package, and for a layout placed\nonly by relations, the center of its body or land pattern. Once you place something\n<code>at (x, y)</code>, coordinates mean what you wrote. <code>origin at pad 1</code>, <code>origin at (x, y)</code> or\n<code>origin centered between A and B</code> moves it, and <code>origin</code> is an object to measure from.\n<a href=\"#spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\">SPEC §6</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-pads-and-holes\">Pads and holes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-pads-and-holes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"reference-pad-shapes-and-holes\">Pad shapes and holes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-pad-shapes-and-holes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>pad NAME [tht] SHAPE [PROPERTY …]</code> makes a pad. With <code>tht</code> it's a plated through-hole\npad: the shape is the copper around the hole, and <code>drill: D</code> or <code>drill: slot W x L</code> is\nthe hole (<code>pad 1 tht circle 6.4 drill: 3.2</code>). <code>hole NAME D</code> or <code>hole NAME slot W x L</code>\nmakes a non-plated hole (NPTH) for a peg or a screw; place it like a pad.\n<a href=\"#spec-10-pads-and-holes\">SPEC §10</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>shape</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>rect W x L</code>, <code>oval W x L</code></td><td>a rectangle; an obround, with fully rounded ends</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>circle D</code></td><td>a circle D across</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>roundrect W x L r: R</code></td><td>rounded corners: R is a length, or a percentage of the shorter side</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>dshape W x L flat: SIDE</code></td><td>a D, flat on SIDE (<code>left</code> if left out) and round at the other end</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>polygon (x, y) (x, y) (x, y) …</code></td><td>three or more corners, around their centroid</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-pad-properties-and-the-default-pad\">Pad properties and the default pad<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-pad-properties-and-the-default-pad\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Properties follow the shape in any order, or change a pad on a <code>pad NAME</code> line of their\nown; a <code>mask:</code> or <code>paste:</code> value needs its sign. <code>default pad [tht] SHAPE [PROPERTY …]</code>\nis the geometry of every pad without its own, generated pads included.\n<a href=\"#spec-102-pad-properties\">SPEC §10.2</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>property</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>rotate: A</code></td><td>turns the pad counterclockwise about its center</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>mask: +E</code>, <code>mask: -E</code></td><td>grows or shrinks the solder-mask opening by E per side; by default it's 0.05 mm larger than the pad</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>mask: none</code>, <code>mask: defined</code></td><td>no opening; a solder-mask-defined pad, whose opening sets the land</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>paste: +E</code>, <code>paste: -E</code>, <code>paste: none</code></td><td>grows or shrinks the paste stencil opening, or leaves it out</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>paste: divide NX x NY [coverage: P%]</code></td><td>paste in NX x NY windows covering P% of the pad, 50% if left out</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>paste: divide NX x NY gutter: G</code></td><td>the same windows, G apart</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>vias: NX x NY drill: D pitch: P</code></td><td>an array of thermal vias in the pad</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-changing-and-deleting-pads\">Changing and deleting pads<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-changing-and-deleting-pads\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>pad NAME PROPERTY …</code> changes one pad and <code>pads SELECTOR PROPERTY …</code> several; a selector\nlists names and ranges (<code>1..4, 9</code>, <code>A1..A8</code>, <code>8..5</code>). Both also take <code>width:</code>, <code>length:</code>,\n<code>size: W x L</code>, <code>shape: SHAPE</code> and <code>drill:</code>. A <code>pad NAME SHAPE</code> line for a pad that\nexists already replaces its geometry, and <code>delete pad|hole|ball NAME</code> removes one.\n<a href=\"#spec-103-defaults-selectors-overrides-deletion\">SPEC §10.3</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"1\"><code>footprint &quot;DIP-7_W7.62mm&quot;               # an 8-pin DIP without pin 6, for creepage\n  body 6.35 x 9.2 x 3.3\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 2.54 span: 7.62 centers\n  lead pin 0.46 x 0.25\n  default pad tht oval 1.6 x 2.0 drill: 0.8\n  pad 1 tht rect 1.6 x 2.0 drill: 0.8   # replaces pad 1: a square pin 1\n  pads 5, 7 mask: +0.1\n  delete pad 6\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"reference-placement\">Placement<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-placement\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Positions come from placements, in any order. +x is right and +y is up, seen from above,\nrelative to the origin. Objects are named by kind and name: <code>pad 3</code>, <code>hole M1</code>, <code>row A</code>,\n<code>group G</code>, <code>ball A1</code>, <code>side left</code> (one side's pads), <code>body</code> and <code>origin</code>; the kind can go\nwhere the name is unambiguous. <a href=\"#spec-9-placement-and-dimensioning\">SPEC §9</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"2\"><code>footprint &quot;TO-252&quot;\n  pad 1 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 3 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 2 rect 5.5 x 6.2                  # the tab\n  pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1          # center to center\n  pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally\n  pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1        # the tab's near edge to the pin centers\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-at-relations-and-anchors\">At, relations and anchors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-at-relations-and-anchors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>OBJECT at (x, y)</code> fixes an object's center.\n<code>TARGET [ANCHOR] is DIST left of|right of|above|below REF [ANCHOR]</code> puts the target DIST\nfrom the reference on one axis, and weakly lines their centers up on the other until a\nstatement places it. <code>and</code> chains clauses, each with its own distance, direction and\nreference: <code>hole PEG1 is 2.89 left of pad 1 and 0.55 below pad 1</code>. An anchor picks the\nedge measured, on the box after rotation (for a row, group or side, the box around its\nmembers). <a href=\"#spec-92-directional-relations-is--of\">SPEC §9.2</a>,\n<a href=\"#spec-82-anchors\">§8.2</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>anchor</th><th>measures</th><th>for example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>none, or <code>center</code></td><td>the center</td><td><code>pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>outer</code></td><td>the edge facing away from the other object</td><td><code>pad 8 outer is 6.0 right of pad 1 outer</code>: a span</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>inner</code></td><td>the edge facing the other object</td><td><code>pad 2 inner is 0.4 right of pad 1 inner</code>: a gap</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code></td><td>that edge, on the relation's axis</td><td><code>body bottom is 1.1 above pad 1</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-mirrors-and-measured-pairs\">Mirrors and measured pairs<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-mirrors-and-measured-pairs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>A mirrors B across x|y</code> makes A the mirror image of B about the footprint's x or y axis,\ncreating A if needed. <code>span A to B is D</code>, <code>gap A to B is D</code> and <code>pitch A to B is D</code>\nmeasure outer edges, facing edges and centers, along the axis of a mirror or <code>align</code>\nbetween the two, or of <code>horizontally</code> or <code>vertically</code> added at the end. One measurement\nplaces a mirrored pair not yet placed: B on the -x side for <code>across y</code>, on the +y side\nfor <code>across x</code>. Otherwise it places whichever is free, B on A's positive side.\n<a href=\"#spec-94-symmetry-mirrors-and-measured-pairs-span--gap--pitch\">SPEC §9.4</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"3\"><code>pad &quot;SH4&quot; mirrors pad &quot;SH3&quot; across y    # SH3 takes the -x side\nspan pad &quot;SH3&quot; to pad &quot;SH4&quot; is 8.0</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-centering-alignment-rows-and-groups\">Centering, alignment, rows and groups<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-centering-alignment-rows-and-groups\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>statement</th><th>places</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>T is centered between A and B [horizontally|vertically]</code></td><td>T at the midpoint of A's and B's centers</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>T is centered on A [horizontally|vertically]</code></td><td>T's center on A's</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>align A with B horizontally|vertically</code></td><td>A on one horizontal line with B (the same y), or one vertical line (the same x)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>row NAME pads SELECTOR pitch: P [going: right|left|up|down]</code></td><td>the pads in order, P apart, going right unless told; the row is one object to place</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>group NAME = OBJECT, OBJECT, …</code></td><td>nothing: it's one bounding box to measure against</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>horizontally</code> always means along a horizontal line: centering moves x, aligning shares\ny. A row makes the pads it names, which then need a shape (<code>default pad</code>, say). Groups and\nsides can't be placed: place their members. <a href=\"#spec-95-centering-and-alignment\">SPEC §9.5</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-constraint-rules\">Constraint rules<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-constraint-rules\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Each axis of each object takes its position from exactly one strong statement: <code>at</code>,\na relation, <code>centered</code>, <code>align</code>, <code>mirrors</code> or a measured pair. A second one is an\nover-constraint error that cites both statements.</li>\n<li>Weak defaults fill the axes left: a relation's cross-axis alignment, then generated\npositions. An axis with no position is an under-constraint error naming the object.</li>\n<li>Order doesn't matter, references can point forward, and circular placements are\nerrors that list the cycle. <a href=\"#spec-97-constraint-semantics\">SPEC §9.7</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"reference-part-mode\">Part mode<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-part-mode\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Describe the component as its drawing does, and stomp derives the pads, IPC-7351 style,\nand the 3D model; explicit pads still win. The comments use JEDEC letters, which package\ndrawings use for dimensions: <code>e</code> the pitch, <code>b</code> the lead width, <code>L</code> the foot length, <code>A</code>\nthe seated height, <code>A1</code> the standoff. <a href=\"#spec-11-parametric-part-description\">SPEC §11</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"4\"><code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8&quot;\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5                  # E1 x D x A\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27               # e; pin 1 at the top left\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0   # b x L; the span E, tip to tip\n  pin1: dot\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-body\">Body<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-body\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>One per footprint: it shapes the automatic silk, assembly outline, courtyard and 3D\nmodel, and it's an object to measure from. Unless placed, it's centered on the generated\npins or the land pattern. <code>body W x L x H</code> is a box, with H the seated height; without H\nthe model is a flat plate. <code>body cylinder d: D h: H</code> is a can standing on the board, or\nlying along its pins for end caps (a MELF) and for wires outside its circle (an axial\npart). <a href=\"#spec-111-body\">SPEC §11.1</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-pins\">Pins<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-pins\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>form</th><th>pins</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>pins N dual</code></td><td>two rows, left and right, counterclockwise from pin 1 at the top left</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pins N quad</code></td><td>four sides, counterclockwise from the top left; N a multiple of 4</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pins 2 horizontal</code>, <code>pins 2 vertical</code></td><td>two terminals, pin 1 on the left, or at the top</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pins N single SIDE</code></td><td>one row along SIDE: <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>top</code> or <code>bottom</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pins grid NX x NY</code></td><td>NX columns by NY rows</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>balls NX x NY</code></td><td>a ball grid: a grid with <code>lead ball</code> and <code>numbering: bga</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Without an arrangement, <code>pins 2</code> is horizontal and other counts dual. These clauses go on\na <code>pins</code> or <code>balls</code> line in any order: <a href=\"#spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement\">SPEC §11.2</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>clause</th><th>sets</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>pitch: P</code>, <code>pitch: PX x PY</code></td><td>the pitch, center to center, one per axis on a grid (<code>pitch: P</code> also works on its own line)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>span: S [outer|inner|centers]</code></td><td>the spacing across the package: see <a href=\"#reference-span-and-gap\">span and gap</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pad: SHAPE …</code></td><td>the default pad for these pins</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>numbering: ccw|cw|zigzag|row-major|col-major|bga</code></td><td>counterclockwise (the default), clockwise, in pairs across the short way as on box headers (the grid default), along each row, down each column, or JEDEC ball names (the default for balls: rows A, B, C, … skipping I, O, Q, S, X and Z, columns from 1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>start: top-left|top-right|bottom-left|bottom-right</code></td><td>the corner numbering starts from; <code>top-left</code> by default</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-side-lists\">Side lists<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-side-lists\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>For irregular parts, <code>pins</code> alone on its line lists the sides, one per line, then <code>end</code>:\n<code>SIDE: NAME, NAME, … [pitch: P]</code>. Positions run counterclockwise: the left side top to\nbottom, the bottom left to right, the right side bottom to top, the top right to left.\n<code>nc</code> leaves a position empty (<code>right: 4, nc, 5</code>). Each run is centered on its side, a\nside without <code>pitch:</code> takes the first one given, and the pins keep the names you list.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-omit-and-only\">Omit and only<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-omit-and-only\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>omit</code> removes generated pins and <code>only</code> keeps just the listed ones. Both take names,\nname ranges and, on a grid, regions: <code>rows R1..R2 [cols C1..C2]</code> or <code>cols C1..C2</code>. The\nrest keep their places: <code>omit A1, H8, rows D..E cols 4..5</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-leads\">Leads<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-leads\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>lead FORM [SIZE] [span: S [outer|inner|centers]]</code> describes every lead. The size is\n<code>W x L</code> (width by foot length), <code>d: D</code> for a round lead, or one number.\n<a href=\"#spec-113-leads\">SPEC §11.3</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>form</th><th>for</th><th>size</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>gullwing</code></td><td>SOIC, SOP, QFP, SOT</td><td><code>b x L</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>jlead</code></td><td>PLCC, SOJ: the lead curls under the body</td><td><code>b x L</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lbend</code></td><td>tantalums, SMA: the foot folds under the body</td><td><code>b x L</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>straight</code></td><td>SOD-123F, SMF: flat leads out of the body's bottom</td><td><code>b x L</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>flat</code></td><td>QFN, DFN and LGA terminals</td><td><code>b x L</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>endcap</code></td><td>chip passives and MELFs</td><td>the cap length T, as wide as the body; or <code>W x T</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ball</code>, <code>column</code></td><td>BGA balls; CGA (column grid array) columns</td><td>the diameter, or <code>d: D</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pin</code>, <code>wire</code></td><td>through-hole pins (DIPs, headers); axial and radial wires</td><td><code>d: D</code> round, or <code>W x T</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>tab</code></td><td>a DPAK-style tab, usually as a per-pin lead</td><td><code>W x L</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>castellated</code></td><td>plated half-holes on a module's edge; provisional, with a warning</td><td><code>d: D</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Gull-wing, J-lead and straight leads need a span, unless the part has two pins and a\n<code>pitch:</code>. Flat, L-bend, end-cap and tab leads reach the body's edges unless a span says\notherwise. Through-hole rows need <code>span: S centers</code> unless they're a grid or two pins.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-per-pin-leads\">Per-pin leads<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-per-pin-leads\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>lead SELECTOR FORM [SIZE] [span: …]</code> gives the named pins a lead of their own, and\n<code>lead SELECTOR none</code> leaves them without a 3D lead. A pin whose land comes from its lead\ngets it from this one, on the same span; with no size, the lead takes the land's. In land\nmode, per-pin leads only add 3D leads: <code>lead 1, 3 gullwing 0.75 x 1.0</code> on a DPAK.</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"5\"><code>footprint &quot;SOT-223&quot;\n  body 6.5 x 3.5 x 1.8\n  pins                                  # a side list\n    bottom: 1, 2, 3 pitch: 2.3\n    top: 4                              # the tab, opposite pin 2\n  end\n  lead gullwing 0.7 x 0.9 span: 7.0\n  lead 4 gullwing 3.0 x 0.9             # the wide tab lead: a wider land too\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-span-and-gap\">Span and gap<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-span-and-gap\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A span measures across the package, between opposite rows: <code>outer</code> (the default, tip to\ntip as datasheets draw it), <code>inner</code> (facing edges) or <code>centers</code>. It goes on a <code>lead</code> or\n<code>pins</code> line, or alone (<code>span: 5.4 centers</code>); <code>gap: G</code> alone is <code>span: G inner</code>. With a\n<code>lead</code>, a span measures the leads, otherwise the pads, as in a chip land pattern:\n<code>pins 2 horizontal</code>, <code>default pad rect 0.5 x 0.6</code>, <code>gap: 0.5</code>. A <code>span:</code> or <code>gap:</code> line\nsets the spacing outright, and in part mode the pads then need <code>default pad</code> or <code>pad:</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-thermal-pads\">Thermal pads<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-thermal-pads\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>thermal pad [NAME] W x L [PROPERTY …]</code> adds an exposed pad centered on the body, named\n<code>EP</code> unless you name it. Change or place it like any other pad.\n<a href=\"#spec-114-thermal--exposed-pad\">SPEC §11.4</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"6\"><code>footprint &quot;QFN-32_5x5mm_P0.5mm_EP3.7x3.7mm&quot;\n  body 5 x 5 x 0.9\n  pins 32 quad pitch: 0.5\n  lead flat 0.25 x 0.4                  # flush with the body's edges\n  pin1: chamfer\n  thermal pad 33 3.7 x 3.7 paste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 68%\n  pad 33 vias: 3 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.1\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-where-pad-geometry-comes-from\">Where pad geometry comes from<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-where-pad-geometry-comes-from\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A pad's geometry comes from, in order: its own <code>pad NAME SHAPE</code> line; a <code>pins … pad:</code>\nclause or <code>default pad</code>; IPC sizing from the lead. With none, it's an error. Change lines\napply on top. Generated pads face out from their side, so for them <code>W x L</code> is the width\nacross the lead by the length along it: <code>default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2</code> on a SOIC is 0.6\nalong the row and 2.2 outward. Surface-mount lands use the IPC-7351 toe, heel and side\ngoals for the density, with corners rounded at 25%. Ball lands are 80% of the ball,\ncolumn lands 0.3 mm wider than the column, and through-hole drills 0.15 to 0.25 mm over\nthe lead, with a ring of 0.2 to 0.4 mm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-pin-1-and-polarity\">Pin 1 and polarity<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-pin-1-and-polarity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>pin1: dot|chamfer|notch|bar|none|FEATURE</code> picks the silk mark at pin 1: by default <code>dot</code>\nfor dual, quad, single and side-list packages, <code>none</code> for two-terminal parts and grids.\nPin 1 is pad <code>1</code> (<code>A1</code> on a ball grid), else the first pin in numbering order. The 3D\nbody gets a mark too: see the <a href=\"#reference-the-model-block\">model block</a>.\n<a href=\"#spec-115-pin-1-marker-and-polarity\">SPEC §11.5</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>mark</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>dot</code></td><td>a dot outside the corner nearest pin 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>chamfer</code></td><td>a chamfered outline corner, the QFN convention</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>notch</code></td><td>a notch in the outline at the pin-1 end, DIP style</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>bar</code></td><td>a bar across the pin-1 end</td></tr>\n<tr><td>a feature</td><td>its graphics where the dot would go, moved out to clear the copper: one of <a href=\"#reference-features\">yours</a>, or a library symbol, <code>pin1: triangle(a: -90)</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>polarized</code> marks a two-terminal part whose pin 1 is positive (the anode) with a silk bar\nand a 3D stripe at the end its family marks: the cathode (pin 2) of a diode or LED and\nthe negative lead (pin 2) of a can, but the positive end (pin 1) of a chip or molded\ncapacitor, as tantalums are marked. A round part gets a hatched half and a &quot;+&quot; instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-silk-assembly-and-courtyard\">Silk, assembly and courtyard<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-silk-assembly-and-courtyard\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"reference-automatic-layers\">Automatic layers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-automatic-layers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Every footprint with a body gets, without another line:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>silk:</strong> the body outline, cut back to clear the copper, and the pin-1 and polarity\nmarks;</li>\n<li><strong>assembly:</strong> the body outline, chamfered at pin 1, and the refdes label, <code>U?</code>;</li>\n<li><strong>courtyard:</strong> the room the part needs, which other courtyards mustn't overlap: the\nsmallest rectangle around lands and body, plus 0.5, 0.25 or 0.1 mm for <code>most</code>,\n<code>nominal</code> or <code>least</code> density (less for chips under 1.6 mm), rounded out to 0.01 mm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Graphics you write add to these. <code>silk: none</code> turns the automatic silk off and\n<code>silk: auto</code> back on, and <code>assembly: none|auto</code> does the same for the assembly layer;\nthe last one wins. <a href=\"#spec-12-graphic-layers\">SPEC §12</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-graphics-and-text\">Graphics and text<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-graphics-and-text\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>graphic</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>line (x, y) (x, y)</code>, <code>polygon (x, y) (x, y) (x, y) …</code></td><td>a line; a closed polygon</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>arc (x, y) r: R from A to B</code></td><td>an arc about (x, y), counterclockwise from angle A to B</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>circle (x, y) d: D</code>, <code>rect (x, y) W x L</code></td><td>a circle; a rectangle, each centered on (x, y)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>hatch (x, y) d: D from A to B pitch: P</code></td><td>chords across the circle at (x, y), D across, at x offsets A, A + P, … below B</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>outline body [offset: O]</code></td><td>the body's outline, pushed out by O</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>text &quot;…&quot;|ref|value at (x, y) [size: S]</code></td><td>a string, the refdes field (<code>U?</code>) or the value field (the footprint's name), 1 mm tall unless sized</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each takes <code>layer: silk</code> (the default) or <code>layer: assembly</code>; text can also go on\n<code>copper</code> or <code>mask</code>, and <code>value</code> defaults to the assembly layer. Graphics take <code>width: W</code>\n(0.12 mm on the silk, 0.1 mm on assembly, if left out); <code>fill</code>, for a circle, rect or\npolygon, which then has no width; and <code>clear</code>, which cuts lines and outlines back from\nthe copper as the automatic outline is.</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"7\"><code>footprint &quot;LED_0603_1608Metric&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;D&quot;\n  body 1.6 x 0.8 x 0.6\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead endcap 0.3\n  polarized                             # a bar by the cathode, pin 2\n  text &quot;&gt;K&quot; at (1.9, 0) size: 0.5\n  rect (0, 0) 2.4 x 1.2 width: 0.05 layer: assembly\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-courtyard\">Courtyard<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-courtyard\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A courtyard is always one rectangle. The last <code>courtyard:</code> line wins; two in one\nfootprint get a warning.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>form</th><th>courtyard</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>courtyard: auto [C]</code></td><td>the automatic one, the default; C sets the clearance</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>courtyard: rect W x L</code></td><td>W x L, centered on the body</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>courtyard: rect (x, y) W x L</code></td><td>W x L, centered on (x, y)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>courtyard: polygon (x, y) (x, y) (x, y) (x, y)</code></td><td>four corners, with sides along x and y</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"reference-templates-tables-and-inheritance\">Templates, tables and inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-templates-tables-and-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"reference-templates-and-calls\">Templates and calls<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-templates-and-calls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>template NAME(PARAM, PARAM = DEFAULT, …)</code>, footprint statements, then <code>end</code>: a footprint\nwith parameters, names that get their values in each footprint it makes. A default can\nuse other parameters (<code>E = D</code>). <code>footprint &quot;NAME&quot; from TEMPLATE(ARGS)</code> makes one, with\narguments by position, by name (<code>lspan: 10.3</code>) or both; a parameter without a default\nmust be given. End the line with <code>with</code> to add statements, closed by <code>end</code>. There are no\nconditionals or loops. <a href=\"#spec-141-templates-are-functions\">SPEC §14.1</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"8\"><code>template soic(n, lspan = 6.0, e = 1.27, bw = 3.9)\n  body bw x (n / 2 - 1) * e + 2.0 x 1.5\n  pins n dual pitch: e\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: lspan\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-8&quot; from soic(8)\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-16W&quot; from soic(16, lspan: 10.3, bw: 7.5) with\n  description: &quot;wide body&quot;\nend\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-8-HS&quot; extends &quot;SOIC-8&quot;\n  default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2            # longer pads for hand soldering\n  courtyard: auto 0.5\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>name: &quot;…&quot;</code> in a template names its footprints, usually by interpolating parameters, so\n<code>footprint from TEMPLATE(…)</code> needs no name. A name in the header wins, and <code>name:</code> in the\n<code>with</code> block of an unnamed call renames that one. Every footprint, generated or not,\nneeds a name of its own. <a href=\"#spec-142-naming-name\">SPEC §14.2</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-tables\">Tables<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-tables\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>footprints from TEMPLATE[(ARGS)]</code>, a header row of parameter names, one row per\nfootprint, then <code>end</code>. Cells are separated by spaces; a cell is a number with an attached\nunit or minus sign (<code>20mil</code>, <code>-0.1</code>), a name, a <code>&quot;string&quot;</code>, or an expression in\nparentheses, <code>(D - 0.2)</code>. Call arguments apply to every row, a column can't repeat one,\nand other parameters take their defaults. The template must name the footprints.\n<a href=\"#spec-143-tables-footprints-from\">SPEC §14.3</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"9\"><code>template qfn(n, D, D2, e = 0.5)\n  name: &quot;QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{D}mm_P{e}mm_EP{D2}x{D2}mm&quot;\n  body D x D x 0.9\n  pins n quad pitch: e\n  lead flat e / 2 x 0.4\n  thermal pad &quot;{n + 1}&quot; D2 x D2\nend\n\nfootprints from qfn(e: 0.5)\n  n    D    D2\n  16   3    1.7\n  48   7    (7 - 1.4)\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-inheritance\">Inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>footprint &quot;NAME&quot; extends &quot;PARENT&quot;</code>, statements, then <code>end</code>, replays the parent and\napplies the child's statements on top, as <code>SOIC-8-HS</code> does above. Where a footprint takes\none statement (<code>body</code>, <code>pins</code>, <code>lead</code>, <code>default pad</code>, the courtyard, …), the child's\nreplaces the parent's, and so does a placement on the same axis of the same object.\nModel blocks merge. The parent can be any footprint, a generated one included.\n<a href=\"#spec-144-inheritance-extends\">SPEC §14.4</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-features\">Features<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-features\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A feature is a named set of graphics and solids drawn around its own origin: a mark, a\nsymbol, a body. <code>feature NAME[(PARAMS)]</code> at the left margin, its graphics, solids, names\nand sections, then <code>end</code>, defines one. Graphics go on the silk unless they say\n<code>layer: assembly</code>, text stays upright however the feature turns, and <code>outline body</code>\ncan't go in one. <a href=\"#spec-121-custom-features\">SPEC §12.1</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"10\"><code>feature plus(s = 0.7, w = 0.12)\n  h = s / 2                             # a name, from a parameter\n  line (-h, 0) (h, 0) width: w\n  line (0, -h) (0, h) width: w\n  section big\n    s = 1.2                             # with this flag, a new default for s\n    section bold; w = 0.2; end          # only with both flags\n  end\n  section ring, circled                 # with either flag\n    circle (0, 0) d: 1.6 * s width: w\n  end\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;CP_Tantalum_Case-B_EIA-3528-21&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;C&quot;\n  body 3.5 x 2.8 x 1.9\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead lbend 2.2 x 0.8\n  polarized\n  pin1: plus                            # where the dot would go\n  feature plus(big, ring) at (0, 2.6)   # s = 1.2, in a circle\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-placing-a-feature\">Placing a feature<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-placing-a-feature\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>feature NAME[(ARGS)] at (x, y)|(x, y, z)|OBJECT [rotate: A] [on body]</code> in a footprint\ndraws its graphics on the footprint's layers and its solids in the 3D model. A z lifts\nits origin above the board; <code>on body</code>, with or without <code>at</code>, rests it on the body.\n<code>rotate: A</code> turns it about z, <code>rotate: (rx, ry, rz)</code> in 3D. <code>pin1: NAME(ARGS)</code> makes it\nthe pin-1 mark. A feature places another the same way, at a point, up to 32 deep. A plain\nnumber argument takes the unit of the feature's statements: pass <code>40mil</code> from elsewhere.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-sections-and-flags\">Sections and flags<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-sections-and-flags\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A <code>section FLAG[, FLAG …]</code> block counts only when the call passes one of its flags: a\nword in the call naming a section, before or after the values, never between them.\nSections are how a feature chooses; stomp has no <code>if</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>NAME = value</code> in a section gives a parameter or name a new value while it's on. A\nvalue in the call wins, then the innermost section that's on, then the default.</li>\n<li>A nested section needs all of its flags. Two sections that are on can set one name\nonly if one is nested in the other.</li>\n<li>Flags flow down: a feature placed by another sees all the flags above it.</li>\n<li>To pass a value named like a flag, put it in parentheses: <code>plus((big))</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"reference-replacing-a-library-feature\">Replacing a library feature<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-replacing-a-library-feature\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A feature named like one of the standard library's replaces it everywhere in your file,\neven where the library uses it. Yours must take what the library passes; a parameter it\nnever fills gets a warning unless it has a default. The\n<a href=\"#library\">standard library page</a> lists the features and what each takes.\n<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">SPEC §13.5</a></p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"11\"><code>feature pin1_dot(d = 0.3)               # every `pin1: dot` in the file is now a triangle\n  polygon (-d / 2, -d / 2) (d / 2, -d / 2) (0, d / 2) fill\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"reference-the-3d-model\">The 3D model<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-the-3d-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"reference-whats-generated\">What's generated<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-whats-generated\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A footprint with a <code>body</code> has a 3D model without another line, from its body, pins and\nlead form. z is up from the board. H is the seated height (JEDEC A), and the body's\nbottom sits at the standoff (JEDEC A1), which the lead form picks: 0.1 mm under a\ngull-wing body, 0.02 mm under a leadless one, three quarters of the ball under a BGA.\nThe form picks the body too: molded for gull-wing, J-lead, L-bend and straight leads, a\nsawn block for flat ones, a chip or MELF for end caps, a BGA or CGA for balls and\ncolumns, a DIP or header for pins, a can, LED or axial body for wires, a module for\ncastellations. Leads are tin; through-hole leads reach 3 mm below the board.\n<a href=\"#spec-131-the-generated-model\">SPEC §13.1</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-the-model-block\">The model block<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-the-model-block\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>model</code>, settings and solids, then <code>end</code>, in a footprint; a setting given twice gets a\nwarning, and the later one wins. <code>no model</code> turns the 3D output off. Model blocks merge\ndown an <code>extends</code> chain: the child's settings replace the parent's and its solids add to\nthem; <code>no model</code> ends the model until a later <code>model</code> block.\n<a href=\"#spec-132-the-model-block\">SPEC §13.2</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>setting</th><th>sets</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>standoff: Z</code>, <code>height: H</code></td><td>the body's bottom above the board (JEDEC A1); the model's height, where it isn't the body's H</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>draft: A</code></td><td>a molded body's side draft, under 45 degrees; 0 is straight-sided</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body color: C</code>, <code>leads color: C</code></td><td>the body's or the leads' <a href=\"#reference-colors\">color</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>leads thickness: T</code></td><td>the lead-frame thickness (JEDEC c)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>leads below: L</code>, <code>leads above: L</code></td><td>how far through-hole leads reach below the board; how far grid pins rise above their housing (0 makes a socket)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pin1: dimple|dot|notch|stripe|none</code></td><td>the body's pin-1 mark</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body shape: FEATURE</code></td><td>draws the body with a feature: yours, or a library package (<code>to92</code>, <code>to220</code>, <code>hc49</code>, <code>tact_switch</code>, <code>buzzer</code>)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>leads shape: FEATURE</code></td><td>draws every lead with a feature</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>step: &quot;FILE&quot; [offset: (x, y, z)] [rotate: (rx, ry, rz)] [scale: S]</code></td><td>an external STEP model instead; the editor can't load one, so it shows the generated model</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"12\"><code>footprint &quot;CP_Radial_D8.0mm_P3.50mm&quot;\n  refdes: &quot;C&quot;\n  body cylinder d: 8 h: 11.5\n  pins 2 horizontal pitch: 3.5\n  lead wire d: 0.6\n  polarized\n  model\n    body color: &quot;#26476f&quot;               # a dark blue sleeve\n    leads below: 2\n  end\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"reference-solids\">Solids<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-solids\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Solids add what the lead form doesn't describe, in a model block or a feature.\n<a href=\"#spec-133-solids\">SPEC §13.3</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>solid</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>box W x L x H</code></td><td>a box</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>cylinder d: D h: H</code></td><td>an upright cylinder; <code>d: D to D2</code> makes a cone</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sphere d: D [h: H]</code></td><td>a sphere, squashed to H tall</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>disc d: D [to D2]</code></td><td>a flat disc, or a ring from D to D2</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>prism PATH h: H</code>, <code>face PATH</code></td><td>a flat outline, H thick; one with no thickness, facing up</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>loft W x L at Z, W x L at Z, …</code></td><td>a solid through rectangles stacked at heights Z</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sweep W x T [fillet: R] along PATH</code></td><td>a W x T strip along a path in x and z, as leads are drawn</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>wire d: D [fillet: R] along PATH</code></td><td>a round wire along a path in x and z</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>slab W x L x T [castellated]</code></td><td>a board, with a half-hole at each castellated lead</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Options, in any order: <code>at (x, y)</code> or <code>at (x, y, z)</code>; <code>on body</code>, in a model block;\n<code>rotate: A</code> about z, or <code>rotate: (rx, ry, rz)</code> about x, y, then z; <code>color: C</code>;\n<code>name: &quot;…&quot;</code> for the mesh in exports; <code>kind: body|lead|mark</code>, in a feature, for its\nhighlight; <code>segments: N</code>; <code>open</code>, <code>open top</code> or <code>open bottom</code> for a cylinder without\nends; <code>from A to B</code> for part of a cylinder or disc; <code>down</code> for a disc or face facing down.</p>\n<p>In a model block a solid starts centered on the body, on the board, in the body's color:\n<code>at (x, y)</code> moves its center, a z sets its bottom's height, and <code>on body</code> rests it on the\nbody. In a feature, <code>at</code> puts the solid's own origin (the middle of a box's, cylinder's\nor sphere's bottom) at a point of the feature, a size of zero or less draws nothing, and\nplacing the feature puts its solids in the model.</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"13\"><code>model\n  box 2.9 x 2.2 x 0.1 on body color: tin              # a lid on the body\n  cylinder d: 0.8 h: 0.2 at (1.0, 0.6) on body\n  cylinder d: 2 h: 6 at (0, 0, 1) rotate: (0, 90, 0)  # its bottom 1 above the board\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A path is points and arcs separated by commas:\n<code>(0, 0), (1, 0), arc (1, 1) r: 1 from -90 to 0 segments: 8, (2, 2)</code>. A prism's or face's\npath is an outline in x and y; a sweep's or wire's runs in x and z. <code>fillet: R</code> rounds\nthe corners between points, in 10 degree steps (<code>fillet: R step: 8</code> for 8); an arc's own\npoints stay as they are.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference-part-families\">Part families<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-part-families\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The refdes names the part's family, which the library uses for colors and details: chip\nresistors are white ceramic with a black top, chip capacitors tan, inductors ferrite. A\ncrystal or oscillator on a block body is a ceramic package under a metal lid, a diode on\nwires with a round body is an LED, one with J-leads a PLCC LED, and an axial resistor\ngets color bands. The longest prefix wins: <code>R</code> resistor, <code>C</code> capacitor, <code>L</code> or <code>FB</code>\ninductor, <code>D</code> or <code>LED</code> diode, <code>Q</code> transistor, <code>U</code> or <code>IC</code> ic, <code>Y</code> or <code>XTAL</code> crystal,\n<code>X</code>, <code>XO</code> or <code>OSC</code> oscillator, <code>S</code> or <code>SW</code> switch, <code>BZ</code> or <code>LS</code> buzzer, <code>J</code>, <code>P</code>, <code>CON</code>\nor <code>CN</code> connector, <code>F</code> fuse. The family is a flag for every\n<a href=\"#library-families\">library feature</a> too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-diagnostics\">Diagnostics<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#reference-diagnostics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Each problem is reported at the statement that causes it: an error, or a warning when it\nmay be intended. <a href=\"#spec-15-diagnostics\">SPEC §15</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Syntax:</strong> a missing <code>end</code> (at its block's header), an <code>end</code> with nothing to close,\nbraces, <code>pitch 1.27</code> without its colon, <code>at:</code> with one, or a value a statement doesn't\ntake.</li>\n<li><strong>Names:</strong> an unknown object, name, template, feature, flag, color or function, with\nthe nearest match suggested; a name given two values at one level; a duplicate pad,\nhole or footprint name.</li>\n<li><strong>Placement:</strong> an over- or under-constrained axis, citing the statements; a circular\nplacement; an anchor that doesn't fit the axis.</li>\n<li><strong>Values:</strong> unit errors (a length times a length, a ratio for a length), a function\ngiven the wrong kind of value, a misplaced side mark, a late <code>units:</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Part mode and tables:</strong> a pad with no geometry, a count that doesn't fit its\narrangement, <code>omit</code> of a pin that doesn't exist; a row with the wrong number of cells,\nan unknown column, a missing <code>name:</code>. A template's errors show on the call or row.</li>\n<li><strong>Warnings:</strong> overlapping pads, a drill as large as its pad, a courtyard or model\nsetting given twice, <code>polarized</code> on a part that isn't two-terminal, castellated leads,\na library replacement with a parameter the library never fills.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A problem in the standard library is reported at your statement that used it (a <code>body</code>,\n<code>lead</code>, <code>pin1:</code> or <code>body shape:</code>), naming the library features involved.</p>","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"reference-contents","text":"Contents"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-file-structure","text":"File structure"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-statements-and-blocks","text":"Statements and blocks"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-named-values-keywords-and-names","text":"Named values, keywords and names"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-strings-and-interpolation","text":"Strings and interpolation"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-numbers-units-and-sizes","text":"Numbers, units and sizes"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-units","text":"Units"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-sizes-side-marks-percentages-and-angles","text":"Sizes, side marks, percentages and angles"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-expressions-and-functions","text":"Expressions and functions"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-colors","text":"Colors"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-footprints-and-metadata","text":"Footprints and metadata"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-the-origin","text":"The origin"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-pads-and-holes","text":"Pads and holes"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-pad-shapes-and-holes","text":"Pad shapes and holes"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-pad-properties-and-the-default-pad","text":"Pad properties and the default pad"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-changing-and-deleting-pads","text":"Changing and deleting pads"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-placement","text":"Placement"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-at-relations-and-anchors","text":"At, relations and anchors"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-mirrors-and-measured-pairs","text":"Mirrors and measured pairs"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-centering-alignment-rows-and-groups","text":"Centering, alignment, rows and groups"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-constraint-rules","text":"Constraint rules"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-part-mode","text":"Part mode"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-body","text":"Body"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-pins","text":"Pins"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-side-lists","text":"Side lists"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-omit-and-only","text":"Omit and only"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-leads","text":"Leads"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-per-pin-leads","text":"Per-pin leads"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-span-and-gap","text":"Span and gap"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-thermal-pads","text":"Thermal pads"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-where-pad-geometry-comes-from","text":"Where pad geometry comes from"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-pin-1-and-polarity","text":"Pin 1 and polarity"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-silk-assembly-and-courtyard","text":"Silk, assembly and courtyard"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-automatic-layers","text":"Automatic layers"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-graphics-and-text","text":"Graphics and text"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-courtyard","text":"Courtyard"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-templates-tables-and-inheritance","text":"Templates, tables and inheritance"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-templates-and-calls","text":"Templates and calls"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-tables","text":"Tables"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-inheritance","text":"Inheritance"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-features","text":"Features"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-placing-a-feature","text":"Placing a feature"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-sections-and-flags","text":"Sections and flags"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-replacing-a-library-feature","text":"Replacing a library feature"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-the-3d-model","text":"The 3D model"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-whats-generated","text":"What's generated"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-the-model-block","text":"The model block"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-solids","text":"Solids"},{"level":3,"id":"reference-part-families","text":"Part families"},{"level":2,"id":"reference-diagnostics","text":"Diagnostics"}],"snippets":[{"code":"L = 1.6                                 # a name for the datasheet's L\n\nfootprint \"R_0603_1608Metric\"           # a block: its header line,\n  description: \"Resistor, {L} mm long\"  # its statements,\n  refdes: \"R\"\n  body L x 0.8 x 0.45; pins 2 horizontal  # (two on one line)\n  lead endcap 0.3\nend                                     # then `end`","heading":"File structure","id":"reference-file-structure","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"DIP-7_W7.62mm\"               # an 8-pin DIP without pin 6, for creepage\n  body 6.35 x 9.2 x 3.3\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 2.54 span: 7.62 centers\n  lead pin 0.46 x 0.25\n  default pad tht oval 1.6 x 2.0 drill: 0.8\n  pad 1 tht rect 1.6 x 2.0 drill: 0.8   # replaces pad 1: a square pin 1\n  pads 5, 7 mask: +0.1\n  delete pad 6\nend","heading":"Changing and deleting pads","id":"reference-changing-and-deleting-pads","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"TO-252\"\n  pad 1 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 3 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 2 rect 5.5 x 6.2                  # the tab\n  pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1          # center to center\n  pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally\n  pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1        # the tab's near edge to the pin centers\nend","heading":"Placement","id":"reference-placement","runnable":true},{"code":"pad \"SH4\" mirrors pad \"SH3\" across y    # SH3 takes the -x side\nspan pad \"SH3\" to pad \"SH4\" is 8.0","heading":"Mirrors and measured pairs","id":"reference-mirrors-and-measured-pairs","runnable":false},{"code":"footprint \"SOIC-8\"\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5                  # E1 x D x A\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27               # e; pin 1 at the top left\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0   # b x L; the span E, tip to tip\n  pin1: dot\nend","heading":"Part mode","id":"reference-part-mode","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"SOT-223\"\n  body 6.5 x 3.5 x 1.8\n  pins                                  # a side list\n    bottom: 1, 2, 3 pitch: 2.3\n    top: 4                              # the tab, opposite pin 2\n  end\n  lead gullwing 0.7 x 0.9 span: 7.0\n  lead 4 gullwing 3.0 x 0.9             # the wide tab lead: a wider land too\nend","heading":"Per-pin leads","id":"reference-per-pin-leads","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"QFN-32_5x5mm_P0.5mm_EP3.7x3.7mm\"\n  body 5 x 5 x 0.9\n  pins 32 quad pitch: 0.5\n  lead flat 0.25 x 0.4                  # flush with the body's edges\n  pin1: chamfer\n  thermal pad 33 3.7 x 3.7 paste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 68%\n  pad 33 vias: 3 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.1\nend","heading":"Thermal pads","id":"reference-thermal-pads","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"LED_0603_1608Metric\"\n  refdes: \"D\"\n  body 1.6 x 0.8 x 0.6\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead endcap 0.3\n  polarized                             # a bar by the cathode, pin 2\n  text \">K\" at (1.9, 0) size: 0.5\n  rect (0, 0) 2.4 x 1.2 width: 0.05 layer: assembly\nend","heading":"Graphics and text","id":"reference-graphics-and-text","runnable":true},{"code":"template soic(n, lspan = 6.0, e = 1.27, bw = 3.9)\n  body bw x (n / 2 - 1) * e + 2.0 x 1.5\n  pins n dual pitch: e\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: lspan\nend\n\nfootprint \"SOIC-8\" from soic(8)\nfootprint \"SOIC-16W\" from soic(16, lspan: 10.3, bw: 7.5) with\n  description: \"wide body\"\nend\nfootprint \"SOIC-8-HS\" extends \"SOIC-8\"\n  default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2            # longer pads for hand soldering\n  courtyard: auto 0.5\nend","heading":"Templates and calls","id":"reference-templates-and-calls","runnable":true},{"code":"template qfn(n, D, D2, e = 0.5)\n  name: \"QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{D}mm_P{e}mm_EP{D2}x{D2}mm\"\n  body D x D x 0.9\n  pins n quad pitch: e\n  lead flat e / 2 x 0.4\n  thermal pad \"{n + 1}\" D2 x D2\nend\n\nfootprints from qfn(e: 0.5)\n  n    D    D2\n  16   3    1.7\n  48   7    (7 - 1.4)\nend","heading":"Tables","id":"reference-tables","runnable":true},{"code":"feature plus(s = 0.7, w = 0.12)\n  h = s / 2                             # a name, from a parameter\n  line (-h, 0) (h, 0) width: w\n  line (0, -h) (0, h) width: w\n  section big\n    s = 1.2                             # with this flag, a new default for s\n    section bold; w = 0.2; end          # only with both flags\n  end\n  section ring, circled                 # with either flag\n    circle (0, 0) d: 1.6 * s width: w\n  end\nend\n\nfootprint \"CP_Tantalum_Case-B_EIA-3528-21\"\n  refdes: \"C\"\n  body 3.5 x 2.8 x 1.9\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead lbend 2.2 x 0.8\n  polarized\n  pin1: plus                            # where the dot would go\n  feature plus(big, ring) at (0, 2.6)   # s = 1.2, in a circle\nend","heading":"Features","id":"reference-features","runnable":true},{"code":"feature pin1_dot(d = 0.3)               # every `pin1: dot` in the file is now a triangle\n  polygon (-d / 2, -d / 2) (d / 2, -d / 2) (0, d / 2) fill\nend","heading":"Replacing a library feature","id":"reference-replacing-a-library-feature","runnable":false},{"code":"footprint \"CP_Radial_D8.0mm_P3.50mm\"\n  refdes: \"C\"\n  body cylinder d: 8 h: 11.5\n  pins 2 horizontal pitch: 3.5\n  lead wire d: 0.6\n  polarized\n  model\n    body color: \"#26476f\"               # a dark blue sleeve\n    leads below: 2\n  end\nend","heading":"The model block","id":"reference-the-model-block","runnable":true},{"code":"model\n  box 2.9 x 2.2 x 0.1 on body color: tin              # a lid on the body\n  cylinder d: 0.8 h: 0.2 at (1.0, 0.6) on body\n  cylinder d: 2 h: 6 at (0, 0, 1) rotate: (0, 90, 0)  # its bottom 1 above the board\nend","heading":"Solids","id":"reference-solids","runnable":false}]},{"key":"using","nav":"Using stomp","title":"Using stomp","file":"docs/using.md","html":"<h1 id=\"using-using-stomp\">Using stomp</h1>\n<p>stomp runs in your browser: open <code>demo/index.html</code> from the repository, with nothing to\ninstall, or use a copy someone has published. Everything happens on your computer.\nEach time you pause typing, stomp parses the file, expands its templates, generates the\npads, solves the placements and draws every layer and the 3D model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-the-screen\">The screen<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-the-screen\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left:</strong> your stomp file. Problems are underlined and listed under the code; click one\nto jump to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Right:</strong> the footprint the cursor is in, as a drawing or in 3D, with a title block\nand a panel of facts and pad sizes underneath.</li>\n<li><strong>Top:</strong> the <strong>File</strong> menu, the file picker, <strong>Tutorial</strong>, <strong>Docs</strong>, <strong>Export to\nKiCad</strong> and a link to the project on GitHub.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drag the bar between the two sides to change their widths. On a narrow screen they\nstack, the drawing first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-writing\">Writing<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-writing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The drawing follows the footprint your cursor is in. With more than five footprints\nin a file, the tabs above the drawing become a dropdown grouped by table.</li>\n<li><strong>Put the cursor on a statement</strong> to highlight what it makes or moves. A placement\ndraws its dimension: <code>pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1</code> shows the 4.56 between the two\npad centers.</li>\n<li><strong>Hover over a pad</strong> to see its size, position, mask and paste; the lines behind it\nlight up in the editor. <strong>Click</strong> it to jump to the statement that defines it.</li>\n<li>A half-typed line doesn't blank the drawing: while a footprint has a syntax error,\nthe last version that parsed stays on screen, dimmed.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Tab, Shift+Tab</td><td>indent, unindent the selected lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ctrl+/ (⌘/ on a Mac)</td><td>comment or uncomment the selected lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enter</td><td>a new line, indented; after a block's header, one level in</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Esc, then Tab</td><td>leave the editor (Tab alone indents)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ctrl+S (⌘S)</td><td>save your file, or save a copy of an example (see below)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+S (⌘⇧S)</td><td>save as</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ctrl+O (⌘O)</td><td>open files from disk</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"using-the-drawing\">The drawing<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-the-drawing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Drag to pan, scroll or pinch to zoom, and double-click (or <strong>Fit</strong>) to fit the\nfootprint. <strong>mm</strong> and <strong>mil</strong> switch the units of every number shown; the file's own\nunits don't change. The bar under the drawing turns layers on and off:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>layer</th><th>shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Copper</td><td>pads, with their drills and thermal vias</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Paste</td><td>stencil openings, including windowpanes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mask</td><td>solder-mask openings (off at first)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drills</td><td>plated and unplated holes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Silk</td><td>the outline, kept clear of copper, and the pin-1 and polarity marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Courtyard</td><td>the keep-out rectangle around the lands and body</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fab</td><td>the assembly outline and reference</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Numbers</td><td>pad names</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Constraints</td><td>every placement's dimension at once, not only the one under the cursor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grid</td><td>the background grid</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Library</strong>, above the drawing, shows every footprint in the file as a thumbnail,\ngrouped by the table that made it. Click one to open it, or move the cursor through a\ntable's rows to step through their footprints.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-3d\">3D<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-3d\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><strong>3D</strong> shows the part's model on a piece of board. Drag to orbit, shift-drag or\nright-drag to pan, scroll to zoom, and double-click to reset. The bar under it has\nviews from above, the front, the side and below (the board turns see-through, to check\nballs and terminals), and toggles for the board and the courtyard. The statement under\nthe cursor tints the parts it makes: with the cursor on <code>lead 1, 3 gullwing …</code>, those\ntwo leads light up.</p>\n<p><strong>VRML</strong> saves the model for KiCad (<code>.wrl</code>, in KiCad's 0.1-inch units) and <strong>STL</strong> as a\nbinary STL in millimeters. Where the page runs inside another site that saves files for\nit, <strong>Save .zip</strong> saves both in one archive.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-your-files\">Your files<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-your-files\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Examples are yours to change: your edits to each one are kept, and <strong>Reset</strong> puts the\noriginal back. Files of your own are under <strong>My files</strong> at the top of the file picker,\nand the <strong>File</strong> menu works on them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New file</strong> starts one, and <strong>Save as</strong> keeps a copy of whatever is open, an\nexample, a library file or code from the docs, under a name you choose.</li>\n<li>Your files are <strong>saved as you type</strong>: the name above the code says &quot;saved in this\nbrowser&quot; once a change is stored. <strong>Save</strong> (Ctrl+S) stores it at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Rename</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> work on the file that's open.</li>\n<li><strong>Save to disk</strong> writes the open file to your computer as a <code>.stomp</code> file. Where the\nbrowser can (Chrome and Edge), it asks where to put it; elsewhere it downloads.\n<strong>Save all my files to disk</strong> puts every file of yours in one <code>.zip</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Open from disk</strong> reads <code>.stomp</code> files, or a <code>.zip</code> of them, into your files. You can\nalso drop files anywhere on the page. A file named like one you have is kept under\na new name, unless the two are the same.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"using-how-long-the-browser-keeps-them\">How long the browser keeps them<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-how-long-the-browser-keeps-them\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Your files are kept twice in this browser: in IndexedDB, the storage browsers keep\nlongest, and in a second copy in local storage, in case one of them fails. When you\nfirst save a file, stomp asks the browser to keep this site's storage for good. Most\nbrowsers decide without asking you, by how often you use the site; Firefox asks. The\nFile menu says what the browser answered.</p>\n<p>Browser storage still ends when you clear the site's data, and Safari clears a site's\nstorage once you've browsed for seven days without visiting it. So keep anything that matters on disk\ntoo: <strong>Save all my files to disk</strong> gives you every file in one go, and <strong>Open from\ndisk</strong> reads the <code>.zip</code> back.</p>\n<p>Your files belong to the browser and the address you opened stomp at: the same page\nopened from another address, or in a private window, has its own files. Two tabs share\nthem, and a change in one shows in the other.</p>\n<p>Where the page is published inside another site that saves files for it, saving to\ndisk asks you first, and a single file arrives zipped (<code>NAME.zip</code>, with <code>NAME.stomp</code>\ninside), since that host saves only some kinds of file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-the-standard-library\">The standard library<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-the-standard-library\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The library's files, which draw every model and silk mark, are at the bottom of the\nfile picker. Edit one and every model that uses it follows; the drawing shows an\nexample that uses the file while it's open. Your edits apply to the other files too,\nuntil you press <strong>Reset</strong>. To change a model for one file only, write a feature with the\nlibrary feature's name in that file instead. The <a href=\"#library\">standard library page</a>\nlists what's in it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-export-to-kicad\">Export to KiCad<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-export-to-kicad\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Export to KiCad</strong> saves every footprint in the file as a KiCad 9 library: a zipped\n<code>.pretty</code> folder with one <code>.kicad_mod</code> per footprint. Each footprint carries its own 3D\nmodel, embedded, so there's no separate model folder to keep with it. Footprints with\nerrors are skipped, and the message says how many. Where the page can't save files, the\nbutton copies the footprint on screen instead, to paste into a <code>.kicad_mod</code> file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-the-docs\">The docs<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-the-docs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Tutorial</strong> and <strong>Docs</strong> open these pages in place of the editor; <strong>Back to the\neditor</strong>, or the same button again, returns to it. Every page and heading has its own\nlink: copy it from the <code>#</code> beside a heading. <strong>Try it</strong> on an example opens it in the\neditor, and <strong>Back to the tutorial</strong> above the code returns you to where you were.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-choices-this-version-makes\">Choices this version makes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-choices-this-version-makes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Where the <a href=\"#spec\">specification</a> leaves a meaning open, stomp picks one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Generated pads are sized across × along.</strong> For <code>pins …</code>, <code>W x L</code> is the width\nacross the row by the length along the lead, and each side's pads turn to face\noutward. That's how <code>default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2</code> reads on a SOIC.</li>\n<li><strong>The origin rule places floating layouts.</strong> A footprint placed only by relations\n(like the DPAK in the tutorial) has no fixed starting point, so it's solved from its\nfirst object, then centered on the body.</li>\n<li><strong>One coordinate frame.</strong> <code>at (x, y)</code> and graphics use the same coordinates, and\n<code>origin</code> then moves the finished footprint's (0, 0).</li>\n<li><strong>IPC-style lands</strong> use the IPC-7351B fillet goals with nominal part dimensions (no\npart tolerances), F = 0.05 mm and P = 0.025 mm, with corners rounded at 25%. Ball\nlands are 80% of the ball. The mask opens 0.05 mm per side by default.</li>\n<li><strong>Courtyards are rectangles:</strong> the smallest around the lands and body, plus the IPC\nclearance for the density.</li>\n<li><strong>The body's height is the seated height</strong> (JEDEC A), from the board to the top of the\npart. The body sits on its standoff (A1), which depends on the lead form: 0.1 mm for\ngull-wings, 0.02 mm for leadless parts, three quarters of the ball for a BGA.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpolated numbers print in their shortest form.</strong> Lengths print in the file's\nunits, and <code>{e:.2}</code> sets two decimals.</li>\n<li><strong>Small grammar extensions:</strong> <code>omit</code> and <code>only</code> take name ranges (<code>only 1..5</code>), and\nsizes can be written without spaces (<code>0.6x1.5</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"using-not-supported-yet\">Not supported yet<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-not-supported-yet\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>step:</code> files aren't loaded: a footprint that names one shows the generated model\ninstead, and its KiCad export points to the file rather than embedding a model.\nCastellated leads get a temporary land until the spec settles them, though the model\ndraws their plating. Export is for KiCad 9 and later.</p>","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"using-the-screen","text":"The screen"},{"level":2,"id":"using-writing","text":"Writing"},{"level":2,"id":"using-the-drawing","text":"The drawing"},{"level":2,"id":"using-3d","text":"3D"},{"level":2,"id":"using-your-files","text":"Your files"},{"level":3,"id":"using-how-long-the-browser-keeps-them","text":"How long the browser keeps them"},{"level":2,"id":"using-the-standard-library","text":"The standard library"},{"level":2,"id":"using-export-to-kicad","text":"Export to KiCad"},{"level":2,"id":"using-the-docs","text":"The docs"},{"level":2,"id":"using-choices-this-version-makes","text":"Choices this version makes"},{"level":2,"id":"using-not-supported-yet","text":"Not supported yet"}],"snippets":[]},{"key":"library","nav":"Standard library","title":"The standard library","file":"stdlib/README.md","html":"<h1 id=\"library-the-standard-library\">The standard library</h1>\n<p>These stomp files are loaded before every file, in the order below. They draw every 3D\nmodel and the automatic silk marks, and they hold shapes, symbols, packages and colors\nfor your own files to use. They're written in mm, with angles in degrees. How the 3D\nmodel calls them, and with what, is in <a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">SPEC §13.5</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"library-using-it\">Using it<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-using-it\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Place a feature</strong> in a footprint or in a feature of your own:\n<code>feature rounded_box(2, 1, 0.5, r: 0.2, color: nickel) at (0, 0, 1)</code>. A footprint\nplaces its solids in the 3D model and its graphics in the silk.</li>\n<li><strong>Name a package</strong> for a footprint's body: <code>body shape: to220</code> in its <code>model</code> block.</li>\n<li><strong>Mark pin 1 with a symbol</strong>, <code>pin1: triangle</code>, or put one anywhere,\n<code>feature arrow at pad 1 rotate: 90</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a color</strong> wherever a color goes: <code>color: led_green</code>, <code>body color: nickel</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Replace a feature</strong> by defining one with the same name in your file. Yours is used\neverywhere in that file, including where the library places it itself, so\n<code>feature resistor_bands(…)</code> recolors every axial resistor in the file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/tht_parts.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"tht_parts.stomp\"><code>examples/tht_parts.stomp</code></a> uses the packages, shapes and\nsymbols; <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/custom_models.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"custom_models.stomp\"><code>examples/custom_models.stomp</code></a> replaces\nlibrary features and writes a body of its own.</p>\n<h2 id=\"library-families\">Families<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-families\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The refdes's first letters name the part's family, the longest match first. The family\nis a flag for every body, lead and silk mark feature, which is how these details come\nwithout being asked for:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>refdes</th><th>family</th><th>what the library does with it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>R</code></td><td><code>resistor</code></td><td>an axial body gets color bands; a chip a black film</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>C</code></td><td><code>capacitor</code></td><td>chips are tan; a chip or molded body marks its positive end</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>L</code>, <code>FB</code></td><td><code>inductor</code></td><td>chip and sawn bodies are ferrite</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>D</code>, <code>LED</code></td><td><code>diode</code></td><td>a round body on wires is an LED; a J-lead body is a PLCC LED; chips get a lens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>Y</code>, <code>XTAL</code></td><td><code>crystal</code></td><td>a sawn or block body is a ceramic package under a metal lid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>X</code>, <code>XO</code>, <code>OSC</code></td><td><code>oscillator</code></td><td>as a crystal</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>Q</code></td><td><code>transistor</code></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>U</code>, <code>IC</code></td><td><code>ic</code></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>S</code>, <code>SW</code></td><td><code>switch</code></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>BZ</code>, <code>LS</code></td><td><code>buzzer</code></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>J</code>, <code>P</code>, <code>CON</code>, <code>CN</code></td><td><code>connector</code></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>F</code></td><td><code>fuse</code></td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"library-files\">Files<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-files\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"library-colorsstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/colors.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/colors.stomp\"><code>colors.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-colorsstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The palette. These names work in your files too, unless you define the name yourself.\nThe built-in names (<code>black</code>, <code>white</code>, <code>red</code>, <code>tin</code>, <code>gold</code>, <code>aluminum</code>, …) are in\n<a href=\"#spec\">SPEC §7</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>epoxy</code></td><td>black molded plastic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>solder</code></td><td>balls and columns (a metal)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>alumina</code></td><td>white ceramic: chip resistors, CGA bodies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>thick_film</code></td><td>the resistive layer of a chip resistor</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>mlcc</code></td><td>ceramic capacitors</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ferrite</code></td><td>inductors</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>led_white</code>, <code>led_lens</code>, <code>led_mark</code></td><td>a chip LED's body, a white LED's lens, a chip LED's green cathode mark</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>led_red</code>, <code>led_green</code>, <code>led_yellow</code>, <code>led_orange</code>, <code>led_blue</code>, <code>led_clear</code></td><td>radial LED lenses</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>laminate</code>, <code>pcb_blue</code></td><td>a BGA's substrate, a module's board</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>can_blue</code>, <code>rubber</code>, <code>vent_metal</code></td><td>an electrolytic's sleeve, its seal, its vent</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>marking</code>, <code>band</code>, <code>etch</code></td><td>printed stripes, an axial cathode band, an etched pin-1 dot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>resistor_tan</code>, <code>diode_black</code>, <code>cap_blue</code>, <code>inductor_green</code></td><td>axial bodies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>melf_blue</code>, <code>melf_red</code>, <code>glass_band</code></td><td>MELF bodies, a glass MELF's band</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>nickel</code></td><td>lids and covers (a metal)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>solid_color</code></td><td>a model block's solids, when the part has no body</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-shapesstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/shapes.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/shapes.stomp\"><code>shapes.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-shapesstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Solids to build with. Each is drawn around its own origin, and each also takes\n<code>color</code> and <code>name</code> (the mesh's name in the model and the exports).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>rounded_box(W, L, H, r = 0.1)</code></td><td>a box with its vertical edges rounded to radius r, its origin in the middle of its bottom</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>chamfered_box(W, L, H, c = 0.1)</code></td><td>a box with its vertical edges cut at 45 degrees, c back from each corner</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>beveled_box(W, L, H, b = 0.1)</code></td><td>a box with its top edges beveled, b in and b down</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>dome(d, h, segments = 32)</code></td><td>a dome d across and h tall above its origin; the rest of the ellipsoid lies below, hidden in what it sits on</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>torus(d, wd, segments = 12)</code></td><td>a flat ring of round wire d across its outside and wd thick, centered on its origin</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>hex_prism(af, h)</code></td><td>a hexagonal prism af across its flats, which face +y and -y: a nut, a standoff</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>post(b, T = b, z0 = 0, z1 = 1, bevel = …)</code></td><td>a pin from z0 up to z1 with beveled ends: round and b across, or b x T with the flag <code>square</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-symbolsstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/symbols.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/symbols.stomp\"><code>symbols.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-symbolsstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Silk symbols, drawn around their own origin.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>triangle(s = 0.6, a = 0)</code></td><td>a filled triangle s along each side, pointing a degrees from +x</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>arrow(len = 1.2, head = 0.4)</code></td><td>an arrow len long with a filled head, its tip at the origin, pointing +x</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>plus(s = 0.5)</code>, <code>minus(s = 0.5)</code>, <code>cross(s = 0.5)</code></td><td>a &quot;+&quot;, a &quot;-&quot;, an &quot;x&quot;</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ring(d = 0.6)</code></td><td>a circle d across</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>frame(W, L)</code></td><td>a W x L rectangle</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>corners(W, L, len = 0.6)</code></td><td>the corners of a W x L rectangle, arms len long, its sides left open</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>corner_mark(len)</code></td><td>one of those corners: arms down and left from the origin</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-marksstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/marks.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/marks.stomp\"><code>marks.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-marksstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The marks on a body's top: <code>top_marks</code> places <code>mark_dimple</code>, <code>mark_dot</code>, <code>mark_notch</code>\nor <code>mark_stripe</code> by the flag the model passes. <code>polarity</code> says which pin a polarity\nstripe or bar marks (<code>stripe_at</code>): pin 2 of a diode or an electrolytic, pin 1 of a\nchip or molded capacitor.</p>\n<h3 id=\"library-moldedstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/molded.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/molded.stomp\"><code>molded.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-moldedstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_molded</code>, <code>body_dip</code></td><td>a molded body, drafted above and below the parting line, with its top marks (<code>molded_block</code>); a PLCC LED's is white with a lens on top</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body_block</code></td><td>a plain block, for a body with no lead form; a crystal's or oscillator's gets a lid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body_radial</code></td><td>a box body standing on its wires</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lead_gullwing</code>, <code>lead_jlead</code>, <code>lead_lbend</code>, <code>lead_straight</code></td><td>the leads of a molded body</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lead_tab</code></td><td>a plate on the board under the body, as a DPAK's drain tab</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-leadlessstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/leadless.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/leadless.stomp\"><code>leadless.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-leadlessstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_sawn</code></td><td>a sawn block on its terminals; a crystal or oscillator is ceramic under a metal lid, an inductor ferrite</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>package_lid(W, L, t, inset = …)</code></td><td>the metal lid on a ceramic package, t thick, inset from its edges</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lead_flat</code>, <code>lead_endcap</code>, <code>lead_contact</code></td><td>flat terminals, end caps, contacts with no side</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>exposed_pad</code></td><td>a plate under the body, for each thermal pad</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-chipsstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/chips.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/chips.stomp\"><code>chips.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-chipsstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_chip</code></td><td>a chip passive or LED: a ceramic block between metal caps (<code>chip_parts</code>)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body_melf</code></td><td>a MELF, lying on its caps (<code>melf_parts</code>)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body_axial</code></td><td>an axial body with rounded shoulders, lying on its wires (<code>axial_parts</code>), with a cathode band (<code>axial_band</code>) when it's polarized, and color bands when it's a resistor</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>resistor_bands(len, D, first = brown, second = black, multiplier = red, tolerance = gold)</code></td><td>an axial resistor's four bands, 1 kOhm at 5% unless you replace it</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>color_band(D, bw, color = marking)</code></td><td>one band bw wide around a cylinder lying along x</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-arraysstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/arrays.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/arrays.stomp\"><code>arrays.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-arraysstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>body_bga</code> (a laminate substrate under a molded cap), <code>body_cga</code> (a ceramic body),\n<code>lead_ball</code> and <code>lead_column</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"library-cansstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/cans.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/cans.stomp\"><code>cans.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-cansstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_can</code></td><td>a can, sleeved or bare (in a metal color), with its seal and lid; an SMD can stands on a base</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>can_vent(D)</code></td><td>the cross scored in a sleeved can's lid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>can_stripe(D, h)</code></td><td>the printed stripe down the sleeve</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lid_mark(D)</code></td><td>a bare can's mark: a dark segment across its lid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>can_base(D, h, ma = 0)</code></td><td>an SMD can's plastic base, its corners cut on the + side</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-ledsstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/leds.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/leds.stomp\"><code>leds.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-ledsstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_led</code></td><td>a radial LED: a lens with a domed top on a flange whose flat marks the cathode, red unless <code>body color:</code> says otherwise</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>plcc_lens(W, L, color = led_lens)</code></td><td>the round window on a PLCC LED's top</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-through_holestomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/through_hole.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/through_hole.stomp\"><code>through_hole.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-through_holestomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_header</code></td><td>a header's housing: one pitch tall, or as tall as the part (a socket)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lead_header</code></td><td>a header pin (<code>post</code>), or a socket's pin under the opening it sits in</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lead_dip</code>, <code>lead_axial</code>, <code>lead_tht</code></td><td>a DIP's leads, an axial part's wires, plain pins and wires</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-packagesstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/packages.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/packages.stomp\"><code>packages.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-packagesstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Bodies to name with <code>body shape:</code>. Each gets the values the library's own body would\n(<code>W</code>, <code>L</code>, <code>H</code>, <code>z0</code>, <code>body_color</code>, <code>ma</code>, …), and the leads are drawn as usual.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>draws</th><th>for example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>to92(face = -90)</code></td><td>a TO-92: round at the back, its flat face toward <code>face</code> degrees</td><td><code>body 4.8 x 3.8 x 5.8</code>, <code>lead wire 0.45 x 0.4</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>to220(t = …)</code></td><td>a TO-220, standing: the body, and the metal tab behind it with its hole</td><td><code>body 10.2 x 4.5 x 15.6</code>, <code>lead wire 0.8 x 0.5</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>hc49(rim = …)</code></td><td>an HC-49 crystal's metal can on its rim</td><td><code>body 11.5 x 4.65 x 3.5</code>, <code>lead wire d: 0.43</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>tact_switch(cap = …)</code></td><td>a tact switch: a base, its metal cover, and the actuator</td><td><code>body 6 x 6 x 5</code>, <code>lead wire 0.7 x 0.3</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>buzzer</code></td><td>a round buzzer with its sound hole, and a &quot;+&quot; when it's polarized</td><td><code>body cylinder d: 12 h: 9.5</code>, <code>lead wire d: 0.6</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>slot_prism(W, L, h)</code></td><td>a prism W x L with round ends, which <code>hc49</code> is built from</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>top_plus(s)</code></td><td>a printed &quot;+&quot; on a top face</td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"library-modulesstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/modules.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/modules.stomp\"><code>modules.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-modulesstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>body_module</code> (a board, under a shield can when it's over 1.2 mm tall) and\n<code>lead_castellated</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"library-silkstomp\"><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/silk.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/silk.stomp\"><code>silk.stomp</code></a><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#library-silkstomp\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The automatic silk marks: <code>pin1_dot</code>, <code>pin1_bar</code>, <code>polarity_bar</code>, <code>polarity_hatch</code>\n(a round part's) and <code>polarity_plus</code>. They get the style and family flags, so a\nreplacement can draw an LED's mark differently from a capacitor's.</p>","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"library-using-it","text":"Using it"},{"level":2,"id":"library-families","text":"Families"},{"level":2,"id":"library-files","text":"Files"},{"level":3,"id":"library-colorsstomp","text":"colors.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-shapesstomp","text":"shapes.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-symbolsstomp","text":"symbols.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-marksstomp","text":"marks.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-moldedstomp","text":"molded.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-leadlessstomp","text":"leadless.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-chipsstomp","text":"chips.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-arraysstomp","text":"arrays.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-cansstomp","text":"cans.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-ledsstomp","text":"leds.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-through_holestomp","text":"through_hole.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-packagesstomp","text":"packages.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-modulesstomp","text":"modules.stomp"},{"level":3,"id":"library-silkstomp","text":"silk.stomp"}],"snippets":[]},{"key":"spec","nav":"Specification","title":"stomp: a footprint description language","file":"SPEC.md","html":"<h1 id=\"spec-stomp-a-footprint-description-language\">stomp: a footprint description language</h1>\n<p><em>A stomp leaves a footprint. Files use the <code>.stomp</code> extension.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Status: draft 1, syntax and grammar only.</strong> This document pins down the language you\nwrite. Parsing (probably with PEGTL), constraint solving, IPC pad synthesis and 2D/3D\ngeneration are out of scope for this revision. Where the syntax forces a decision about\nmeaning, the spec states the default and marks it as a decision. Open items are\ncollected in <a href=\"#spec-17-open-questions\">§17 Open questions</a>.</p>\n<p>The formal grammar is in <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/grammar.peg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>grammar.peg</code></a>, and worked examples are in\n<a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/tree/HEAD/examples\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>examples/</code></a>. The 3D models and the automatic silk marks are drawn by a\nstandard library written in stomp itself, in <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/tree/HEAD/stdlib\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>stdlib/</code></a>\n(<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>). Every construct in this spec appears in at least one example\nfile. Most appear in real parts; the rare ones are in\n<a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/syntax_zoo.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"syntax_zoo.stomp\"><code>examples/syntax_zoo.stomp</code></a>, which isn't a real part and\ncan double as a parser test later. Not every keyword <em>variant</em> is used; the grammar file\nis the authority on those.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"spec-table-of-contents\">Table of contents<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-table-of-contents\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-1-goals\">Goals</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-2-design-principles\">Design principles</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-3-quick-tour\">Quick tour</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-4-lexical-structure\">Lexical structure</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-5-files-footprints-metadata\">Files, footprints, metadata</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\">Coordinate system and units</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\">Values, expressions, names</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-8-objects-and-anchors\">Objects and anchors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-9-placement-and-dimensioning\">Placement and dimensioning</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-10-pads-and-holes\">Pads and holes</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-11-parametric-part-description\">Parametric part description</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-12-graphic-layers\">Graphic layers</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-13-3d-model\">3D model</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-14-templates-tables-and-inheritance\">Templates, tables and inheritance</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-15-diagnostics\">Diagnostics</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-16-formal-grammar\">Formal grammar</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-17-open-questions\">Open questions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#spec-18-example-index\">Example index</a></li>\n</ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"spec-1-goals\">1. Goals<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-1-goals\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>stomp is a small language for describing electronic component footprints. From one\nsource file, it must be able to generate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>2D land pattern</strong>: copper pads, drills, mask and paste, silkscreen, courtyard and\nassembly outline;</li>\n<li>optionally, a <strong>3D model</strong> of the part (body and leads) for MCAD export and board\nrendering.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are two ways to write a footprint, and one footprint can mix them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Land mode:</strong> describe the pads directly (sizes and positions). Use it when the\ndatasheet gives a recommended land pattern, or when you're copying an existing one.</li>\n<li><strong>Part mode:</strong> describe the <em>component</em> (body size, pins per side, pitch, lead form,\nlead span, thermal pad, ...) and let the tool derive the land pattern (IPC-7351\nstyle) and the 3D model. Explicit pads always override derived ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main feature is <strong>relational dimensioning</strong>: you can place any object by saying it\nis some distance, in some direction, from another object. The distance can be measured\n<strong>center to center, edge to edge, or mixed (center to edge)</strong>. Datasheets dimension\ntheir drawings as a web of offsets between whatever features the drafter chose. stomp\nlets you type those dimensions in <em>as drawn</em>, without converting them to a common\norigin by hand.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-2-design-principles\">2. Design principles<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-2-design-principles\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Type the datasheet in directly.</strong> Every number in a mechanical drawing should go\nin as written: pitches (center to center), spans (outer to outer), gaps (inner to\ninner), edge offsets, letter dimensions (<code>e = 0.65</code>). Variation tables go in the\nsame way, one row per variant (<a href=\"#spec-143-tables-footprints-from\">§14.3</a>). If you have to\ndo arithmetic while typing in a drawing, the language has failed.</li>\n<li><strong>Short for the common case, explicit for the odd one.</strong> A JEDEC-standard package\ntakes 4 to 6 lines. An awkward connector takes more, but is never impossible: you\ndrop from generators to primitives and relations without changing files or\ndialects.</li>\n<li><strong>Declarative, and order mostly doesn't matter.</strong> Statements are facts, not\ncommands. The solver orders them. Contradictions and gaps are hard errors that cite\nboth statements involved (<a href=\"#spec-97-constraint-semantics\">§9.7</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>One namespace, real names.</strong> Pads, holes, rows, groups, the body and the origin\nare all named objects, and any of them can be a dimension reference.</li>\n<li><strong>Generated defaults are weak.</strong> A generator (<code>pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27 …</code>) makes\nordinary objects with <em>default</em> positions and geometry. Any explicit statement about\na generated object overrides the default. So &quot;a standard package, except pad 5 is\nwider and there's a mounting hole&quot; is a generator plus two lines, not a full\ndescription by hand.</li>\n<li><strong>Reads like English, but small.</strong> Statements read aloud (<code>pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1</code>), yet the grammar is a deterministic PEG. Words that relate things read as a\nsentence, and a value with a name says it, with a colon (<code>pitch: 1.27</code>). A block is\nits header line, its statements and <code>end</code>. There are no optional filler words, and\nthere's one way to write each construct.</li>\n<li><strong>The models are stomp too.</strong> Every body, lead and mark the tool draws comes from a\nstandard library of stomp features. A file can replace any of them, so the way a\npart looks is code you can read and change, not something built into the tool.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"spec-3-quick-tour\">3. Quick tour<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-3-quick-tour\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A passive in land mode: pad sizes plus one inner-edge gap, as land-pattern drawings\ngive them:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"0\"><code>footprint &quot;R0402&quot;\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  default pad rect 0.5 x 0.6\n  gap: 0.5                     # inner edge to inner edge between the two pads\n  body 1.0 x 0.5 x 0.35        # optional: enables silk, courtyard and 3D\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A block is a header line, the statements under it, and <code>end</code>. A value with a name\ntakes a colon (<code>gap: 0.5</code>); words that relate things, like <code>x</code> in <code>rect 0.5 x 0.6</code> or\n<code>is</code> and <code>of</code> in a placement, don't.</p>\n<p>An IC in part mode: you describe the component and the pads are derived (IPC density\n<code>nominal</code> unless you say otherwise):</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"1\"><code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8&quot;\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0  # lead width x foot length; span outer to outer\n  pin1: dot\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A DPAK in relational mode: dimensions copied straight off the drawing, each measured\nthe way the drawing measured it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"2\"><code>footprint &quot;TO-252&quot;\n  pad 1 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 3 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 2 rect 5.5 x 6.2                              # the tab\n\n  pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1                      # center to center\n  pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally\n  pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1                    # the tab's near edge to the pin centers\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>pad 2 inner</code> is the tab edge <em>facing</em> pad 1. <code>inner</code> and <code>outer</code> follow from the\ndirection of the relation, so you never have to work out which absolute edge a drawing\nmeant.</p>\n<p>A library, one footprint per row. The template builds each footprint's name from its\nparameters, and a table calls it once per row:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"3\"><code>template qfn(n, D, D2, e = 0.5)\n  name: &quot;QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{D}mm_P{e}mm_EP{D2}x{D2}mm&quot;\n  body D x D x 0.9\n  pins n quad pitch: e\n  lead flat e / 2 x 0.4\n  thermal pad &quot;{n + 1}&quot; D2 x D2\nend\n\nfootprints from qfn\n  n    D    D2\n  16   3    1.7\n  32   5    3.45\n  48   7    5.6\nend</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"spec-4-lexical-structure\">4. Lexical structure<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-4-lexical-structure\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Encoding:</strong> UTF-8. Non-ASCII characters are allowed only in quoted strings and\ncomments.</li>\n<li><strong>Comments:</strong> <code>#</code> to the end of the line. There are no block comments.</li>\n<li><strong>Statements:</strong> one per line, ended by the newline. Several statements can share a\nline, separated by <code>;</code>. Newlines don't count inside parentheses <code>( … )</code> or right\nafter <code>,</code> or an infix operator, which is enough to wrap long statements naturally.\nThere is no continuation character.</li>\n<li><strong>Blocks:</strong> a header line (<code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8&quot;</code>, <code>feature tick(n = 1)</code>,\n<code>section long</code>, <code>model</code>, …), the statements under it, then the word <code>end</code>. Blocks\nnest. The parser goes by <code>end</code>, not by indentation, so a short block fits on one line\nwith <code>;</code>: <code>section fine; c = 0.15; end</code>. There are no braces.</li>\n<li><strong>Named values:</strong> a word followed by a colon names the value after it: <code>pitch: 1.27</code>,\n<code>description: &quot;…&quot;</code>, <code>color: tin</code>. The space after the colon is optional. Words that\nrelate one thing to another (<code>at</code>, <code>is</code>, <code>of</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>along</code>, <code>between</code>,\n<code>across</code>, <code>with</code>, <code>and</code>, <code>on</code>) and flags (<code>fill</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>down</code>,\n<code>castellated</code>, <code>tht</code>, <code>polarized</code>, <code>inner</code>, <code>outer</code>, <code>centers</code>) take none.</li>\n<li><strong>Identifiers:</strong> <code>[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*</code>, case-sensitive.</li>\n<li><strong>Keywords are contextual.</strong> A keyword means something only where the grammar expects\nit, so <code>draft</code>, <code>below</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>d</code> or <code>pitch</code> can name a parameter or a value. Only\n<code>x</code> (the size separator) and <code>end</code> are reserved everywhere. Objects are the\nexception: a pad, hole, row or group named after a keyword is quoted (<code>pad &quot;at&quot;</code>),\nsince a bare one would read as part of the statement.</li>\n<li><strong>Names</strong> (of pads, holes, rows, groups, footprints and templates): an identifier, an\nunsigned integer (<code>1</code>, <code>14</code>: by far the most common pad name), or a quoted string for\nanything else: <code>pad &quot;D+&quot;</code>, <code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8_3.9x4.9mm_P1.27mm&quot;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Strings:</strong> <code>&quot;…&quot;</code> with <code>\\&quot;</code>, <code>\\\\</code>, <code>\\{</code> and <code>\\}</code> escapes. A <code>{…}</code> in any string\ninterpolates an expression (<a href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\">§7</a>), so a literal brace is\nwritten <code>\\{</code>. Strings end at the end of the line.</li>\n<li><strong>Numbers:</strong> decimal, with an optional exponent: <code>1</code>, <code>0.65</code>, <code>.5</code>, <code>1e-9</code>, <code>2.5E3</code>.\nA number can carry a suffix, attached with no space:\n<ul>\n<li>the length units <code>mm</code> <code>um</code> <code>nm</code> <code>mil</code> <code>in</code> (e.g. <code>50mil</code>, <code>0.65mm</code>);</li>\n<li><code>%</code> for ratios (<code>25%</code>);</li>\n<li><code>deg</code> (the default) or <code>rad</code> for angles;</li>\n<li>a side mark, <code>w</code> or <code>h</code>, after any unit: <code>1.1w</code>, <code>43milw</code> (§7). A closing\nparenthesis can carry one too: <code>(a + b)h</code>.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"spec-5-files-footprints-metadata\">5. Files, footprints, metadata<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-5-files-footprints-metadata\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A file holds any number of file-level <code>units:</code> lines and named values (<code>NAME = value</code>),\n<code>template</code> declarations (<a href=\"#spec-14-templates-tables-and-inheritance\">§14</a>), <code>footprint</code> declarations\nand <code>footprints</code> tables (<a href=\"#spec-143-tables-footprints-from\">§14.3</a>), and <code>feature</code> definitions\n(<a href=\"#spec-121-custom-features\">§12.1</a>). Related footprints\n(variants, a family, a whole library) usually share one file. A footprint or\ntemplate can also start with its own <code>units:</code> (<a href=\"#spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\">§6</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"4\"><code>units: mm                                # the file's default unit (mm if not given)\n\nfootprint &quot;QFN-32&quot;\n  …\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;QFN-32-HS&quot; extends &quot;QFN-32&quot;   # inherit and override (§14)\n  …\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-16&quot; from soic(16)        # a template call (§14)\n\nfootprints from qfn                      # one footprint per table row (§14.3)\n  …\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>Metadata statements are all optional, and all take a string except <code>tags:</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"5\"><code>description: &quot;32-lead 5x5 mm QFN, 0.5 mm pitch, exposed pad&quot;\ndatasheet: &quot;https://example.com/qfn32.pdf&quot;\nmanufacturer: &quot;Acme&quot;\nmpn: &quot;ACME-32Q&quot;\nrefdes: &quot;U&quot;                       # reference designator prefix\ntags: qfn, leadless, ipc</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\">6. Coordinate system and units<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>+x is right and +y is up.</strong> Angles are in degrees, counterclockwise, with <code>0°</code>\nalong +x. (This matches Gerber. The y-down convention of some EDA tools is rejected\non purpose.)</p></li>\n<li><p>The footprint is described <strong>from the top side, seen from above</strong>. Placing or\nmirroring it on the bottom side is the layout tool's job, not the footprint's.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>The default length unit is mm.</strong> A file can change it (<code>units: mil</code>), and any number\ncan override it (<code>0.3mm</code>, or <code>12mil</code> in the middle of an expression; units mix\nfreely). Internally, everything becomes int64 nanometers.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>A footprint or template can set its own unit</strong> with <code>units:</code> as its first\nstatement. It lasts until the block's <code>end</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"6\"><code>template dip(n, D, E = 300)\n  units: mil                           # everything in here is in mils\n  body 250 x D x 130\n  pins n dual pitch: 100 span: E centers\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>A plain number has no unit until it's used as a length. Then it takes the unit of\nthe footprint or template that uses it, so a table cell of <code>375</code> is 375 mil in the\ntemplate above, and so is the default <code>E = 300</code>. A number written with a unit keeps\nit. Every statement keeps the unit of the block it's written in, so a footprint\nthat <code>extends</code> a parent in mils can add statements in mm.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>The origin</strong> defaults to the center of the body if there is one, else the center\nof the land pattern's bounding box. You can move it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"7\"><code>origin at pad 1                        # e.g. connectors dimensioned from pin 1\norigin at (0, -1.25)\norigin centered between pad 1 and pad 2</code></pre></div>\n<p>All <code>(x, y)</code> coordinates in the footprint are relative to the final origin. <code>origin</code>\nis also an object you can refer to, with zero size (<a href=\"#spec-8-objects-and-anchors\">§8</a>).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"spec-7-values-expressions-names\">7. Values, expressions, names<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Length expressions:</strong> <code>+ - * /</code>, parentheses, numbers with optional unit suffixes,\nand names: <code>(6.4 - 4.4) / 2</code>, <code>e * 3</code>, <code>span/2 - foot</code>. Multiplying two lengths\nis a unit error; dividing two lengths gives a plain ratio.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Sizes:</strong> <code>WIDTH x LENGTH</code>, two length expressions joined by the keyword <code>x</code>:\n<code>0.6 x 1.5</code>, <code>(a+b) x 1.5</code>. The width is the x size and the length the y size,\n<em>before</em> any <code>rotate</code>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Side marks:</strong> either side of a size can be marked, <code>w</code> for the width and <code>h</code> for\nthe height (the length). A marked side goes in its slot wherever it's written, so\n<code>rect 1.6h x 1.1w</code> is <code>rect 1.1 x 1.6</code>, and one mark is enough: <code>1.6h x 1.1</code>. The\nmark goes at the end of the side, after any unit: <code>43milw</code>, <code>(D - 0.2)h</code>. A name\nneeds parentheses, since <code>Dw</code> would be a different name: <code>(D)w</code>. Marks are for 2D\nsizes only (pads, drills, holes, leads, thermal pads and rectangles); a <code>body</code> or\n<code>box</code> is always <code>W x L x H</code>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Points:</strong> <code>(x, y)</code>. 3D points <code>(x, y, z)</code> appear only where solids go: in <code>model</code>\nblocks, and in features and their placements.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Percentages:</strong> <code>25%</code>, used for the round-rect corner ratio and paste coverage.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Angles:</strong> degrees, counterclockwise: <code>45</code>, <code>45deg</code>, <code>0.7854rad</code>, or any expression\n(<code>a + 90</code>, <code>atan2(1, 2)</code>). <code>rotate:</code>, arcs, <code>draft:</code> and the 3D solids use them.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Functions:</strong> a name touching its <code>(</code> is a call, so a table cell <code>D (D - 1)</code> is\nstill two cells:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>function</th><th>gives</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>min(a, …)</code>, <code>max(a, …)</code>, <code>clamp(v, lo, hi)</code>, <code>abs(a)</code>, <code>hypot(a, …)</code></td><td>as <code>+</code> would combine the arguments: lengths, or plain numbers</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sqrt(a)</code></td><td>of a plain number or a ratio, not a length</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sin(a)</code>, <code>cos(a)</code>, <code>tan(a)</code></td><td>of an angle in degrees; exact at right angles</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>asin(a)</code>, <code>acos(a)</code>, <code>atan2(y, x)</code></td><td>an angle in degrees</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>mix(c1, c2, t)</code></td><td>a color part way, t from 0 to 1, from c1 to c2</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>metal(c)</code></td><td>the color, rendered as a shiny metal</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>pi</code> is a name for 3.14159…. Neither it nor the function names are reserved; a name\ngiven its own value hides <code>pi</code>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Colors</strong> are values too: <code>&quot;#rrggbb&quot;</code>, a built-in name (<code>tin</code>, <code>gold</code>, <code>black</code>;\n§13.2), a palette name from the standard library (<code>can_blue</code>,\n<code>ferrite</code>, <a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>), <code>metal(…)</code> or <code>mix(…)</code>. A name is\nlooked up as a name or parameter first, then as a built-in color, then in the\npalette. Colors can't take part in arithmetic.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Names:</strong> <code>NAME = value</code> gives a name a value, for the whole file or for one\nfootprint:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"8\"><code>e  = 0.65              # type in the datasheet's letter dimensions...\nD  = 6.4\nA1 = 0.05\npins 20 dual pitch: e  # ...then use them by name</code></pre></div>\n<p>A name gets one value at each level: giving it two in one file, footprint or feature\nis an error. A footprint's names hide the file's, a child footprint's (<code>extends</code>, or a\n<code>from … with</code> block) replace its parent's, and a feature's sections can give its names\nother values (<a href=\"#spec-121-custom-features\">§12.1</a>). A name can be any word but <code>x</code> and\n<code>end</code>, keywords included (§4). (Names like <code>A1</code> look like BGA ball names but never\nmean them: a ball reference always has its prefix, as in <code>ball A1</code>.)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Text:</strong> a <code>&quot;string&quot;</code> is a value too: <code>part = &quot;TPS7A02&quot;</code>, a template argument or\na table cell. Text can't be used in arithmetic; it goes inside strings, through\ninterpolation.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Interpolation:</strong> in any string, <code>{expr}</code> is replaced by the expression's value:\n<code>name: &quot;QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{E}mm&quot;</code>, <code>thermal pad &quot;{n + 1}&quot; …</code>. Plain numbers print in\ntheir shortest decimal form (<code>0.5</code>, <code>32</code>, <code>1.65</code>), lengths in the file's units, and\ntext as written. <code>{expr:.N}</code> prints exactly N decimals (<code>{e:.2}</code> gives <code>0.50</code>).\nInterpolation is evaluated where the string is used, so it sees template parameters\nand the footprint's names.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"spec-8-objects-and-anchors\">8. Objects and anchors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-8-objects-and-anchors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"spec-81-object-kinds\">8.1 Object kinds<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-81-object-kinds\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>kind</th><th>made by</th><th>notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>pad</code></td><td><code>pad</code> statements, generators</td><td>copper (SMD or plated through-hole)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>hole</code></td><td><code>hole</code> statements</td><td>non-plated (NPTH), round or slot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>row</code></td><td><code>row</code> statements, generators</td><td>a run of pads in order, at a pitch</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>group</code></td><td><code>group</code> statements</td><td>any set of objects, measured by its bounding box</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>side</code></td><td>generators (<code>pins … dual/quad/sides</code>)</td><td>the pads on one side of the package, grouped automatically</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>body</code></td><td><code>body</code> statement</td><td>the physical outline; one per footprint</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>origin</code></td><td>implicit</td><td>a zero-size point at the footprint origin</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>All names share <strong>one namespace per footprint</strong>: a pad and a group can't have the same\nname. References normally include the kind keyword (<code>pad 3</code>, <code>side left</code>, <code>row A</code>), but\nit can be left out where that's unambiguous (<code>3 is 4.56 right of 1</code> is legal; house\nstyle keeps the keyword).</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-82-anchors\">8.2 Anchors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-82-anchors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Dimensions in drawings measure along one axis, so anchors are <strong>per axis</strong>, written as\na bare word after the reference:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\"><code>pad 8 outer      side left inner      body left      pad 1        (= pad 1 center)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>anchor</th><th>meaning on the measurement axis</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>center</code></td><td>the center (the default; you never need to write it)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>inner</code></td><td>the edge <strong>facing the other object</strong> in the relation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>outer</code></td><td>the edge facing <strong>away</strong> from the other object</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>left</code> <code>right</code> <code>top</code> <code>bottom</code></td><td>that edge of the bounding box (it must match the axis: <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> with horizontal relations, <code>top</code> and <code>bottom</code> with vertical ones)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>inner</code> and <code>outer</code> are what let span and gap dimensions go in as drawn. In <code>B outer is 6.0 right of A outer</code>, A's outer edge is its far left edge and B's is its far right\nedge: the classic lead-span <code>D</code> dimension, typed in as is. Mixed measurements come for\nfree: <code>pad 2 inner is 3.81 above pad 1</code> (edge to center).</p>\n<p>Edges are those of the object's <strong>axis-aligned bounding box after rotation</strong>: the\ncopper's for pads, the hole's for <code>hole</code>s, the outline's for <code>body</code>, and the union of\nthe members' boxes for rows, groups and sides. <code>origin</code> has only <code>center</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-9-placement-and-dimensioning\">9. Placement and dimensioning<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-9-placement-and-dimensioning\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Sizes and shapes are fixed properties; <strong>positions are solved from constraints</strong>. Each\nstatement below adds constraints.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-91-absolute-at\">9.1 Absolute: <code>at</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-91-absolute-at\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"9\"><code>pad 1 at (0, 2.2)\nhole M1 at (-3.5, 0)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Fixes both axes of the object's center. Rotation is a property of the object\n(<code>rotate</code>, §10.2), not part of its placement.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-92-directional-relations-is--of\">9.2 Directional relations: <code>is … of</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-92-directional-relations-is--of\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The workhorse. The general form:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\"><code>TARGET [anchor] is DIST left of|right of|above|below REFERENCE [anchor]</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"10\"><code>pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1                 # center to center\npad 8 outer is 6.0 right of pad 1 outer      # span, outer to outer\npad 2 inner is 0.4 right of pad 1 inner      # gap, inner to inner (facing edges)\npad 2 inner is 3.81 above pad 1              # mixed: edge to center\nhole M1 is 2.89 left of pad 1 and 0.55 below pad 1    # chained with `and`</code></pre></div>\n<p>The exact meaning of <code>T tA is d DIR of R tR</code>, with axis <em>m</em> (x for <code>left</code>/<code>right</code>, y\nfor <code>above</code>/<code>below</code>) and sign <em>s</em> (+1 for <code>right</code>/<code>above</code>, −1 for <code>left</code>/<code>below</code>):</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>coord</em>(T, tA) = <em>coord</em>(R, tR) + s·d  on axis m,\nwhere <code>inner</code> on R is R's edge in direction +s (toward T) and <code>outer</code> its edge in −s;\n<code>inner</code> on T is T's edge in direction −s (toward R) and <code>outer</code> its edge in +s.</p></blockquote>\n<p>A negative <code>d</code> (from an expression, say) flips the direction, with no warning.</p>\n<p><strong>Cross-axis default:</strong> a directional relation also <em>weakly</em> lines up the target's\ncenter with the reference's center on the other axis. A weak constraint is dropped as\nsoon as any explicit statement constrains that axis, so</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"11\"><code>pad 2 is 1.27 right of pad 1        # pad 2 fully placed: y defaults to pad 1's y</code></pre></div>\n<p>is one line for the common &quot;same row&quot; case, and adding <code>pad 2 is 0.3 above pad 3</code>\nquietly replaces the default.</p>\n<p><em>Decision:</em> weak cross-axis alignment. The alternative was &quot;one axis constrained, the\nother an error until stated&quot;, which doubles the line count of every real footprint.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-93-chains-with-and\">9.3 Chains with <code>and</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-93-chains-with-and\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>TARGET is CLAUSE and CLAUSE [and …]</code>. Each clause has its own distance, direction and\nreference, and clauses can refer to different objects:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"12\"><code>pad &quot;SH1&quot; is 1.2 left of pad 1 and 0.35 below body bottom</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"spec-94-symmetry-mirrors-and-measured-pairs-span--gap--pitch\">9.4 Symmetry: <code>mirrors</code>, and measured pairs: <code>span</code> / <code>gap</code> / <code>pitch</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-94-symmetry-mirrors-and-measured-pairs-span--gap--pitch\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"13\"><code>pad 8 mirrors pad 1 across y        # position and shape mirrored about the y axis\nspan pad 1 to pad 8 is 6.0          # outer edge to outer edge\ngap  pad 4 to pad 5 is 0.4          # facing edge to facing edge\npitch pad 1 to pad 2 is 1.27        # center to center</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>mirrors … across x|y</code> mirrors the geometry and position about the footprint's axis.\nIf the prototype's position isn't fixed yet, the pair becomes one symmetric unknown\nthat a single <code>span</code>, <code>gap</code> or <code>pitch</code> measurement then pins down. That's exactly how\npackages are dimensioned (&quot;leads symmetric about the centerline, span 6.0&quot;). In that\ncase the prototype (the object <em>being mirrored</em>, the right-hand operand) takes the −x\nside for <code>across y</code> and the +y side for <code>across x</code>, to match pin 1 sitting at the left\nor top, so the pair is fully determined.</p>\n<p><code>span</code>, <code>gap</code> and <code>pitch</code> are <strong>unsigned, symmetric</strong> constraints. Their axis comes from\nan existing relation or mirror between the two objects. If there's none, add\n<code>horizontally</code> or <code>vertically</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-95-centering-and-alignment\">9.5 Centering and alignment<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-95-centering-and-alignment\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"14\"><code>pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3               # both axes (midpoint of centers)\npad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally  # x only\npad &quot;EP&quot; is centered on body                            # both axes\nalign pad 7 with pad 2 horizontally                     # same y: they lie on one horizontal line\nalign hole M2 with hole M1 vertically                   # same x: one vertical line</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>horizontally</code> always means &quot;along a shared horizontal line&quot; (equal y), and\n<code>vertically</code> &quot;along a shared vertical line&quot; (equal x). English is ambiguous here, but\nstomp isn't: this meaning holds everywhere the two words appear.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-96-rows-and-groups\">9.6 Rows and groups<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-96-rows-and-groups\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"15\"><code>row L pads 1..4 pitch: 1.27 going: down     # 1 at the top, centers 1.27 apart\ngroup mounting = hole M1, hole M2, pad &quot;SH1&quot;, pad &quot;SH2&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>A <code>row</code> places its pads relative to each other (at the pitch, center to center, in the\n<code>going</code> direction) and is itself one object: place the row with any §9 statement\n(<code>row L outer is …</code>, <code>row L at (…)</code>). <code>..</code> ranges run either way (<code>8..5</code>). A <code>group</code>\nadds no constraints; it only gives a shared bounding box to dimension against.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-97-constraint-semantics\">9.7 Constraint semantics<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-97-constraint-semantics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><em>Decision:</em> these rules were chosen for solvability and clear errors. Revisit them if a\nfull solver is ever wanted.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Single assignment per axis.</strong> Each axis of each object takes its position from\nexactly one strong constraint. A second strong constraint on the same axis is an\n<strong>over-constraint error</strong> that cites both statements. That's a feature: it catches a\nmistyped datasheet at compile time instead of as a subtly wrong pad.</li>\n<li>Weak constraints (the §9.2 cross-axis default, and generator default placements,\n§11) fill any axis without a strong constraint. Explicit always beats generated.</li>\n<li>An axis with no constraint at all is an <strong>under-constraint error</strong> that names the\nobject and axis.</li>\n<li>The order of constraints in the file doesn't matter, and references can point\nforward. Cycles that can't be resolved (A places B places A, other than through\n<code>mirrors</code> plus a measurement) are errors that list the cycle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"spec-10-pads-and-holes\">10. Pads and holes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-10-pads-and-holes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"spec-101-pad-definition\">10.1 Pad definition<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-101-pad-definition\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\"><code>pad NAME [tht] SHAPE [PROPERTY …]        # a pad: its shape, then any properties\npad NAME PROPERTY [PROPERTY …]           # change a pad: its shape, size or properties</code></pre></div>\n<p>Shapes:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"16\"><code>pad 1  rect 0.6 x 1.5\npad 2  oval 0.6 x 1.5                    # obround\npad 3  circle 0.45                       # diameter\npad 4  roundrect 0.6 x 1.5 r: 25%        # corner radius: % of the smaller side, or absolute (r: 0.1)\npad 5  dshape 0.6 x 1.5 flat: left       # &quot;D&quot; pad: one flat side, the other end fully round\npad 6  polygon (0,0) (1.2,0) (1.2,0.8) (0.5,0.8) (0.5,2.0) (0,2.0)   # vertices; local origin at the centroid</code></pre></div>\n<p>Through-hole (<code>tht</code>) pads add a drill; the shape and size describe the copper around\nthe hole:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"17\"><code>pad 1 tht rect 1.7 x 1.7 drill: 1.0      # square pad, the classic pin 1\npad 2 tht circle 1.7 drill: 1.0\npad 7 tht oval 1.7 x 2.4 drill: slot 1.0 x 1.7  # slotted hole</code></pre></div>\n<p>Non-plated holes are their own kind:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"18\"><code>hole M1 0.85                             # NPTH, diameter\nhole M2 slot 0.85 x 1.4                  # NPTH slot</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"spec-102-pad-properties\">10.2 Pad properties<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-102-pad-properties\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>These go after a pad's shape, in any order, or on a <code>pad NAME</code> line of their own to\nchange a pad:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"19\"><code>rotate: 45                   # about the pad center, CCW degrees\nmask: +0.05                  # solder-mask expansion per side (default: the stack rule)\nmask: none                   # no mask opening\nmask: defined                # solder-mask-defined pad (the mask sets the land)\npaste: -0.05                 # paste shrink per side\npaste: none\npaste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 68%        # windowpane stencil (thermal pads)\npaste: divide 3 x 3 gutter: 0.3          # or a fixed gutter between windows\nvias: 3 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.1        # thermal via array inside the pad</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"spec-103-defaults-selectors-overrides-deletion\">10.3 Defaults, selectors, overrides, deletion<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-103-defaults-selectors-overrides-deletion\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"20\"><code>default pad rect 0.4 x 1.4        # the prototype for every pad without its own geometry\n                                  # (generated pads included)\npad 5 width: 2.0                  # change one property: keep everything else, widen pad 5\npads 1..4, 9 mask: +0.1           # change several pads by selector\ndelete pad 11                     # remove a generated object (depopulated variants)</code></pre></div>\n<p>The properties that change a pad: <code>width:</code>, <code>length:</code>, <code>size: W x L</code>, <code>shape: SHAPE</code>,\n<code>drill:</code>, and the §10.2 ones. A <code>pad NAME rect …</code> line, with a shape of its own, for a\npad that's already generated <em>replaces</em> its geometry outright.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-11-parametric-part-description\">11. Parametric part description<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-11-parametric-part-description\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Part mode describes the component, and the tool derives the pads (IPC-7351 style,\n<code>density: nominal</code> by default) and the 3D model. All generated objects are ordinary\nnamed objects that you can override (<a href=\"#spec-2-design-principles\">§2</a>, point 5).</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-111-body\">11.1 Body<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-111-body\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"21\"><code>body 5 x 5 x 0.9                 # W x L x H box (molded, default draft and chamfer)\nbody cylinder d: 8 h: 11.5       # radial can\nbody 9.5 x 6.5                   # height optional (no height: a flat model)</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement\">11.2 Pins: count and arrangement<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"22\"><code>pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27          # left and right sides, CCW from the top left (pin 1 top left)\npins 32 quad pitch: 0.5          # four sides, CCW from the top left\npins 2 horizontal                # two-terminal chip, pin 1 left\npins 2 vertical                  # pin 1 top\npins 3 single bottom pitch: 0.95 # all pins on one side\npins grid 5 x 2 pitch: 2.54      # NX columns x NY rows (x count first, like sizes)\nballs 8 x 8 pitch: 0.8           # shorthand for grid + `lead ball` + `numbering: bga`</code></pre></div>\n<p>Clauses that can go on any <code>pins</code> or <code>balls</code> statement: <code>pitch: P [x PY]</code> (a pitch per\naxis, for grids), <code>pad: SHAPE SIZE …</code> (a default pad for this statement),\n<code>span: S [QUAL]</code>, <code>numbering: …</code> and <code>start: …</code>.</p>\n<p>Explicit sides, for irregular parts: <code>pins</code> alone on its line, one line per side,\n<code>SIDE: positions [pitch: P]</code>, then <code>end</code>. Positions are listed <strong>counterclockwise</strong>\n(left: top to bottom, bottom: left to right, right: bottom to top, top: right to left),\nand <code>nc</code> leaves a position empty:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"23\"><code>pins\n  left: 1, 2, 3 pitch: 0.95\n  right: 4, nc, 5                # SOT-23-5: pins 4 and 5 at the outer positions\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each side's run of positions, <code>nc</code> included, is centered on that side. The numbering is\nwhatever you listed; no scheme applies.</p>\n<p>Numbering schemes for the regular forms: <code>numbering: ccw</code> (the default for dual, quad\nand single), <code>cw</code>, <code>zigzag</code> (the default for <code>grid</code>: 1-2 across the short pitch, then\nadvance, as on 0.1&quot; box headers), <code>row-major</code>, <code>col-major</code>, and <code>bga</code> (the default for\n<code>balls</code>: rows lettered A, B, C, ... skipping I O Q S X Z as JEDEC does, and columns\nnumbered from 1; ball names like <code>A1</code> are pad names). <code>start: top-left</code> (the default),\n<code>start: top-right</code> and so on turn any scheme to start from another corner.</p>\n<p>Depopulation:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"24\"><code>omit A1, H8, rows D..E cols 4..5        # by name and/or rectangular region\nonly rows A..B, rows G..H               # the reverse: keep only the listed regions</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"spec-113-leads\">11.3 Leads<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-113-leads\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"25\"><code>lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0  # width x foot length; span outer to outer\nlead jlead 0.5 x 0.9 span: 8.6\nlead lbend 1.2 x 0.8                 # inward L-bend (tantalums, SMA): the foot folds under the body; span optional\nlead straight 0.85 x 0.55 span: 3.6  # flat leads straight out of the bottom of the body (SOD-123F, SMF)\nlead flat 0.25 x 0.4                 # leadless lands (QFN/DFN/LGA); span optional (defaults to the body edge)\nlead endcap 0.35                     # chip terminations; width defaults to the body width\nlead ball 0.4                        # BGA ball diameter\nlead column d: 0.51                  # CGA solder column diameter; on a grid, like ball\nlead pin d: 0.64                     # THT round pin; also: lead pin 0.64 x 0.64 (square)\nlead wire d: 0.6                     # axial or radial wire leads\nlead tab 5.5 x 6.2                   # DPAK-style tab (becomes highest-numbered pin unless named)\nlead castellated d: 0.6              # half-hole edge castellations</code></pre></div>\n<p>A pin whose lead differs from the rest gets its own <code>lead</code> statement that names it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"26\"><code>lead gullwing 0.7 x 0.9 span: 7.0    # SOT-223: pins 1 to 3...\nlead 4 gullwing 3.0 x 0.9            # ...and the wide tab lead, pin 4\nlead 2 none                          # a pin cut off at the body: no 3D lead</code></pre></div>\n<p>The named pins take this lead instead of the general one. Where a pin's land is derived\nfrom its lead, it's derived from this one, measured on the same span, so pin 4's land\ncomes out wider without a <code>pad 4 size: …</code> override. A per-pin lead with no size takes its\nsize from the pin's land. In land mode (explicit pads, no general <code>lead</code>), per-pin leads\nput leads on those pads for the 3D model (<a href=\"#spec-13-3d-model\">§13</a>) and leave the pads alone:\n<code>lead 1, 3 gullwing 0.75 x 1.0</code> on a DPAK.</p>\n<p><code>span</code> qualifiers, wherever a symmetric distance across the package appears\n(<code>lead … span:</code>, <code>span:</code> on its own line, <code>pins … span:</code>): <strong><code>outer</code></strong> (the default:\nlead span as datasheets draw it), <strong><code>inner</code></strong> and <strong><code>centers</code></strong>. The statements <code>span: 5.4 centers</code>\nand <code>gap: 4.0</code> (the same as <code>span: 4.0 inner</code>) set the spacing of the land-pattern rows\ndirectly in land mode, or override the derived spacing in part mode. In part mode,\n<code>span</code> measures the <em>leads</em>; in land mode (no <code>lead</code>), it measures the <em>pads</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Decision:</em> a pad's geometry comes from, in order: an explicit <code>pad NAME</code>, then\n<code>default pad</code> or a <code>pins … pad:</code> clause, then IPC sizing from the <code>lead</code>. If none of\nthese applies, it's an error (&quot;no pad geometry&quot;).</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-114-thermal--exposed-pad\">11.4 Thermal / exposed pad<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-114-thermal--exposed-pad\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"27\"><code>thermal pad 3.7 x 3.7                          # named EP by default, centered on the body\nthermal pad 33 3.7 x 3.7 paste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 68%   # or give it its pin number\npad 33 vias: 3 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.1       # and change it like any other pad</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"spec-115-pin-1-marker-and-polarity\">11.5 Pin 1 marker and polarity<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-115-pin-1-marker-and-polarity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"28\"><code>pin1: dot         # silk dot outside the corner nearest pin 1 (default for dual and quad)\npin1: chamfer     # chamfered silk corner (the QFN convention)\npin1: notch       # DIP-style notch at the pin-1 end\npin1: bar         # bar across the pin-1 end (tantalum or diode style)\npin1: none\npin1: triangle    # a feature (§12.1), yours or the library's, drawn where the dot would go\npolarized         # two-terminal polarity: pin 1 is positive (the anode); adds a silk bar\n                  # and the 3D stripe at the end the part's family marks (§13.1)\ndensity: most     # IPC density level: least | nominal | most</code></pre></div>\n<p>The silk marks these draw (the dot, the bar, a polarized part's bar, or a round part's\nhatching and &quot;+&quot;) are features of the standard library, <code>pin1_dot</code>, <code>pin1_bar</code>,\n<code>polarity_bar</code>, <code>polarity_hatch</code> and <code>polarity_plus</code>, and which end a polarity mark\ngoes on is its <code>polarity</code> feature's to say (<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>). They\nget the same style and family flags as the 3D model's features, so one can draw an LED\ndifferently from a can. A file can replace any of them. The chamfer and the notch\nreshape the outline itself, so they stay built in. The library's symbols, <code>triangle</code>,\n<code>arrow</code>, <code>plus</code>, <code>minus</code>, <code>cross</code>, <code>ring</code>, <code>frame</code> and <code>corners</code>, work as <code>pin1:</code> marks\nand anywhere a feature goes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-12-graphic-layers\">12. Graphic layers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-12-graphic-layers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Everything here defaults to <code>auto</code> and rarely needs writing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>silk</strong> <code>auto</code>: the body outline, kept clear of copper, plus the <code>pin1</code> marker;</li>\n<li><strong>courtyard</strong> <code>auto</code>: the smallest rectangle around the lands and body, plus the IPC\nclearance for the density level, rounded out to 0.01 mm (<code>courtyard: auto 0.5</code>\noverrides the clearance);</li>\n<li><strong>assembly</strong> <code>auto</code>: the body outline plus the refdes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Graphics written in a footprint <strong>add to</strong> the automatic ones. A graphic goes on the\nsilk unless it says <code>layer: assembly</code>. <code>silk: none</code> turns the automatic silk off, and\n<code>silk: auto</code> back on (the last one wins), so <code>silk: none</code> and lines of your own is fully\nmanual; <code>assembly:</code> works the same way.</p>\n<p>A courtyard is always one rectangle: <code>courtyard: auto</code>, <code>courtyard: rect W x L</code>\n(centered on the body), <code>courtyard: rect (x, y) W x L</code>, or <code>courtyard: polygon</code> with\nfour corners and sides along x and y. The last courtyard statement wins.</p>\n<p>Manual additions and overrides:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"29\"><code>outline body offset: 0.15             # follow the body outline, pushed out\nline (-2.5, 1.0) (-2.5, -1.0) width: 0.12\nline (-3, 0) (3, 0) clear             # cut back from the copper, as the auto outline is\narc (0, 0) r: 1.5 from 30 to 150 width: 0.12\ncircle (0, 2.1) d: 0.3 fill\nhatch (0, 0) d: 4 from 1 to 2 pitch: 0.25   # chords across x of the circle (0, 0) d 4,\n                                            # at x = 1, 1.25, ... while x &lt; 2\nrect (0, 0) 3 x 2 layer: assembly     # on the assembly layer instead\ntext &quot;&gt;CATHODE&quot; at (1.8, 0) size: 0.6\nsilk: none                            # no automatic silk\ncourtyard: rect 7.4 x 7.4\ncourtyard: rect (0, 0.5) 7.4 x 8.0\nassembly: none\ntext ref at (0, 0) size: 1.0          # refdes and value fields; default layers: ref on silk, value on assembly\ntext value at (0, -1.2) size: 0.8 layer: assembly</code></pre></div>\n<p>Every graphic takes <code>width: W</code> and <code>layer: silk</code> or <code>layer: assembly</code>. A circle, rect\nor polygon can <code>fill</code>, and a line, arc, outline, rect, polygon or hatch marked <code>clear</code>\nis cut back from the copper. Text takes <code>size:</code> and <code>layer:</code>, which can also be\n<code>copper</code> or <code>mask</code> in a footprint.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-121-custom-features\">12.1 Custom features<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-121-custom-features\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A feature is a named set of graphics and solids, drawn around its own origin, that any\nfootprint in the file can use. Define it at the top level: its graphics (on the silk\nunless they say <code>layer: assembly</code>) and its solids (<a href=\"#spec-133-solids\">§13.3</a>) go straight in\nit, and it takes parameters as a template does:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"30\"><code>feature plus(s = 0.7)\n  line (-s / 2, 0) (s / 2, 0)\n  line (0, -s / 2) (0, s / 2)\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;CP-A&quot;\n  body 3.2 x 1.6 x 1.6\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead lbend 1.2 x 0.8\n  pin1: plus                            # the pin-1 mark, where the dot would go\n  feature plus(0.5) at pad 2 rotate: 45 # or anywhere: at a point or an object\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>pin1: NAME</code> draws the feature's graphics as the pin-1 mark, centered where the dot\nwould go and moved out by its own size to clear the copper. Like the other pin-1 marks,\nit's part of the automatic silk. <code>feature NAME at (x, y)</code>, <code>at (x, y, z)</code> or <code>at pad 3</code>\nplaces all of a feature anywhere: its graphics on the footprint's layers, and its\nsolids in the 3D model, z up from the board (<a href=\"#spec-133-solids\">§13.3</a>). <code>rotate: A</code> turns\nit, and <code>on body</code>, with or without an <code>at</code>, rests its solids on the body top. Text in it\nstays upright. Arguments work as in a template call\n(<a href=\"#spec-141-templates-are-functions\">§14.1</a>): a plain number takes the unit of the feature's\nstatements, so pass <code>40mil</code> from a footprint in other units. <code>outline body</code> can't go in\na feature, since a feature has no body.</p>\n<p>A feature places another the same way, at a point of its own: <code>feature NAME at (x, y)</code>,\nor <code>at (x, y, z) rotate: (rx, ry, rz)</code> to place its solids in 3D. A line with <code>at</code> (or\n<code>on body</code>) places a feature; at the left margin and without one, it starts a feature's\ndefinition. A feature can place itself only so deep: 32 levels is an error.</p>\n<p>A feature can also give names values (<code>half = len / 2</code>), which see its parameters, and\nhold <strong>sections</strong>: blocks included only when the call passes a <strong>flag</strong> with the\nsection's name. Sections are how a feature makes choices; stomp has no <code>if</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"31\"><code>feature tick(len = 1, w2 = 0.12)\n  half = len / 2\n  section long\n    len = 2                           # with the flag, a new default for len\n    section bold; len = 3; end        # both flags: nested sections need all of theirs\n  end\n  section bold, heavy; w2 = 0.3; end  # either flag turns it on\n  line (-half, 0) (half, 0) width: w2\n  section boxed\n    rect (0, 0) len x 0.5             # drawn only with the flag\n  end\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;T&quot;\n  …\n  feature tick(long, bold) at (0, 2)  # len 3, w2 0.3\n  feature tick(0.5, boxed) at (0, 3)  # len 0.5: the value given wins\nend</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flags</strong> are words in the call, before or after the values but never between them,\nin any order: <code>tick(long, 0.5)</code> or <code>tick(0.5, long)</code>. A flag is any word that names a\nsection of the feature, or of a feature it places (<code>dot</code> and <code>gullwing</code> work too). A\nlone word that names no section is a value; to pass a value whose name is also a\nflag, put it in parentheses: <code>tick((long))</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Flags flow down.</strong> A feature placed by another sees the flags of everything that\nplaced it, so a flag given once reaches every feature that needs it.</li>\n<li><strong><code>NAME = value</code> in a section</strong> gives a parameter of the feature, or a name it\ndefines, that value while the section is on. A value the call gives wins; otherwise the innermost\nsection that's on; otherwise the default. Two sections that are both on and set the\nsame name must be nested, one in the other; side by side, they're an error.</li>\n<li>A section holds <code>NAME = value</code> lines, other sections, graphics, solids and placed\nfeatures. In a section, <code>NAME = value</code> gives a name that exists a new value; a\nfeature defines its names at its top level. Footprints and templates have no\nsections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The standard library's features place the same way: its shapes (<code>rounded_box</code>,\n<code>dome</code>, <code>hex_prism</code>, …) and symbols (<code>arrow</code>, <code>corners</code>, …) are there to build with\n(<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>). A feature named like one of the library's replaces\nit for the whole file, including where the library uses it itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-13-3d-model\">13. 3D model<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-13-3d-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A footprint with a <code>body</code> has a 3D model without another line: the body, the pins and\nthe lead form already describe the part. A <code>model</code> block adjusts what that\ndescription can't say and adds anything else; <code>no model</code> turns it off. Every part of\nthe model is drawn by a stomp feature from the standard library\n(<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>), which a file can replace.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-131-the-generated-model\">13.1 The generated model<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-131-the-generated-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The model uses the footprint's x and y (<a href=\"#spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units\">§6</a>), with z\nup from the board surface: z = 0 is the top of the board. The body's H is the seated\nheight, JEDEC <em>A</em>: the top of the part above the board. The body's bottom sits at the\nstandoff, JEDEC <em>A1</em>. A body without an H is drawn as a flat plate.</p>\n<p>The lead form picks the rest. This table is what the standard library draws:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>lead</th><th>body</th><th>leads</th><th>standoff</th><th>pin-1 mark</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>gullwing</code></td><td>molded, 8° draft, parting line at mid-body</td><td>leave the parting line, bend down to a foot on the board; toes at the span</td><td>0.1</td><td>dimple</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>jlead</code></td><td>molded, 8° draft</td><td>leave the parting line, run down the body side and curl under it</td><td>0.5</td><td>dimple</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lbend</code></td><td>molded, 8° draft</td><td>leave the end face low, run down it and fold flat under the body</td><td>0.15</td><td>dimple</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>straight</code></td><td>molded, 8° draft</td><td>flat strips straight out of the bottom of the body, on the board</td><td>0.05</td><td>dimple</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>flat</code></td><td>sawn block; a can on flat leads stands on a plastic base</td><td>terminals flush with the body's bottom and edge (or at the span); exposed pads under the body</td><td>0.02</td><td>dot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>endcap</code></td><td>chip; a cylinder body is a MELF, lying along the pins</td><td>metal caps over each end, T long</td><td>0</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ball</code></td><td>laminate substrate and mold cap</td><td>balls d wide, collapsed to the standoff</td><td>0.75 d</td><td>dot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>column</code></td><td>ceramic block</td><td>solder columns d wide from the board up into the body</td><td>2.2</td><td>dot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pin</code>, <code>dual</code></td><td>molded DIP</td><td>shoulders down to the board, then the lead through it</td><td>0.5</td><td>dimple</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pin</code>, other</td><td>housing one pitch tall when H is over two pitches, else H: a socket, with an opening over each pin</td><td>straight pins from below the board to H</td><td>0</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>wire</code></td><td>cylinder: a can, an LED for a diode, or an axial body when the pins lie outside it</td><td>wires down through the board; axial wires leave the body's ends</td><td>0.5 / 0</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>castellated</code></td><td>module board, plus a shield can when H &gt; 1.2</td><td>plated half-holes in the board edge</td><td>0</td><td>dot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>tab</code></td><td>—</td><td>a metal slab under its pad</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>no <code>lead</code></td><td>plain block</td><td>—</td><td>0</td><td>as <code>pin1</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leads</strong> are tin. Gull-wings are 0.15 thick below a 1 mm pitch, 0.2 up to 2 mm and\n0.3 above. J-leads are 0.2, or 0.25 at a 1.27 mm pitch or more. Flat terminals are\n0.2, and L-bends and straight leads 0.12. Grid pins are gold. Through-hole leads reach 3 mm below the board. A gull-wing's\nfoot is the lead's foot length, shortened if the bend needs the room.</li>\n<li><strong>Pin 1.</strong> The mark goes on the body top at the corner nearest pin 1. <code>pin1: notch</code>\ngives a notch at the pin-1 end, <code>pin1: bar</code> a stripe, and <code>pin1: none</code> no mark.</li>\n<li><strong>Polarity.</strong> A <code>polarized</code> part gets a stripe at its marked end: the cathode (pin 2)\nof a diode or LED and the negative lead of a can, but the positive end (pin 1) of a\nchip or molded capacitor, which is how tantalums are marked. The silk bar goes on the\nsame end. A can in a metal <code>body color:</code> is bare, as polymer caps are: it has no vent,\nand its mark is a dark segment on the lid instead of a stripe on a sleeve.</li>\n<li><strong>Colors</strong> follow the part family, from the <code>refdes</code> (<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>).\nChip resistors are white ceramic with a black top, chip capacitors are tan, inductors\nferrite gray, and chip LEDs white with a clear lens. Molded bodies are black. The\ncolors are the standard library's palette.</li>\n<li><strong>Families</strong> add their own details. A crystal or oscillator (<code>Y</code>, <code>X</code>) on a sawn or\nblock body is a ceramic package under a metal lid, all within H. A diode on wires\nwith a round body is an LED: a lens with a domed top, on a flange whose flat marks\nthe cathode, red unless <code>body color:</code> says otherwise. A J-lead diode is a PLCC LED: a\nwhite housing with the leads folded flat under it and a round lens on top. An axial\nresistor has color bands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Omitted pins have no lead. Per-pin <code>lead</code> statements (<a href=\"#spec-113-leads\">§11.3</a>) change one\npin's lead, remove it (<code>lead 2 none</code>), or, in land mode, put leads on explicit pads.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-132-the-model-block\">13.2 The <code>model</code> block<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-132-the-model-block\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>model</code> alone on its line, its settings and solids, then <code>end</code>. Every setting is\noptional:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"32\"><code>model\n  standoff: 0.05             # the body's bottom above the board (JEDEC A1)\n  height: 0.85               # the model's height, where it isn't the body's H\n  draft: 0                   # molded-body side draft (0: a straight-sided block)\n  body color: &quot;#20232a&quot;      # any color (§7): &quot;#rrggbb&quot;, a name, mix(…), metal(…)\n  leads color: gold\n  leads thickness: 0.2       # lead-frame thickness (JEDEC c)\n  leads below: 3.5           # through-hole leads: length below the board\n  leads above: 0             # grid pins: height above the housing (0: a socket)\n  pin1: dot                  # the body mark: dimple | dot | notch | stripe | none\n  body shape: drum(shielded) # draw the body with this feature (§13.5)\n  leads shape: thin_lead     # draw every lead with this one\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>Named colors: <code>black</code>, <code>gray</code>, <code>white</code>, <code>red</code>, <code>green</code>, <code>blue</code>, <code>yellow</code>, <code>orange</code>,\n<code>brown</code>, <code>tan</code>, <code>ceramic</code>, and the metals <code>tin</code>, <code>silver</code>, <code>gold</code>, <code>copper</code> and\n<code>aluminum</code>. Metals render shiny. The standard library's palette adds names of its own.</p>\n<p><code>body shape: NAME(…)</code> and <code>leads shape: NAME(…)</code> draw this footprint's body or leads with\nanother feature than the library would pick: one of your own, or one of the library's\npackages, <code>to92</code>, <code>to220</code>, <code>hc49</code>, <code>tact_switch</code> and <code>buzzer</code>\n(<a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/tht_parts.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"tht_parts.stomp\"><code>examples/tht_parts.stomp</code></a>). The feature gets the values and\nflags the library's would (<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>); the arguments written\nin the statement come first.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-133-solids\">13.3 Solids<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-133-solids\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Anything the lead form doesn't describe can be built from solids, in a footprint's\n<code>model</code> block or straight in a feature:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>solid</th><th>draws</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>box W x L x H</code></td><td>a box</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>cylinder d: D h: H</code></td><td>an upright cylinder; <code>cylinder d: D to D2 h: H</code> is a cone</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sphere d: D</code></td><td>a sphere; <code>sphere d: D h: H</code> is squashed to H</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>disc d: D</code></td><td>a flat disc; <code>disc d: D to D2</code> is a ring from D to D2</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>prism PATH h: H</code></td><td>a flat outline, H thick</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>face PATH</code></td><td>a flat outline with no thickness, facing up</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>loft W x L at Z, W x L at Z, …</code></td><td>a solid through rectangles stacked at heights Z</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sweep W x T along PATH</code></td><td>a W x T strip along a path in x and z, as a lead is</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>wire d: D along PATH</code></td><td>a round wire along a path in x and z</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>slab W x L x T castellated</code></td><td>a board, with a half-hole cut wherever the part has a castellated lead</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A PATH is points and arcs, joined by commas:\n<code>(0, 0), (1, 0), arc (1, 1) r: 1 from -90 to 0 segments: 8, (2, 2)</code>. A prism's or\nface's path is an outline in x and y; a sweep's or wire's runs in x and z. <code>fillet: R</code>\nbefore <code>along</code> rounds the corners between points with radius R, in steps of 10°\n(<code>fillet: R step: 8</code> for 8°); an arc's own points stay as they are.</p>\n<p>Each solid takes these, in any order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>at (x, y)</code> or <code>at (x, y, z)</code>: where it goes (below);</li>\n<li><code>rotate: (rx, ry, rz)</code> in degrees, about x, then y, then z; <code>rotate: A</code> turns it about z;</li>\n<li><code>color: C</code>: any color (§7);</li>\n<li><code>name: &quot;…&quot;</code>: the name of its mesh in the model and the exports; a parameter or\nother name that holds text works too, and empty text means no name;</li>\n<li><code>kind: body</code>, <code>kind: lead</code> or <code>kind: mark</code>, in a feature: what it's highlighted with,\nand where it goes in the model's order (§13.5);</li>\n<li><code>segments: N</code>: how many sides a round solid has;</li>\n<li><code>open</code>, <code>open top</code> or <code>open bottom</code>: a cylinder without its ends, or one of them;</li>\n<li><code>from A to B</code>: part of a cylinder or disc, between two angles;</li>\n<li><code>down</code>: a disc or face that faces down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In a feature,</strong> <code>at</code> puts the solid's own origin at a point of the feature, then\n<code>rotate</code> turns it about that point. A box's, cylinder's or sphere's origin is the\nmiddle of its bottom; a path's is (0, 0). A size of zero or less draws nothing, so a\nfeature can leave out a part with arithmetic. A footprint places a feature's solids\nby placing the feature, <code>feature NAME(…) at (x, y, z) rotate: (…)</code> (§12.1), its origin at\nthat point: z from the board, or from the body's top with <code>on body</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>In a footprint's model block,</strong> a solid starts centered on the body (or on the\norigin, if there's no body), resting on the board. <code>at (x, y)</code> moves its center; a\nthird coordinate sets the height of its bottom, and <code>on body</code> rests it on the body top\ninstead. <code>rotate</code> turns the solid about its center before it's placed. A solid takes\nthe body's color unless it names one.</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"33\"><code>model\n  box 2.9 x 2.2 x 0.1 on body color: tin              # a lid, centered on the body top\n  box 6.6 x 6.6 x 0.5 color: black                    # a base plate on the board\n  cylinder d: 0.8 h: 0.2 at (1.0, 0.6) on body        # at (x, y): the solid's center\n  cylinder d: 2 h: 6 at (0, 0, 1) rotate: (0, 90, 0)  # at (x, y, z): z is its bottom\nend\nfeature heatsink(5, 5, 3) on body                     # a feature's solids, on the body top</code></pre></div>\n<p>(<code>heatsink</code> is in <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/custom_models.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"custom_models.stomp\"><code>examples/custom_models.stomp</code></a>.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-134-external-models-inheritance\">13.4 External models, inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-134-external-models-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"34\"><code>model\n  step: &quot;models/QFN32.step&quot; offset: (0, 0, 0) rotate: (0, 0, 90) scale: 1.0\nend\nno model                           # no 3D output at all</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>step:</code> replaces the generated model, and the transform maps the file into the model's\nframe. Model blocks merge down an <code>extends</code> chain: a later level's settings replace\nearlier ones, and its solids add to theirs. <code>no model</code> at any level ends the model\nthere, and a later <code>model</code> block starts it again.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-135-the-standard-library\">13.5 The standard library<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The model in §13.1 and the automatic silk marks of §11.5 are drawn by the <strong>standard\nlibrary</strong>: stomp files of features and named values in <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/tree/HEAD/stdlib\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>stdlib/</code></a>, loaded\nbefore every file. It's written in <code>units: mm</code>, so the values the 3D model passes are mm, and\nits angles are degrees.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>file</th><th>holds</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/colors.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/colors.stomp\"><code>colors.stomp</code></a></td><td>the palette: <code>epoxy</code>, <code>can_blue</code>, <code>ferrite</code>, <code>solder</code>, <code>led_red</code>, …</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/shapes.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/shapes.stomp\"><code>shapes.stomp</code></a></td><td>solids to build with: <code>rounded_box</code>, <code>chamfered_box</code>, <code>beveled_box</code>, <code>dome</code>, <code>torus</code>, <code>hex_prism</code>, <code>post</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/symbols.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/symbols.stomp\"><code>symbols.stomp</code></a></td><td>silk symbols: <code>triangle</code>, <code>arrow</code>, <code>plus</code>, <code>minus</code>, <code>cross</code>, <code>ring</code>, <code>frame</code>, <code>corners</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/marks.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/marks.stomp\"><code>marks.stomp</code></a></td><td><code>top_marks</code> and the marks it draws; <code>polarity</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/molded.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/molded.stomp\"><code>molded.stomp</code></a></td><td>molded, DIP, block and radial bodies; gull-wing, J-lead, L-bend, straight and tab leads</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/leadless.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/leadless.stomp\"><code>leadless.stomp</code></a></td><td>sawn bodies; flat, end-cap and grid-contact leads; exposed pads</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/chips.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/chips.stomp\"><code>chips.stomp</code></a></td><td>chip, MELF and axial bodies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/arrays.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/arrays.stomp\"><code>arrays.stomp</code></a></td><td>BGA and CGA bodies; balls and columns</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/cans.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/cans.stomp\"><code>cans.stomp</code></a></td><td>cans, and their vent, stripe, lid mark and SMD base</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/leds.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/leds.stomp\"><code>leds.stomp</code></a></td><td>radial LEDs, and a PLCC LED's lens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/through_hole.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/through_hole.stomp\"><code>through_hole.stomp</code></a></td><td>header housings; header, DIP, axial and plain through-hole leads</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/packages.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/packages.stomp\"><code>packages.stomp</code></a></td><td>bodies to name with <code>body shape:</code>: <code>to92</code>, <code>to220</code>, <code>hc49</code>, <code>tact_switch</code>, <code>buzzer</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/modules.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/modules.stomp\"><code>modules.stomp</code></a></td><td>module boards and castellations</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/stdlib/silk.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"stdlib/silk.stomp\"><code>silk.stomp</code></a></td><td>the silk's pin-1 and polarity marks</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Replacing a feature.</strong> A feature in your file named like one of the library's\nreplaces it everywhere in your file, including where the library's own features place\nit. So <code>feature can_vent(D)</code> (its solids, then <code>end</code>) changes the vent of every can,\nand <code>feature pin1_dot(d = 0.3)</code> every <code>pin1: dot</code>. The full list, with what each\nfeature takes, is <a href=\"#library\"><code>stdlib/README.md</code></a>. A replacement must take what the\nlibrary passes it; a parameter the library never gives a value gets a warning unless it\nhas a default. Library features see the library's names; your file sees them too,\nafter its own, so <code>color: can_blue</code> works anywhere.</p>\n<p><strong>What the model calls.</strong> The 3D model measures the part, then calls one feature for\nthe body and one per lead, by name:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>feature</th><th>for</th><th>values passed (by name, each only if the feature declares it)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>body_STYLE</code></td><td>the body: <code>body_molded</code>, <code>body_dip</code>, <code>body_block</code>, <code>body_radial</code>, <code>body_sawn</code>, <code>body_chip</code>, <code>body_melf</code>, <code>body_axial</code>, <code>body_bga</code>, <code>body_cga</code>, <code>body_can</code>, <code>body_led</code>, <code>body_header</code>, <code>body_module</code></td><td><code>W</code>, <code>L</code>, <code>H</code> (the body), <code>z0</code> (the standoff), <code>draft</code> (<code>draft:</code>), <code>body_color</code>, <code>lead_color</code>, <code>e</code> (the pitch), <code>above</code> (<code>leads above:</code>), <code>ball_d</code>, <code>cast_d</code> (the widest castellation), <code>turn</code> (0, or 90 for a part along y), <code>T</code> (an end cap's length), and for the marks <code>sx</code>, <code>sy</code> (toward pin 1's corner), <code>nx</code>, <code>ny</code> (toward the notched end), <code>ex</code>, <code>ey</code> (toward the striped end) and <code>ma</code> (the striped lead's direction, in degrees)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>lead_FORM</code></td><td>each lead: <code>lead_gullwing</code>, <code>lead_jlead</code>, <code>lead_lbend</code>, <code>lead_straight</code>, <code>lead_flat</code>, <code>lead_endcap</code>, <code>lead_tab</code>, <code>lead_contact</code> (no side), <code>lead_ball</code>, <code>lead_column</code>, <code>lead_castellated</code>, and for pins and wires <code>lead_dip</code>, <code>lead_axial</code>, <code>lead_header</code> or <code>lead_tht</code></td><td><code>edge</code> (the body's edge), <code>pad_u</code> (the pad's center), <code>toe</code> (the lead's tip, if the span gives it), <code>across</code> (the length of the body's side), <code>b</code>, <code>T</code>, <code>d</code> (the lead statement's sizes), <code>drill</code>, <code>pad_across</code>, <code>pad_along</code>, <code>z0</code>, <code>H</code>, <code>e</code>, <code>c</code> (<code>leads thickness:</code>), <code>below</code> (<code>leads below:</code>), <code>lead_color</code>, and what the body exports: <code>zp</code>, <code>housing</code>, <code>zc</code>, <code>len</code>, <code>ts</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>exposed_pad</code></td><td>each thermal pad</td><td><code>w</code>, <code>l</code> (the pad), <code>z0</code>, <code>H</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>lead_color</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>polarity</code></td><td>which pin a stripe or bar marks: its <code>stripe_at</code></td><td>(flags only)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The silk's marks are called the same way: <code>pin1_dot</code>, <code>pin1_bar(len, depth)</code>,\n<code>polarity_bar(len)</code>, <code>polarity_hatch(r)</code> and <code>polarity_plus</code>, with the style and family\nflags.</p>\n<p>A body is drawn with the body's center at the origin. A lead with a side is drawn in\nits side's frame: x outward from the body's center, y along the side, z up, so <code>edge</code>,\n<code>pad_u</code> and <code>toe</code> are x positions; the left and top sides are mirror images of the\nright and bottom. A ball, column, grid contact, header pin or plain pin is drawn around\nits pad's center. An end cap of a chip or MELF is drawn by the body, not by a lead.</p>\n<p><strong>Flags passed.</strong> The style (<code>molded</code>, <code>chip</code>, <code>led</code>, …), the main lead form\n(<code>gullwing</code>, …), the family (below), the mark asked for (<code>dimple</code>, <code>dot</code>, <code>notch</code> or\n<code>stripe</code>), <code>bare</code> or <code>sleeved</code> (whether the body color is a metal), and <code>silk_mark</code>\nwhen the silk marks pin 1 with a dot or a chamfer. A lead gets its form, the style and\nthe family, and a pin or wire <code>round</code> or <code>square</code>. A feature gets only the flags it has\nsections for; a mark it can't draw is reported as none.</p>\n<p>The <strong>family</strong> comes from the refdes's first letters, the longest match first:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>refdes</th><th>family</th><th></th><th>refdes</th><th>family</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>R</code></td><td><code>resistor</code></td><td></td><td><code>Y</code>, <code>XTAL</code></td><td><code>crystal</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>C</code></td><td><code>capacitor</code></td><td></td><td><code>X</code>, <code>XO</code>, <code>OSC</code></td><td><code>oscillator</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>L</code>, <code>FB</code></td><td><code>inductor</code></td><td></td><td><code>S</code>, <code>SW</code></td><td><code>switch</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>D</code>, <code>LED</code></td><td><code>diode</code></td><td></td><td><code>BZ</code>, <code>LS</code></td><td><code>buzzer</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>Q</code></td><td><code>transistor</code></td><td></td><td><code>J</code>, <code>P</code>, <code>CON</code>, <code>CN</code></td><td><code>connector</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>U</code>, <code>IC</code></td><td><code>ic</code></td><td></td><td><code>F</code></td><td><code>fuse</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Flags worked out.</strong> Some flags depend on the part's measurements in ways the library\ndecides. A feature that defines <code>NAME_if = …</code> gets flag NAME when that value is\nabove zero, and its partner, if it has one, otherwise; one it can't work out (a value\nnobody gave) gets neither. They're worked out in this order, each seeing the ones\nbefore it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>for bodies: <code>drafted</code> / <code>plain</code>, <code>tall</code>, <code>shield</code> / <code>unshielded</code>, <code>vented</code>;</li>\n<li>for leads: <code>fine</code>, <code>wide</code>, <code>coarse</code> (pitch classes), <code>straddle</code>, <code>flush</code>,\n<code>bent</code> / <code>cramped</code>, <code>under</code> / <code>beyond</code>, <code>socket</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, a gull-wing lead with no room to bend lies flat:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"35\"><code>foot = min(T, room - max(1.2 * c, 0.25 * room))\nbent_if = min(room - 2.5 * c, foot - c)\nsection bent\n  sweep b x c fillet: 1.5 * c along …\nend\nsection cramped\n  sweep b x c along …\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Values read back.</strong> Before drawing, the model reads the body feature's <code>z0</code> (the\nstandoff, then lowered if it reaches the top), <code>body_color</code>, and <code>pin1_mark</code> (the mark a\nstyle gets by default, as text). After it, it reads what the body exports to the leads:\n<code>zp</code> (where leads leave a molded body), <code>housing</code> (a header's), <code>zc</code> (an axial body's\ncenter), <code>len</code> (its length), <code>ts</code> (a module board's thickness), and <code>top</code> (where\n<code>on body</code> rests, if not H).</p>\n<p><strong>Meshes.</strong> Solids of one name, kind and color in one feature call form one mesh. A\nbody's solids are named <code>body</code> unless they say otherwise; a lead's are <code>lead N</code> (or\n<code>NAME N</code> with <code>name: &quot;NAME&quot;</code>), and an exposed pad's <code>exposed pad N</code>. A body's solids of\n<code>kind: mark</code> come after the leads and exposed pads, then the extra solids. A body's\nsolids highlight with the <code>body</code> statement, a lead's with its <code>lead</code> statement, and\nmarks with <code>pin1</code>, <code>polarized</code> and the model block. A lead's marks (a socket's\nopenings) highlight with its <code>lead</code> statement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-14-templates-tables-and-inheritance\">14. Templates, tables and inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-14-templates-tables-and-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"spec-141-templates-are-functions\">14.1 Templates are functions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-141-templates-are-functions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A template takes parameters and makes a footprint:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"36\"><code>template soic(n, lspan = 6.0, e = 1.27, bw = 3.9)   # a default takes `=`\n  body bw x (n/2 - 1) * e + 2.0 x 1.5\n  pins n dual pitch: e\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: lspan\n  pin1: dot\nend\n\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-8&quot;  from soic(8)\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-16&quot; from soic(16)\nfootprint &quot;SOIC-16W&quot; from soic(16, lspan: 10.3, bw: 7.5)   # a named argument takes `:`</code></pre></div>\n<p>A template's parameters are names, given their values in each footprint it makes; any\nword but <code>x</code> and <code>end</code> can be one. A default can refer to other parameters (<code>E = D</code>).\nArguments are positional, named, or both (<code>qfn(32, 5, D2: 3.45)</code>), and a parameter\nwithout a default must be given. A call that ends its line with <code>with</code> takes statements\nof its own, then <code>end</code>, to add to the footprint or change it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"37\"><code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8-WIDE-CY&quot; from soic(8) with\n  courtyard: auto 0.5\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p>There's no control flow (conditionals or loops) in v1: a template is a footprint body with\nparameters, and a table (<a href=\"#spec-143-tables-footprints-from\">§14.3</a>) is how one template\nmakes many footprints. The one way to choose is a feature's sections, turned on by\nflags (<a href=\"#spec-121-custom-features\">§12.1</a>); a template takes values only, never flags.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-142-naming-name\">14.2 Naming: <code>name</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-142-naming-name\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A template can name the footprints it makes, usually by interpolating its parameters\n(<a href=\"#spec-7-values-expressions-names\">§7</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"38\"><code>template qfn(n, D, D2, e = 0.5, E = D, E2 = D2)\n  name: &quot;QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{E}mm_P{e}mm_EP{D2}x{E2}mm&quot;\n  description: &quot;QFN, {n} pins, {D} x {E} mm body, {e:.2} mm pitch&quot;\n  …\n  thermal pad &quot;{n + 1}&quot; D2 x E2            # names interpolate too\nend\n\nfootprint from qfn(32, 5, 3.45)            # named QFN-32-1EP_5x5mm_P0.5mm_EP3.45x3.45mm\nfootprint &quot;MY-QFN&quot; from qfn(20, 4, 2.6)    # a name in the header wins</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>name:</code> belongs in templates, and in a call's <code>with</code> block to rename that one\nfootprint. An ordinary footprint is named in its header. A call with neither is an\nerror. Every footprint in a file needs its own name, generated ones included.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-143-tables-footprints-from\">14.3 Tables: <code>footprints from</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-143-tables-footprints-from\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A table calls a template once per row. The header row names the parameter each column\nfills, and each row after it is one footprint:</p>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"39\"><code>footprints from qfn(e: 0.5)      # arguments in the call apply to every row\n  n    D    D2                   # header: parameter names\n  12   3    1.65                 # a row: one footprint\n  16   3    1.7\n  32   5    3.45\nend</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Cells are separated by spaces or tabs, one row per line (or <code>;</code>), and <code>#</code> comments\ncan go anywhere. A cell is a number (with an optional unit suffix, and a minus sign\nattached: <code>-0.1</code>, <code>20mil</code>), a name, a <code>&quot;string&quot;</code>, or an expression in\nparentheses (<code>(D - 0.2)</code>). Arithmetic outside parentheses is an error, so a column\nnever swallows its neighbor.</li>\n<li>Every row has one cell per column. Parameters not in the header take the call's\narguments, then their defaults. A column can't repeat an argument of the call.</li>\n<li>The template names the footprints (<a href=\"#spec-142-naming-name\">§14.2</a>); a row has no header of\nits own.</li>\n<li>Rows are ordinary footprints: <code>extends</code> can reopen one by its generated name.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tables take in datasheet variation tables (JEDEC MO-220, vendor package lists) the way\nthey're printed: one variant per row, with the drawing's letters as columns.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spec-144-inheritance-extends\">14.4 Inheritance: <code>extends</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-144-inheritance-extends\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"stomp\" data-i=\"40\"><code>footprint &quot;SOIC-8-HS&quot; extends &quot;SOIC-8&quot;   # inherit: reopen and override\n  description: &quot;hand-solder variant&quot;\n  default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2             # longer pads\n  courtyard: auto 0.5\nend</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>extends</code> replays the parent, then applies the child's statements under the usual\noverride rules: strong beats weak, and a later strong constraint on the same axis of the\n<em>same</em> object <strong>replaces</strong> the inherited one instead of over-constraining it.\nInheritance is the one place where replacement is allowed. The parent can be any\nfootprint, including one a table generated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-15-diagnostics\">15. Diagnostics<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-15-diagnostics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The syntax is designed so a checker can be blunt and specific. Required diagnostics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a missing <code>end</code>, at its block's header, before the item that shows it's missing (a\n<code>footprint</code> line, a feature declaration at the left margin, the end of the file, a\nfootprint statement after a <code>model</code> line); when an inner block was closed by an <code>end</code>\nthat lines up with an outer header, at the inner block. An <code>end</code> with nothing to\nclose, and braces;</li>\n<li>a named value without its colon (<code>pitch 1.27</code>: write <code>pitch: 1.27</code>), a colon on a word\nthat takes none (<code>at:</code>), and a named value a statement doesn't take (with the nearest\none suggested); <code>let</code>; a name given two values at one level;</li>\n<li>over- and under-constraint, per object axis, citing the statements (§9.7);</li>\n<li>an unknown object name, with the nearest name suggested;</li>\n<li>an anchor that doesn't match the axis (<code>pad 1 left is … above …</code>);</li>\n<li>unit errors in expressions (length·length, a bare ratio where a length is needed);</li>\n<li><code>nc</code> and count mismatches in <code>pins</code> side lists; duplicate names; <code>omit</code> of balls that don't exist;</li>\n<li>a pad with no geometry after the precedence rules (§11.3);</li>\n<li>suspicious-geometry <em>warnings</em> (a zero-size pad, a drill as large as its pad or larger, overlapping pads whose nets aren't known), never errors, since each can be intended;</li>\n<li>duplicate footprint names, generated ones included; a table row with the wrong number of cells; a column that names no parameter (with the nearest name suggested); a template that doesn't <code>name</code> the footprints a table or an unnamed call needs from it;</li>\n<li>an error inside a template body, reported on the call or table row whose arguments cause it, and once at the template when every call has it;</li>\n<li>a courtyard that isn't one rectangle, and a courtyard set twice in one footprint (a warning); an unknown feature (with the nearest name suggested), a feature defined twice, and missing or unknown feature arguments;</li>\n<li>a side mark outside a 2D size, in the middle of a side, or on both sides with the same letter; <code>units:</code> after other statements in a footprint or template;</li>\n<li>3D: a per-pin <code>lead</code> naming a pin that doesn't exist or was omitted; <code>leads below:</code> or <code>leads above:</code> on leads that can't use them; a solid or color that can't be built; a model setting given twice in one block; a feature placed in a model block, which belongs in the footprint;</li>\n<li>features: an unknown flag (with the nearest flag suggested), a flag between values or given twice, two sections that are both on and set one name side by side, a section setting a name the feature doesn't have, a feature placing itself too deep; an unknown function (with the nearest suggested), a wrong number of arguments, a value of the wrong kind (<code>sqrt</code> of a length, <code>asin(2)</code>, a color in arithmetic); an unknown color (with the nearest suggested);</li>\n<li>a feature that replaces the standard library's but has a parameter the library never gives a value (a warning);</li>\n<li>a problem inside the standard library's code is reported at the statement that used it (the <code>body</code>, the <code>lead</code>, the <code>pin1:</code>, a <code>body shape:</code>), naming the library features it went through. The 3D model's problems are found when it's built, so the editor lists them for the footprint on screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"spec-16-formal-grammar\">16. Formal grammar<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-16-formal-grammar\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The complete grammar is <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/grammar.peg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>grammar.peg</code></a>. It's a PEG (ordered choice), so\nit maps one to one onto a PEGTL implementation later. This spec and the grammar file\nare written to agree. Where they conflict, the grammar file wins for <em>syntax</em> and this\nspec wins for <em>meaning</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spec-17-open-questions\">17. Open questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-17-open-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Name.</strong> Settled: the language and repo are <code>stomp</code> (a stomp leaves a footprint),\nand files are <code>.stomp</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Weak cross-axis alignment</strong> (§9.2): keep it, or require the second axis to be\nstated? The current choice favors short code. The failure mode, a surprising\ndefault alignment, is mitigated by the under-constraint error never firing while\nthe default is active.</li>\n<li><strong>IPC synthesis controls.</strong> Is <code>density: least|nominal|most</code> enough, or do toe,\nheel and side goals need syntax to override them per footprint\n(<code>ipc toe: 0.35 heel: 0.35 side: 0.03</code>)? Leaning: add the long form later; it's\npurely additive.</li>\n<li><strong>Arc and curved pads</strong> (RF, rotary encoders): <code>polygon</code> handles them badly. There\ncould be <code>ring</code> or <code>arcpad</code> shapes later.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-footprint packages</strong> (for example DNP variants that share a body):\n<code>extends</code> plus <code>delete</code> covers them. Is that enough?</li>\n<li><strong>Should the letter and number order of <code>balls</code> be overridable</strong> for non-JEDEC grids\n(some FPGAs)? Probably <code>numbering: bga letters: &quot;ABCDEFGHJK…&quot;</code> later.</li>\n<li><strong>Castellated semantics.</strong> The <code>lead castellated</code> syntax is reserved, and the 3D\nmodel draws each half-hole's plating, but the land's plating and paste rules for\nhalf-holes need their own mini-spec.</li>\n<li><strong>Embedded checks.</strong> A <code>check</code> statement (<code>check gap pad 1 to pad 2 is 0.4</code>) that\nasserts a redundant datasheet dimension without constraining anything. Cheap to add,\nand great for confidence in a transcription. Leaning: yes, in v1.1.</li>\n<li><strong>External tables.</strong> <code>footprints from qfn table: &quot;qfn.tsv&quot;</code>, reading rows from a file\nnext to the source, for vendor data that already lives in a spreadsheet. Purely\nadditive.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpolation formats.</strong> <code>{x:.N}</code> covers fixed decimals, and IPC-7351 names\n(<code>QFN50P500X500X90-33N</code>) can be built with arithmetic (<code>{e * 100}</code>). Zero padding or\nunit conversion in the format may be wanted later.</li>\n<li><strong>Body detail.</strong> Molded bodies have one draft angle and square edges; real ones also\nhave a pin-1 bevel, rounded corners and ejector-pin marks. These can now be written\nas library features (§13.5); past some point <code>step</code> may still be the right answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Polarity conventions.</strong> Settled: the silk bar follows the same <code>polarity</code> feature\nas the 3D stripe (§13.5), so a tantalum's band and bar both mark pin 1, and an axial\nor MELF capacitor's both mark pin 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Repetition.</strong> Sections choose; nothing repeats. Five heatsink fins are five\nsolids, and a feature can't draw n of something. A <code>repeat N</code> for solids, or arrays\nof placements, would be the smallest addition; loops in general stay out.</li>\n<li><strong>Library versions.</strong> The standard library is part of the language: a file that\nreplaces <code>can_vent</code> relies on the library still calling it with <code>D</code>. The hook tables\nin §13.5 should be versioned with the spec, and the library could say which version\nit implements.</li>\n<li><strong>Outlines from the library.</strong> The chamfered and notched silk outlines are still\nbuilt in, since they change the outline the rest of the silk is cut against. A hook\nthat returns an outline would move them into the library too.</li>\n<li><strong>Indentation.</strong> Blocks close with <code>end</code>, and indentation only helps the checker\npoint at the block that's missing one. Making indentation significant would drop\n<code>end</code>, at the cost of whitespace that matters. Leaning: keep <code>end</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"spec-18-example-index\">18. Example index<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#spec-18-example-index\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>file</th><th>shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/r0402.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"r0402.stomp\"><code>examples/r0402.stomp</code></a></td><td>the smallest land mode, and part mode for the same part</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/led0603.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"led0603.stomp\"><code>examples/led0603.stomp</code></a></td><td><code>polarized</code>, custom silk</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/soic8.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"soic8.stomp\"><code>examples/soic8.stomp</code></a></td><td>the standard dual gull-wing, in 4 lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/tssop20.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"tssop20.stomp\"><code>examples/tssop20.stomp</code></a></td><td>named values for the datasheet letters; an <code>extends</code> hand-solder variant</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/qfn32.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"qfn32.stomp\"><code>examples/qfn32.stomp</code></a></td><td>quad leadless, thermal pad, paste windows, vias, <code>pin1: chamfer</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/bga64.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"bga64.stomp\"><code>examples/bga64.stomp</code></a></td><td>ball grid, JEDEC lettering, <code>omit</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/sot23-5.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"sot23-5.stomp\"><code>examples/sot23-5.stomp</code></a></td><td>a <code>pins</code> side list, <code>nc</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/to252.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"to252.stomp\"><code>examples/to252.stomp</code></a></td><td>relational dimensioning (DPAK); per-pin leads on explicit pads for the 3D model</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/dip8.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"dip8.stomp\"><code>examples/dip8.stomp</code></a></td><td>THT dual, oval pads, square pin 1, <code>pin1: notch</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/hdr2x5.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"hdr2x5.stomp\"><code>examples/hdr2x5.stomp</code></a></td><td>THT grid, zigzag numbering, selector overrides</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/usb_microb.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"usb_microb.stomp\"><code>examples/usb_microb.stomp</code></a></td><td>the hardest one: mixed SMD, NPTH and slots, <code>mirrors</code>, chained relations, a polygon pad</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/cp_radial.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"cp_radial.stomp\"><code>examples/cp_radial.stomp</code></a></td><td>cylinder body, THT, polarized, a 3D color override</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/soic_family.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"soic_family.stomp\"><code>examples/soic_family.stomp</code></a></td><td><code>template</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>extends</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/qfn_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"qfn_library.stomp\"><code>examples/qfn_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>templates as functions: interpolated <code>name</code>, <code>description</code> and pad names, <code>footprints from</code> tables, an unnamed call, <code>extends</code> of a generated footprint</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/sot223.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"sot223.stomp\"><code>examples/sot223.stomp</code></a></td><td>per-pin lead: the wide tab lead widens its own land and 3D lead</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/do41.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"do41.stomp\"><code>examples/do41.stomp</code></a></td><td>axial through-hole: a cylinder body lying down, bent wires, a cathode band</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/crystal3225.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"crystal3225.stomp\"><code>examples/crystal3225.stomp</code></a></td><td>a family's details: for a <code>Y</code> refdes, a ceramic package with a metal lid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/tht_parts.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"tht_parts.stomp\"><code>examples/tht_parts.stomp</code></a></td><td>through-hole parts: the library's packages by name (TO-92, TO-220, HC-49, tact switch, buzzer), radial LEDs, a resistor's bands, and an encoder of your own from the library's shapes, with symbols in its silk</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/custom_models.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"custom_models.stomp\"><code>examples/custom_models.stomp</code></a></td><td>features of your own: replacing the library's <code>can_vent</code> and <code>pin1_dot</code>, a <code>body shape:</code> with sections and flags, a feature's solids placed on a body</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/syntax_zoo.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"syntax_zoo.stomp\"><code>examples/syntax_zoo.stomp</code></a></td><td>not a real part: covers everything else, and can double as a parser test</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Libraries, each a template or two and a table per family:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>file</th><th>shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/chip_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"chip_library.stomp\"><code>examples/chip_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>chip resistors, capacitors and inductors, 0201 to 2512; one template, three tables, quoted size codes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/sot_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"sot_library.stomp\"><code>examples/sot_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>SOT-23, SC-70, SOT-523 and SOT-563, one template per pin layout</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/sop_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"sop_library.stomp\"><code>examples/sop_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>SOIC, MSOP, TSSOP, SSOP and QSOP from one template, family values in each call</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/qfp_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"qfp_library.stomp\"><code>examples/qfp_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>LQFP and TQFP, 32 to 208 pins; a default computed from another parameter</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/dfn_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"dfn_library.stomp\"><code>examples/dfn_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>two-row leadless packages with exposed pads</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/bga_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"bga_library.stomp\"><code>examples/bga_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>ball grids from 9 to 484 balls and two column grids; <code>{x:.1}</code> names; rows past Z</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/diode_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"diode_library.stomp\"><code>examples/diode_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>SOD, flat-lead SOD and SMF, SMA/SMB/SMC and MELF; four lead forms</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/tantalum_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"tantalum_library.stomp\"><code>examples/tantalum_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>tantalum cases A to E: <code>lbend</code> leads, and a custom <code>+</code> feature as the pin-1 mark</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/polymer_cap_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"polymer_cap_library.stomp\"><code>examples/polymer_cap_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>aluminum polymer caps, SMD and radial, in bare cans</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/inductor_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"inductor_library.stomp\"><code>examples/inductor_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>shielded power inductors with bottom terminals, in ferrite</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/crystal_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"crystal_library.stomp\"><code>examples/crystal_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>four-pad crystals and oscillators with a ceramic base and metal lid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/led_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"led_library.stomp\"><code>examples/led_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>chip LEDs, and PLCC LEDs, which the library draws with a lens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/dip_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"dip_library.stomp\"><code>examples/dip_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>DIPs from 6 to 48 pins, written in mils with <code>units: mil</code> in the template</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/blob/HEAD/examples/header_library.stomp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-open=\"header_library.stomp\"><code>examples/header_library.stomp</code></a></td><td>2.54, 2.0 and 1.27 mm pin headers in one and two rows</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The standard library (<a href=\"#spec-135-the-standard-library\">§13.5</a>) is in <a href=\"https://github.com/metacollin/stomp/tree/HEAD/stdlib\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>stdlib/</code></a>:\nevery body, lead and mark in these examples comes from it.</p>","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"spec-table-of-contents","text":"Table of contents"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-1-goals","text":"1. Goals"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-2-design-principles","text":"2. Design principles"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-3-quick-tour","text":"3. Quick tour"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-4-lexical-structure","text":"4. Lexical structure"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-5-files-footprints-metadata","text":"5. Files, footprints, metadata"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units","text":"6. Coordinate system and units"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-7-values-expressions-names","text":"7. Values, expressions, names"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-8-objects-and-anchors","text":"8. Objects and anchors"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-81-object-kinds","text":"8.1 Object kinds"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-82-anchors","text":"8.2 Anchors"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-9-placement-and-dimensioning","text":"9. Placement and dimensioning"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-91-absolute-at","text":"9.1 Absolute: at"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-92-directional-relations-is--of","text":"9.2 Directional relations: is … of"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-93-chains-with-and","text":"9.3 Chains with and"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-94-symmetry-mirrors-and-measured-pairs-span--gap--pitch","text":"9.4 Symmetry: mirrors, and measured pairs: span / gap / pitch"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-95-centering-and-alignment","text":"9.5 Centering and alignment"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-96-rows-and-groups","text":"9.6 Rows and groups"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-97-constraint-semantics","text":"9.7 Constraint semantics"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-10-pads-and-holes","text":"10. Pads and holes"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-101-pad-definition","text":"10.1 Pad definition"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-102-pad-properties","text":"10.2 Pad properties"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-103-defaults-selectors-overrides-deletion","text":"10.3 Defaults, selectors, overrides, deletion"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-11-parametric-part-description","text":"11. Parametric part description"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-111-body","text":"11.1 Body"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement","text":"11.2 Pins: count and arrangement"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-113-leads","text":"11.3 Leads"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-114-thermal--exposed-pad","text":"11.4 Thermal / exposed pad"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-115-pin-1-marker-and-polarity","text":"11.5 Pin 1 marker and polarity"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-12-graphic-layers","text":"12. Graphic layers"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-121-custom-features","text":"12.1 Custom features"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-13-3d-model","text":"13. 3D model"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-131-the-generated-model","text":"13.1 The generated model"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-132-the-model-block","text":"13.2 The model block"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-133-solids","text":"13.3 Solids"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-134-external-models-inheritance","text":"13.4 External models, inheritance"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-135-the-standard-library","text":"13.5 The standard library"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-14-templates-tables-and-inheritance","text":"14. Templates, tables and inheritance"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-141-templates-are-functions","text":"14.1 Templates are functions"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-142-naming-name","text":"14.2 Naming: name"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-143-tables-footprints-from","text":"14.3 Tables: footprints from"},{"level":3,"id":"spec-144-inheritance-extends","text":"14.4 Inheritance: extends"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-15-diagnostics","text":"15. Diagnostics"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-16-formal-grammar","text":"16. Formal grammar"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-17-open-questions","text":"17. Open questions"},{"level":2,"id":"spec-18-example-index","text":"18. Example index"}],"snippets":[{"code":"footprint \"R0402\"\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  default pad rect 0.5 x 0.6\n  gap: 0.5                     # inner edge to inner edge between the two pads\n  body 1.0 x 0.5 x 0.35        # optional: enables silk, courtyard and 3D\nend","heading":"3. Quick tour","id":"spec-3-quick-tour","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"SOIC-8\"\n  body 3.9 x 4.9 x 1.5\n  pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0  # lead width x foot length; span outer to outer\n  pin1: dot\nend","heading":"3. Quick tour","id":"spec-3-quick-tour","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"TO-252\"\n  pad 1 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 3 rect 1.1 x 1.6\n  pad 2 rect 5.5 x 6.2                              # the tab\n\n  pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1                      # center to center\n  pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally\n  pad 2 inner is 1.9 above pad 1                    # the tab's near edge to the pin centers\nend","heading":"3. Quick tour","id":"spec-3-quick-tour","runnable":true},{"code":"template qfn(n, D, D2, e = 0.5)\n  name: \"QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{D}mm_P{e}mm_EP{D2}x{D2}mm\"\n  body D x D x 0.9\n  pins n quad pitch: e\n  lead flat e / 2 x 0.4\n  thermal pad \"{n + 1}\" D2 x D2\nend\n\nfootprints from qfn\n  n    D    D2\n  16   3    1.7\n  32   5    3.45\n  48   7    5.6\nend","heading":"3. Quick tour","id":"spec-3-quick-tour","runnable":true},{"code":"units: mm                                # the file's default unit (mm if not given)\n\nfootprint \"QFN-32\"\n  …\nend\n\nfootprint \"QFN-32-HS\" extends \"QFN-32\"   # inherit and override (§14)\n  …\nend\n\nfootprint \"SOIC-16\" from soic(16)        # a template call (§14)\n\nfootprints from qfn                      # one footprint per table row (§14.3)\n  …\nend","heading":"5. Files, footprints, metadata","id":"spec-5-files-footprints-metadata","runnable":false},{"code":"description: \"32-lead 5x5 mm QFN, 0.5 mm pitch, exposed pad\"\ndatasheet: \"https://example.com/qfn32.pdf\"\nmanufacturer: \"Acme\"\nmpn: \"ACME-32Q\"\nrefdes: \"U\"                       # reference designator prefix\ntags: qfn, leadless, ipc","heading":"5. Files, footprints, metadata","id":"spec-5-files-footprints-metadata","runnable":false},{"code":"template dip(n, D, E = 300)\n  units: mil                           # everything in here is in mils\n  body 250 x D x 130\n  pins n dual pitch: 100 span: E centers\nend","heading":"6. Coordinate system and units","id":"spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units","runnable":false},{"code":"origin at pad 1                        # e.g. connectors dimensioned from pin 1\norigin at (0, -1.25)\norigin centered between pad 1 and pad 2","heading":"6. Coordinate system and units","id":"spec-6-coordinate-system-and-units","runnable":false},{"code":"e  = 0.65              # type in the datasheet's letter dimensions...\nD  = 6.4\nA1 = 0.05\npins 20 dual pitch: e  # ...then use them by name","heading":"7. Values, expressions, names","id":"spec-7-values-expressions-names","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 1 at (0, 2.2)\nhole M1 at (-3.5, 0)","heading":"9.1 Absolute: at","id":"spec-91-absolute-at","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 3 is 4.56 right of pad 1                 # center to center\npad 8 outer is 6.0 right of pad 1 outer      # span, outer to outer\npad 2 inner is 0.4 right of pad 1 inner      # gap, inner to inner (facing edges)\npad 2 inner is 3.81 above pad 1              # mixed: edge to center\nhole M1 is 2.89 left of pad 1 and 0.55 below pad 1    # chained with `and`","heading":"9.2 Directional relations: is … of","id":"spec-92-directional-relations-is--of","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 2 is 1.27 right of pad 1        # pad 2 fully placed: y defaults to pad 1's y","heading":"9.2 Directional relations: is … of","id":"spec-92-directional-relations-is--of","runnable":false},{"code":"pad \"SH1\" is 1.2 left of pad 1 and 0.35 below body bottom","heading":"9.3 Chains with and","id":"spec-93-chains-with-and","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 8 mirrors pad 1 across y        # position and shape mirrored about the y axis\nspan pad 1 to pad 8 is 6.0          # outer edge to outer edge\ngap  pad 4 to pad 5 is 0.4          # facing edge to facing edge\npitch pad 1 to pad 2 is 1.27        # center to center","heading":"9.4 Symmetry: mirrors, and measured pairs: span / gap / pitch","id":"spec-94-symmetry-mirrors-and-measured-pairs-span--gap--pitch","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3               # both axes (midpoint of centers)\npad 2 is centered between pad 1 and pad 3 horizontally  # x only\npad \"EP\" is centered on body                            # both axes\nalign pad 7 with pad 2 horizontally                     # same y: they lie on one horizontal line\nalign hole M2 with hole M1 vertically                   # same x: one vertical line","heading":"9.5 Centering and alignment","id":"spec-95-centering-and-alignment","runnable":false},{"code":"row L pads 1..4 pitch: 1.27 going: down     # 1 at the top, centers 1.27 apart\ngroup mounting = hole M1, hole M2, pad \"SH1\", pad \"SH2\"","heading":"9.6 Rows and groups","id":"spec-96-rows-and-groups","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 1  rect 0.6 x 1.5\npad 2  oval 0.6 x 1.5                    # obround\npad 3  circle 0.45                       # diameter\npad 4  roundrect 0.6 x 1.5 r: 25%        # corner radius: % of the smaller side, or absolute (r: 0.1)\npad 5  dshape 0.6 x 1.5 flat: left       # \"D\" pad: one flat side, the other end fully round\npad 6  polygon (0,0) (1.2,0) (1.2,0.8) (0.5,0.8) (0.5,2.0) (0,2.0)   # vertices; local origin at the centroid","heading":"10.1 Pad definition","id":"spec-101-pad-definition","runnable":false},{"code":"pad 1 tht rect 1.7 x 1.7 drill: 1.0      # square pad, the classic pin 1\npad 2 tht circle 1.7 drill: 1.0\npad 7 tht oval 1.7 x 2.4 drill: slot 1.0 x 1.7  # slotted hole","heading":"10.1 Pad definition","id":"spec-101-pad-definition","runnable":false},{"code":"hole M1 0.85                             # NPTH, diameter\nhole M2 slot 0.85 x 1.4                  # NPTH slot","heading":"10.1 Pad definition","id":"spec-101-pad-definition","runnable":false},{"code":"rotate: 45                   # about the pad center, CCW degrees\nmask: +0.05                  # solder-mask expansion per side (default: the stack rule)\nmask: none                   # no mask opening\nmask: defined                # solder-mask-defined pad (the mask sets the land)\npaste: -0.05                 # paste shrink per side\npaste: none\npaste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 68%        # windowpane stencil (thermal pads)\npaste: divide 3 x 3 gutter: 0.3          # or a fixed gutter between windows\nvias: 3 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.1        # thermal via array inside the pad","heading":"10.2 Pad properties","id":"spec-102-pad-properties","runnable":false},{"code":"default pad rect 0.4 x 1.4        # the prototype for every pad without its own geometry\n                                  # (generated pads included)\npad 5 width: 2.0                  # change one property: keep everything else, widen pad 5\npads 1..4, 9 mask: +0.1           # change several pads by selector\ndelete pad 11                     # remove a generated object (depopulated variants)","heading":"10.3 Defaults, selectors, overrides, deletion","id":"spec-103-defaults-selectors-overrides-deletion","runnable":false},{"code":"body 5 x 5 x 0.9                 # W x L x H box (molded, default draft and chamfer)\nbody cylinder d: 8 h: 11.5       # radial can\nbody 9.5 x 6.5                   # height optional (no height: a flat model)","heading":"11.1 Body","id":"spec-111-body","runnable":false},{"code":"pins 8 dual pitch: 1.27          # left and right sides, CCW from the top left (pin 1 top left)\npins 32 quad pitch: 0.5          # four sides, CCW from the top left\npins 2 horizontal                # two-terminal chip, pin 1 left\npins 2 vertical                  # pin 1 top\npins 3 single bottom pitch: 0.95 # all pins on one side\npins grid 5 x 2 pitch: 2.54      # NX columns x NY rows (x count first, like sizes)\nballs 8 x 8 pitch: 0.8           # shorthand for grid + `lead ball` + `numbering: bga`","heading":"11.2 Pins: count and arrangement","id":"spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement","runnable":false},{"code":"pins\n  left: 1, 2, 3 pitch: 0.95\n  right: 4, nc, 5                # SOT-23-5: pins 4 and 5 at the outer positions\nend","heading":"11.2 Pins: count and arrangement","id":"spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement","runnable":false},{"code":"omit A1, H8, rows D..E cols 4..5        # by name and/or rectangular region\nonly rows A..B, rows G..H               # the reverse: keep only the listed regions","heading":"11.2 Pins: count and arrangement","id":"spec-112-pins-count-and-arrangement","runnable":false},{"code":"lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: 6.0  # width x foot length; span outer to outer\nlead jlead 0.5 x 0.9 span: 8.6\nlead lbend 1.2 x 0.8                 # inward L-bend (tantalums, SMA): the foot folds under the body; span optional\nlead straight 0.85 x 0.55 span: 3.6  # flat leads straight out of the bottom of the body (SOD-123F, SMF)\nlead flat 0.25 x 0.4                 # leadless lands (QFN/DFN/LGA); span optional (defaults to the body edge)\nlead endcap 0.35                     # chip terminations; width defaults to the body width\nlead ball 0.4                        # BGA ball diameter\nlead column d: 0.51                  # CGA solder column diameter; on a grid, like ball\nlead pin d: 0.64                     # THT round pin; also: lead pin 0.64 x 0.64 (square)\nlead wire d: 0.6                     # axial or radial wire leads\nlead tab 5.5 x 6.2                   # DPAK-style tab (becomes highest-numbered pin unless named)\nlead castellated d: 0.6              # half-hole edge castellations","heading":"11.3 Leads","id":"spec-113-leads","runnable":false},{"code":"lead gullwing 0.7 x 0.9 span: 7.0    # SOT-223: pins 1 to 3...\nlead 4 gullwing 3.0 x 0.9            # ...and the wide tab lead, pin 4\nlead 2 none                          # a pin cut off at the body: no 3D lead","heading":"11.3 Leads","id":"spec-113-leads","runnable":false},{"code":"thermal pad 3.7 x 3.7                          # named EP by default, centered on the body\nthermal pad 33 3.7 x 3.7 paste: divide 2 x 2 coverage: 68%   # or give it its pin number\npad 33 vias: 3 x 3 drill: 0.3 pitch: 1.1       # and change it like any other pad","heading":"11.4 Thermal / exposed pad","id":"spec-114-thermal--exposed-pad","runnable":false},{"code":"pin1: dot         # silk dot outside the corner nearest pin 1 (default for dual and quad)\npin1: chamfer     # chamfered silk corner (the QFN convention)\npin1: notch       # DIP-style notch at the pin-1 end\npin1: bar         # bar across the pin-1 end (tantalum or diode style)\npin1: none\npin1: triangle    # a feature (§12.1), yours or the library's, drawn where the dot would go\npolarized         # two-terminal polarity: pin 1 is positive (the anode); adds a silk bar\n                  # and the 3D stripe at the end the part's family marks (§13.1)\ndensity: most     # IPC density level: least | nominal | most","heading":"11.5 Pin 1 marker and polarity","id":"spec-115-pin-1-marker-and-polarity","runnable":false},{"code":"outline body offset: 0.15             # follow the body outline, pushed out\nline (-2.5, 1.0) (-2.5, -1.0) width: 0.12\nline (-3, 0) (3, 0) clear             # cut back from the copper, as the auto outline is\narc (0, 0) r: 1.5 from 30 to 150 width: 0.12\ncircle (0, 2.1) d: 0.3 fill\nhatch (0, 0) d: 4 from 1 to 2 pitch: 0.25   # chords across x of the circle (0, 0) d 4,\n                                            # at x = 1, 1.25, ... while x < 2\nrect (0, 0) 3 x 2 layer: assembly     # on the assembly layer instead\ntext \">CATHODE\" at (1.8, 0) size: 0.6\nsilk: none                            # no automatic silk\ncourtyard: rect 7.4 x 7.4\ncourtyard: rect (0, 0.5) 7.4 x 8.0\nassembly: none\ntext ref at (0, 0) size: 1.0          # refdes and value fields; default layers: ref on silk, value on assembly\ntext value at (0, -1.2) size: 0.8 layer: assembly","heading":"12. Graphic layers","id":"spec-12-graphic-layers","runnable":false},{"code":"feature plus(s = 0.7)\n  line (-s / 2, 0) (s / 2, 0)\n  line (0, -s / 2) (0, s / 2)\nend\n\nfootprint \"CP-A\"\n  body 3.2 x 1.6 x 1.6\n  pins 2 horizontal\n  lead lbend 1.2 x 0.8\n  pin1: plus                            # the pin-1 mark, where the dot would go\n  feature plus(0.5) at pad 2 rotate: 45 # or anywhere: at a point or an object\nend","heading":"12.1 Custom features","id":"spec-121-custom-features","runnable":true},{"code":"feature tick(len = 1, w2 = 0.12)\n  half = len / 2\n  section long\n    len = 2                           # with the flag, a new default for len\n    section bold; len = 3; end        # both flags: nested sections need all of theirs\n  end\n  section bold, heavy; w2 = 0.3; end  # either flag turns it on\n  line (-half, 0) (half, 0) width: w2\n  section boxed\n    rect (0, 0) len x 0.5             # drawn only with the flag\n  end\nend\n\nfootprint \"T\"\n  …\n  feature tick(long, bold) at (0, 2)  # len 3, w2 0.3\n  feature tick(0.5, boxed) at (0, 3)  # len 0.5: the value given wins\nend","heading":"12.1 Custom features","id":"spec-121-custom-features","runnable":false},{"code":"model\n  standoff: 0.05             # the body's bottom above the board (JEDEC A1)\n  height: 0.85               # the model's height, where it isn't the body's H\n  draft: 0                   # molded-body side draft (0: a straight-sided block)\n  body color: \"#20232a\"      # any color (§7): \"#rrggbb\", a name, mix(…), metal(…)\n  leads color: gold\n  leads thickness: 0.2       # lead-frame thickness (JEDEC c)\n  leads below: 3.5           # through-hole leads: length below the board\n  leads above: 0             # grid pins: height above the housing (0: a socket)\n  pin1: dot                  # the body mark: dimple | dot | notch | stripe | none\n  body shape: drum(shielded) # draw the body with this feature (§13.5)\n  leads shape: thin_lead     # draw every lead with this one\nend","heading":"13.2 The model block","id":"spec-132-the-model-block","runnable":false},{"code":"model\n  box 2.9 x 2.2 x 0.1 on body color: tin              # a lid, centered on the body top\n  box 6.6 x 6.6 x 0.5 color: black                    # a base plate on the board\n  cylinder d: 0.8 h: 0.2 at (1.0, 0.6) on body        # at (x, y): the solid's center\n  cylinder d: 2 h: 6 at (0, 0, 1) rotate: (0, 90, 0)  # at (x, y, z): z is its bottom\nend\nfeature heatsink(5, 5, 3) on body                     # a feature's solids, on the body top","heading":"13.3 Solids","id":"spec-133-solids","runnable":false},{"code":"model\n  step: \"models/QFN32.step\" offset: (0, 0, 0) rotate: (0, 0, 90) scale: 1.0\nend\nno model                           # no 3D output at all","heading":"13.4 External models, inheritance","id":"spec-134-external-models-inheritance","runnable":false},{"code":"foot = min(T, room - max(1.2 * c, 0.25 * room))\nbent_if = min(room - 2.5 * c, foot - c)\nsection bent\n  sweep b x c fillet: 1.5 * c along …\nend\nsection cramped\n  sweep b x c along …\nend","heading":"13.5 The standard library","id":"spec-135-the-standard-library","runnable":false},{"code":"template soic(n, lspan = 6.0, e = 1.27, bw = 3.9)   # a default takes `=`\n  body bw x (n/2 - 1) * e + 2.0 x 1.5\n  pins n dual pitch: e\n  lead gullwing 0.41 x 1.04 span: lspan\n  pin1: dot\nend\n\nfootprint \"SOIC-8\"  from soic(8)\nfootprint \"SOIC-16\" from soic(16)\nfootprint \"SOIC-16W\" from soic(16, lspan: 10.3, bw: 7.5)   # a named argument takes `:`","heading":"14.1 Templates are functions","id":"spec-141-templates-are-functions","runnable":true},{"code":"footprint \"SOIC-8-WIDE-CY\" from soic(8) with\n  courtyard: auto 0.5\nend","heading":"14.1 Templates are functions","id":"spec-141-templates-are-functions","runnable":false},{"code":"template qfn(n, D, D2, e = 0.5, E = D, E2 = D2)\n  name: \"QFN-{n}-1EP_{D}x{E}mm_P{e}mm_EP{D2}x{E2}mm\"\n  description: \"QFN, {n} pins, {D} x {E} mm body, {e:.2} mm pitch\"\n  …\n  thermal pad \"{n + 1}\" D2 x E2            # names interpolate too\nend\n\nfootprint from qfn(32, 5, 3.45)            # named QFN-32-1EP_5x5mm_P0.5mm_EP3.45x3.45mm\nfootprint \"MY-QFN\" from qfn(20, 4, 2.6)    # a name in the header wins","heading":"14.2 Naming: name","id":"spec-142-naming-name","runnable":false},{"code":"footprints from qfn(e: 0.5)      # arguments in the call apply to every row\n  n    D    D2                   # header: parameter names\n  12   3    1.65                 # a row: one footprint\n  16   3    1.7\n  32   5    3.45\nend","heading":"14.3 Tables: footprints from","id":"spec-143-tables-footprints-from","runnable":false},{"code":"footprint \"SOIC-8-HS\" extends \"SOIC-8\"   # inherit: reopen and override\n  description: \"hand-solder variant\"\n  default pad rect 0.6 x 2.2             # longer pads\n  courtyard: auto 0.5\nend","heading":"14.4 Inheritance: extends","id":"spec-144-inheritance-extends","runnable":false}]}]};
})(typeof self !== 'undefined' ? self : this);
